window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["imhans-2019"] = {"id": "imhans-2019", "title": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2019", "fullTitle": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2019, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, booklet 19034 A)", "sub": "75 real questions · official LBS key, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "------ is the process in which detecting, translating and transmitting from external environment to brain.", "options": {"a": "Adaptation", "b": "Selective attention", "c": "Perception", "d": "Sensation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: sensation is the process of detecting physical energy from the external environment, translating it into nerve signals and transmitting those signals to the brain. Sensation has three steps. First, specialised receptor cells detect a stimulus: light on the rods and cones of the retina, sound waves in the cochlea, chemicals on the tongue and in the nose, pressure and temperature on the skin. Second, the receptors translate that physical energy into electrical nerve impulses, a step called transduction. Third, sensory nerves transmit the impulses to the brain, where most senses are relayed through the thalamus to their own areas of cortex. Perception is the next stage: the brain organises and interprets this input into meaningful objects and events. The scientific study of how physical energy relates to experience is psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860). Example: when Meera steps into a noisy street, sound waves bend the hair cells in her cochlea, which fire signals along the auditory nerve (sensation); recognising that one of the sounds is her brother calling her name is perception. Option d is right because detecting, translating (transducing) and transmitting raw stimulus energy to the brain is the textbook definition of sensation. Exam trap: perception (c) is the near miss, because the two words are so often used together; sensation ends when the signal reaches the brain, while perception begins there, with organising and interpreting that signal.", "topic": "Sensation versus perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Destruction of the ------ part of the brain leads to anterograde amnesia.", "options": {"a": "Cerebrum", "b": "Hippocampus", "c": "Amygdala", "d": "Pons region"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: destruction of the hippocampus leads to anterograde amnesia, the inability to form new long-term memories after the damage. The hippocampus is a curved structure in the medial temporal lobe of each hemisphere. It does not store memories permanently; it consolidates them, binding new experiences and facts into lasting memories that are later held in the cortex. The classic evidence is patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Scoville removed much of the hippocampus, together with the nearby amygdala and surrounding cortex, from both sides of his brain to control severe epilepsy. The seizures eased, but, as William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported, H.M. could no longer remember new people, events or facts for more than a few minutes. His older memories, his intelligence and his short-term memory were largely intact, and he could still learn new motor skills such as mirror drawing without remembering the practice sessions. This showed that declarative (explicit) memory depends on the hippocampus, while procedural memory uses other systems. Retrograde amnesia, by contrast, is loss of memories formed before the damage. Example: after viral encephalitis damages both of Arjun's hippocampi, he can describe his childhood in detail but cannot recall the nurse he met an hour ago. Option b is right because bilateral hippocampal damage is the textbook cause of anterograde amnesia. Exam trap: the amygdala (c) sits right next to the hippocampus and was removed in H.M. too, but it handles fear and the emotional colouring of memory, not the formation of new declarative memories.", "topic": "Hippocampus and anterograde amnesia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals?", "options": {"a": "Depersonalisation Disorder", "b": "Narcissistic Personality Disorder", "c": "Schizophrenia", "d": "Anti-Social Personality Disorder"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: depersonalisation disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals. Forensic surveys ask which mental disorders are over-represented among prisoners and offenders. The best known is the systematic review by Seena Fazel and John Danesh (2002) in The Lancet: about 47% of male prisoners had antisocial personality disorder, making it the most common diagnosis in prison samples, and about 3.7% had a psychotic illness, several times the rate in the general community. Antisocial personality disorder fits offending almost by definition, since its criteria include repeated unlawful acts, deceit, impulsivity, aggression and lack of remorse. Narcissistic personality disorder, with its grandiosity, entitlement and low empathy, is also over-represented in offender groups. Schizophrenia, especially when combined with substance misuse, raises the risk of violence and is common in prisons and forensic psychiatric units. Depersonalisation (now depersonalisation/derealisation) disorder is different: the person feels detached from his or her own self or body, as if watching from outside, or feels that the world is unreal, while reality testing stays intact. It is distressing, but it does not drive antisocial behaviour, and it is not a forensic staple. Example: Kabir, a college student, feels as if he is living in a dream and his hands do not belong to him; he seeks help for his distress, not because of any crime. Option a is right because depersonalisation disorder is the only option not linked with offending or common in prison populations. Exam trap: schizophrenia (c) tempts as the 'innocent' choice because most people with schizophrenia are never violent, yet psychotic illness is still over-represented among prisoners, while depersonalisation disorder is not. Key note: LBS's official key for this paper marks c, schizophrenia. That key is wrong: psychotic illness is several times more common in prisoners than in the general population (Seena Fazel and John Danesh, 2002), while depersonalisation disorder has no link with offending, so a stays.", "topic": "Mental disorders in offenders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An IQ test does not provide:", "options": {"a": "Good validity", "b": "High test-retest reliability", "c": "High internal consistency", "d": "Good predictor of behavior"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: an IQ test does not provide a good prediction of everyday behaviour. Modern intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales (David Wechsler, 1939 onwards) and the Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) are among the best built tools in psychology. Their full-scale IQs show high test-retest reliability, usually .90 or more, so scores stay stable over weeks and years. They show high internal consistency, because their items and subtests cohere in measuring general ability. They also have good validity for what they were built to predict: school grades and educational attainment, and to a fair degree job performance. What they do poorly is predict how a person will actually behave in daily life: social conduct, emotional adjustment, everyday adaptive behaviour, or how someone copes in a crisis. Behaviour depends on motivation, personality, emotional and social skills, culture and the situation, none of which an IQ score captures. That is why a diagnosis of intellectual disability also needs a separate measure of adaptive behaviour, such as the Vineland scales. Example: Arjun scores 125 on the WAIS, yet he struggles to keep friends and cannot manage his money; his IQ did not forecast these everyday difficulties. Option d is right because reliability, internal consistency and validity are strengths of IQ tests, while broad prediction of behaviour is their known limit. Exam trap: 'good validity' tempts because IQ tests are often criticised, but the criticism is about cultural bias and narrowness, not poor validity for academic outcomes.", "topic": "Limits of IQ tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following statements are true? I) Classical conditioning regulates reflexive, involuntary responses exclusively. II) Classical conditioning can increase sexual arousal.", "options": {"a": "Only I is true", "b": "Both are true", "c": "Only II is true", "d": "Both are false"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: only statement II is true. Classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus that already triggers a response, until the neutral stimulus alone triggers a conditioned response. Statement I is false because of the word 'exclusively'. Classical conditioning mainly shapes reflexive, involuntary responses such as salivation, heart rate and fear, but not only these: it also shapes emotional reactions, food preferences and aversions, attitudes (evaluative conditioning) and drug tolerance, and in autoshaping a pigeon's skeletal key-peck is produced simply by pairing a key light with food. Classical and operant processes also overlap in everyday learning. Statement II is true: sexual arousal can be classically conditioned. Stanley Rachman (1966) showed men a picture of women's boots followed by erotic slides; after repeated pairings the boots alone produced mild sexual arousal, measured by changes in penile volume, which offered a model of how fetishes might be learned. Michael Domjan's studies of male Japanese quail in the 1980s showed that a cue signalling access to a female came to produce sexual approach and quicker mating. Example: Riya feels a rush of excitement whenever she smells the perfume her partner wears, because the scent has been paired with intimacy many times. Option c is right because statement I overstates the case with 'exclusively', while statement II is supported by conditioning experiments. Exam trap: 'reflexive and involuntary' is the standard textbook description of classical conditioning, so statement I sounds correct; the single word 'exclusively' is what makes it false.", "topic": "Scope of classical conditioning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "options": {"a": "The items on the test consistently relates to each other", "b": "That two tests are done at the same time", "c": "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", "d": "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: concurrent validity means that scores on a test correlate highly with scores on an accepted measure of the same attribute taken at about the same time. Criterion-related validity is the correlation between a test and an independent, external measure of the attribute, the criterion, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis, an established gold-standard scale, school marks or job ratings. Its two types differ only in timing. In concurrent validity, test and criterion are measured at about the same time, so the test is checked against present status. In predictive validity, the criterion is gathered later, as when entrance scores are correlated with first-year marks. The classic concurrent design gives a new test together with an established one: a new 20-item depression questionnaire that correlates 0.85 with a gold-standard scale completed the same day shows concurrent validity. The correlation is called the validity coefficient. Example: Riya builds a 10-minute reading screener and gives it to 200 children on the same day as a full, well-established reading battery; a high correlation between the two shows concurrent validity. Option d is right because it is the only option that contains the essential idea, a high correlation with an accepted measure of the same attribute. Exam trap: the word 'concurrent' tempts students to pick 'two tests are done at the same time', but timing alone is not validity. Key note: strictly, correlating with tests of the same attribute blends concurrent and convergent validity, and option b is a half-truth (the timing) with nothing about correlation; d is the best of the four and stands.", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Weschler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for:", "options": {"a": "Discrepancy scores", "b": "Disability allowance", "c": "Special educational needs", "d": "Occupational benefits"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for a disability allowance. The WAIS is an individually given adult intelligence test built by David Wechsler (1939, as the Wechsler-Bellevue; WAIS 1955, now in later editions such as the WAIS-IV, 2008). It covers ages 16 to 90 and gives a Full Scale IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, plus index scores for verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed. Because it yields a standardised, norm-based measure of intellectual functioning, it is used to document intellectual disability (IQ about 70 or below, together with deficits in adaptive behaviour) or cognitive decline after brain injury or dementia. When an adult claims a state disability benefit on these grounds, the claim needs objective evidence, and a WAIS report provides it. Example: Arjun, aged 24, applies for a disability pension; his psychologist gives the WAIS-IV and an adaptive behaviour scale, and his IQ of 62 with poor daily living skills documents his intellectual disability. Option b is right because eligibility for a disability allowance can hinge on a documented level of intellectual functioning, which is exactly what an adult IQ test provides. Exam trap: special educational needs (c) also rely on IQ testing, but they concern school children, who are tested with the child version, the WISC, not the adult scale. Key note: the item comes from a UK clinical psychology test bank (the one that accompanies Graham Davey's Psychopathology textbook), where disability allowance is a named benefit; the same question on Amity 2023 is keyed disability allowance too, and discrepancy scores are an output of testing rather than something one is eligible for; b stands. LBS's revised key for this paper (17 Sep 2019) deleted this question, so no one was marked on it; its first key (2 Sep) had marked c, special educational needs.", "topic": "Uses of the WAIS", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An analogue observation enables:", "options": {"a": "Surreptitious observation of the client", "b": "Observations of non-clinical population", "c": "Observation of more than one client at a time", "d": "Observation that require three or more clinicians"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: analogue observation enables surreptitious observation of the client. In behavioural assessment the clinician watches behaviour directly rather than relying only on what the client reports. Naturalistic observation happens in the real setting (home, classroom, ward). Analogue observation happens in a contrived, controlled setting built to resemble the real one: a clinic playroom where a parent and child carry out a set task, or a role-played social situation in which a client must refuse a request or start a conversation. Because the setting is arranged in advance, the clinician can watch from behind a one-way mirror or through a video link, unseen and unobtrusive, which is what 'surreptitious' means. This reduces reactivity (people behaving differently because they know they are being watched) and lets behaviour be coded in a standard way. The cost is generalisation: behaviour in a clinic room may not match behaviour at home. Example: Meera's parents say she refuses every instruction, so the psychologist asks her mother to have Meera tidy toys in a playroom and records Meera's compliance through a one-way mirror. Option a is right because the controlled analogue setting is what makes hidden, unobtrusive observation of the client possible. Exam trap: the word 'analogue' also appears in analogue research, where non-clinical people stand in for patients; that is a research strategy, not an observation method. Key note: the booklet prints 'Superstitious', plainly a slip for 'surreptitious' (unseen, unobtrusive), and the option is shown corrected; b describes analogue research, a different use of the word, so a stands.", "topic": "Analogue observation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:", "options": {"a": "One standard deviation below the mean", "b": "Two standard deviation below the mean", "c": "Three standard deviation below the mean", "d": "Four standard deviation below the mean"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the DSM-IV-TR intellectual criterion for mental retardation was an IQ about two standard deviations below the mean. Intelligence tests such as David Wechsler's scales (from 1939) are standardised to a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, so two SD below the mean is 100 minus 30, an IQ of 70. DSM-IV-TR (2000) required significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, an IQ of about 70 or below on an individually given test, together with deficits in adaptive functioning (communication, self-care, social and practical skills) and onset before age 18. Allowing for measurement error, scores up to about 75 could qualify when adaptive deficits were clear. On the normal curve only about 2.3 percent of people score below 70. Severity was graded by IQ: mild 50 to 55 up to about 70 (about 85 percent of cases), moderate 35 to 40 up to 50 to 55, severe 20 to 25 up to 35 to 40, and profound below 20 to 25. DSM-5 later renamed the condition intellectual disability and graded severity by adaptive functioning instead. Example: Kabir, aged 9, scores 68 on the WISC and needs help with dressing and handling money; he meets both the IQ and the adaptive criteria. Option b is right because 70 lies exactly two SD below the mean of 100. Exam trap: one SD below the mean (85) marks the upper edge of borderline intellectual functioning (71 to 84), not the cut-off for mental retardation.", "topic": "Intellectual disability IQ criterion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Trait theorists use: I. 16 PF - NEO-PI - MBTI II. MMPI - EPQ - Rorschach test III. TAT - MBTI - 16 PF IV. EPQ - MMPI - NEO-PI", "options": {"a": "I and IV only", "b": "I, III and IV only", "c": "I and III only", "d": "II and IV only"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: sets I and IV are the tests trait theorists use. Trait theorists describe personality as a small number of stable dimensions on which everyone can be placed, and they measure them with structured self-report inventories scored against norms. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949) measures 16 source traits found by factor analysis; Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's NEO Personality Inventory (NEO-PI, 1985) measures the Big Five; Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck's Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, 1975) measures psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI, Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is an objective self-report inventory scored on scales, so such questions file it with the trait measures. The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) of Isabel Briggs Myers and Katharine Briggs (published 1962) is also a structured questionnaire. Sets II and III each contain a projective technique: the Rorschach inkblots (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT, Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), which present ambiguous material, assume that people project hidden motives into it, and are scored by interpretation. Example: Meera, studying conscientiousness in bank staff, gives them the NEO-PI and the 16PF, not inkblots. Option a is right because I and IV are the only sets made up entirely of structured inventories. Exam trap: one projective test spoils a whole set, so scan each set for the Rorschach or the TAT first; that removes II and III, and with them options b, c and d. Key note: strictly, the MBTI is a type measure based on Carl Jung's (1921) psychological types, not a trait measure, and the MMPI was built by empirical keying rather than from trait theory; but I and IV is still the only option without a projective test, so a stands.", "topic": "Trait measures versus projectives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Find out the correct answers from the codes given below: Topographical organization: 1. Applies to sensory cortical areas 2. High degree of topographical organisation shows in the auditory pathway 3. Fibres in this pathway originate from the ganglion cells of the retina 4. Fibres then synapse at the lateral geniculate body of the hypothalamus", "options": {"a": "1, 3 and 4 are correct", "b": "2 and 3 are correct", "c": "1 and 3 are correct", "d": "1 and 4 are correct"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 3 are correct. Topographical (topographic) organisation means that neighbouring points on a sensory surface are represented by neighbouring points in the brain, so each sensory cortex carries an orderly map: retinotopic in vision (the primary visual cortex maps the retina, with the small fovea given a disproportionately large share), tonotopic in hearing (each sound frequency has its own place in auditory cortex) and somatotopic in touch (the body map, or homunculus, of the postcentral gyrus). So statement 1, that it applies to sensory cortical areas, is true. Statement 3 describes the visual pathway: the fibres of the optic nerve are the axons of the retina's ganglion cells, which carry the signal out of the eye, so it is true. Statement 4 is false: optic tract fibres synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (body) of the thalamus, not of the hypothalamus, before the optic radiations carry the signal to the striate cortex (area 17). The hypothalamus receives only a small retinal input, to its suprachiasmatic nucleus, for setting circadian rhythms. Example: a small stroke in one spot of Arjun's left primary visual cortex blinds one matching patch of his right visual field, because the map keeps retinal neighbours together. Option c is right because 1 and 3 are true and 4 is false. Exam trap: the lateral geniculate body sounds hypothalamic because both lie in the diencephalon, but the geniculate bodies are thalamic relays (lateral for vision, medial for hearing). Key note: statement 2 names the auditory pathway, which is tonotopic in reality, but statement 3 then says 'this pathway' starts in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot both describe the same pathway; the paired statements describe the visual pathway and c (1 and 3) is the only fully correct set; c stands.", "topic": "Topographic maps, visual pathway", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the notion of confirmatory bias?", "options": {"a": "Clinicians ignore information that does not support their hypotheses", "b": "Clinicians ignore information that does not support their stereotypes", "c": "Clinicians interpret ambiguous information as supporting their hypotheses", "d": "All the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe confirmatory (confirmation) bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice, interpret and remember information in ways that support what one already believes, while giving too little weight to information that contradicts it. Peter Wason (1960) showed it with his 2-4-6 task: told that the triple 2-4-6 fits a rule, people tested only triples that fitted their own guess (such as 8-10-12) and rarely tried triples that could prove the guess wrong, so they often announced a wrong rule with confidence. In clinical work the bias appears in three linked ways. A clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may ignore information that does not fit it (a). A clinician may hold on to a stereotype about a group, such as assuming an elderly client's low mood is just part of ageing, and disregard evidence against it (b). And when information is ambiguous, such as a vague answer in an interview, the clinician tends to read it as support for the favoured hypothesis (c). Together these lead to premature closure, where assessment stops too early. Example: within five minutes Dr Kabir decides that Riya has borderline personality disorder; he notes every sign of mood swings, skips her clear history of hypomanic episodes, and reads her tearfulness as further proof. Option d is right because ignoring disconfirming data, clinging to stereotypes and reading ambiguity as support are all forms of the same bias. Exam trap: each single option is true, so picking one of them alone loses the mark; when every part describes the same process, 'all the above' is the answer.", "topic": "Confirmation bias in clinical judgement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "OCD is NOT thought to play a role in which of the following cognitive constructs?", "options": {"a": "Control of thoughts", "b": "Underestimation of personal ability", "c": "Intolerance of uncertainty", "d": "Beliefs about inflated responsibility"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: underestimation of personal ability is not one of the cognitive constructs thought to play a role in obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Cognitive models of OCD start from the finding that almost everyone has odd, unwanted intrusive thoughts. Paul Salkovskis (1985) argued that OCD develops when a person appraises such intrusions as meaning that he or she is responsible for preventing harm, which drives neutralising and compulsions. Stanley Rachman (1993) added thought-action fusion, the belief that thinking about an act makes it more likely or is as bad as doing it. The Obsessive Compulsive Cognitions Working Group (1997, 2005), an international team of researchers, grouped the central beliefs into six domains: inflated responsibility; overimportance of thoughts; importance of controlling one's thoughts; overestimation of threat; intolerance of uncertainty; and perfectionism. They built the Obsessive Beliefs Questionnaire to measure them. A sense of low competence fits depression or low self-efficacy better than OCD, and it is not one of the domains. Example: Arjun has a sudden thought of pushing a stranger onto the railway tracks; he believes that having the thought means he might do it (overimportance of thoughts), that he must stop such thoughts (control), and he checks the platform edge again and again because he cannot bear not being sure (intolerance of uncertainty). Option b is right because it is the only option outside the recognised OCD belief domains. Exam trap: the stem is worded backwards (it asks which construct does not play a role in OCD), and 'control of thoughts' (a) can look like a healthy skill rather than a problem, yet the belief that one must control one's thoughts is itself one of the six domains.", "topic": "Cognitive domains in OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Standardization allows the clinician to:", "options": {"a": "See where an individual client's score on the test falls in relation to the normal distribution of scores for that test", "b": "Estimate the probability with which a client might meet DSM-IV", "c": "Use the score on a particular test to estimate whether a client might meet the diagnostic criteria for a psychological disorder", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this paper marks all of the above. Standardisation has two parts. First, the test is given and scored in the same way for everyone: identical instructions, materials, time limits and scoring rules, so that differences in scores reflect differences between people rather than differences in procedure. Second, the test is given to a large, representative norm sample (the standardisation sample), matched to the target population on age, sex, region, education and so on, and the distribution of their scores becomes the norms. A raw score means little on its own; compared with the norms, it can be turned into a percentile rank, a z score, a T score or a deviation IQ, which shows how far above or below the average of comparable people the client stands (option a). Many clinical tests are also standardised on diagnosed groups, so the norms carry cut-off scores: a T score of 65 or more on an MMPI-2 clinical scale, or a Beck Depression Inventory-II total of 29 or more (the severe range), tells the clinician that the client scores like people who meet criteria for a disorder, and how likely that is (options b and c). Example: Riya gets a raw score of 38 on an anxiety scale; the norm table for women of her age puts this at the 90th percentile, and the manual says most people above this cut-off in the clinical sample met criteria for generalised anxiety disorder, so the clinician follows up with a diagnostic interview. Option d is right on the key's reading because a standardised score both locates the client in the normal distribution and, through clinical norms and cut-offs, suggests whether the client might meet diagnostic criteria. Exam trap: a test score alone never makes a diagnosis; diagnosis still needs an interview, history and clinical judgement. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because norms on their own only locate a score in a distribution and a test score never diagnoses. LBS's official key for this paper marks d, all of the above. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Standardisation and norms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The famous book 'Principles of psychology' was authored by ------.", "options": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt", "b": "E B Titchener", "c": "William James", "d": "J B Watson"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: The Principles of Psychology was written by William James (1890). William James (1842 to 1910) trained in medicine at Harvard and taught physiology, philosophy and psychology there. He worked on the Principles for about twelve years and published it in two volumes in 1890, followed by a shorter version, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892). In the book he described consciousness as a continuous, ever-changing stream of consciousness rather than a set of elements; called habit 'the enormous fly-wheel of society'; split the self into the 'I' (the self as knower) and the 'me' (the self as known, with material, social and spiritual parts); and set out his theory of emotion, proposed independently by Carl Lange (1885), that we feel afraid because we tremble and run, not the other way round. He defined psychology as 'the science of mental life'. The Principles made James the father of American psychology and the inspiration of functionalism, which asked what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Option c is right because The Principles of Psychology is William James's book. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (a) wrote a book with a look-alike title, Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), the founding text of experimental psychology, so a hurried reader picks a.", "topic": "William James's Principles", "fig": "William James"}, {"q": "The functionalists are strongly influenced by ------.", "options": {"a": "Charles Darwin", "b": "Wilhelm Wundt", "c": "E B Titchener", "d": "William James"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the functionalists were strongly influenced by Charles Darwin. In On the Origin of Species (1859), Charles Darwin argued that living things evolve by natural selection: features that help an organism adapt to its environment survive and are passed on. Functionalism, the first truly American school of psychology, carried this idea into the study of the mind. Instead of asking, as the structuralists did, what consciousness is made of, the functionalists asked what it is for: how mental processes such as perception, memory, emotion and habit help the organism adapt and survive. William James (1890) laid the ground in The Principles of Psychology; John Dewey's paper on the reflex arc (1896) and James Rowland Angell's 'The Province of Functional Psychology' (1907) made Chicago the centre of the school, and Harvey Carr (1925) took the adaptive act as its unit. Darwin's influence also explains functionalism's openness to animal psychology, individual differences, child development and applied fields such as education and mental testing, all of which bear on adaptation. Example: a functionalist studying how Kabir learns to drive would ask how attention and habit help him cope with busy Kozhikode traffic, not which sensations make up his experience of the road. Option a is right because Darwin's evolutionary theory of adaptation was the main outside influence that shaped functionalism. Exam trap: William James (d) is the tempting choice, but he is counted among the founders of functionalism, not as an outside influence on it. Key note: some readers argue that James also shaped the Chicago functionalists, which is true; but he is usually classed as a founder or forerunner of the school, and the influence behind all of them, James included, was Darwin's evolutionary theory; a stands.", "topic": "Darwin and functionalism", "fig": "Charles Darwin"}, {"q": "Rejecting the concept of S-R connectionism, further S-O-R concept was developed by:", "options": {"a": "J B Watson", "b": "I P Pavlov", "c": "Woodworth", "d": "Thorndike"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the S-O-R concept was developed by Robert S. Woodworth, who rejected plain S-R connectionism. Edward Thorndike's (1898) connectionism, and later John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism, explained behaviour as learned bonds between a stimulus (S) and a response (R). Robert S. Woodworth (1918), at Columbia University, argued that this left out the most important part: the organism (O) in between. The same stimulus produces different responses depending on what is going on inside the organism: its drives, needs, past learning, expectations, attention and bodily state. In Dynamic Psychology (1918) he stressed drive and motivation, and in his textbook Psychology (1929 edition) he set out the S-O-R formula. This mediational view, with inner states standing between stimulus and response, was later taken up by neobehaviourists such as Clark Hull (1943) and Edward Tolman (1932) and by cognitive psychology. Woodworth, a functionalist, is remembered for making psychology 'dynamic', concerned with why organisms act as well as how. Example: a plate of hot dosas (S) makes hungry Meera reach for one (R), but after a big lunch the same plate gets no response; the difference lies in the organism (O), her state of hunger. Option c is right because the S-O-R formula is Woodworth's correction to S-R connectionism. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike (d) tempts because his name is tied to connectionism, but he is the author of the S-R bonds that Woodworth rejected, not of the S-O-R alternative.", "topic": "Woodworth's S-O-R formula", "fig": "Robert S. Woodworth"}, {"q": "The concept of building block of consciousness was laid by ------.", "options": {"a": "J B Watson", "b": "Wilhelm Wundt", "c": "Sigmund Freud", "d": "Carl Jung"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the idea of building blocks of consciousness was laid down by Wilhelm Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) founded the first formal psychology laboratory at the University of Leipzig, which is why 1879 is taken as the birth of psychology as a separate experimental science. He defined psychology as the study of immediate conscious experience and tried to analyse that experience into its simplest elements, much as chemists break compounds into elements. He named two kinds of elements: sensations, with quality and intensity, and simple feelings, which he placed on three axes in his tridimensional theory of feeling (1896): pleasure-displeasure, tension-relaxation and excitement-depression. These elements combine by creative synthesis into complex experiences such as perceptions and ideas, the whole gaining properties the parts lack, as a melody is more than its notes. His method was introspection under experimental control, with trained observers making short judgements of signalled stimuli, together with reaction-time measures. His student Edward Titchener (1898) took the element idea to Cornell and made it the core of structuralism, listing sensations, images and feelings as the basic elements. Example: when Arjun hears a single tone in a Wundtian experiment, he reports its pitch and loudness and whether it feels pleasant, tense or calm, breaking the experience into its parts. Option b is right because analysing consciousness into elementary building blocks is Wundt's programme. Exam trap: Edward Titchener is the name usually tied to structuralism, but he is not offered here; John B. Watson (a) is the opposite pole, since he rejected consciousness altogether.", "topic": "Wundt's elements of consciousness", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "A provisional theory to explain observed facts is known as ------.", "options": {"a": "Concept", "b": "Construct", "c": "Hypothesis", "d": "Statement"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a provisional theory put forward to explain observed facts is a hypothesis. A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement about the relationship between two or more variables, offered as a possible explanation of something observed and then checked against data. It is provisional: research may support it, or it may be rejected and replaced. A good hypothesis is clear, specific, testable (falsifiable, in Karl Popper's (1959) sense), consistent with known facts, and stated in terms of variables that can be measured. Researchers usually write a research (alternative) hypothesis, such as 'sleep loss impairs recall', and a null hypothesis of no difference or no relationship, which statistical tests try to reject. The other terms are building blocks rather than explanations. A concept is an abstraction formed by grouping observations that share features, such as 'reward' or 'fatigue'. A construct is a concept deliberately invented or adopted for scientific use, often not directly observable, such as anxiety, intelligence or self-esteem, and defined through the operations that measure it. Example: Meera notices that students who sleep late score poorly in morning tests; she proposes that sleep loss lowers memory performance and tests it by comparing recall in students who slept 4 hours with those who slept 8. Option c is right because a hypothesis is exactly a provisional explanation of observed facts awaiting test. Exam trap: construct (b) sounds technical and theory-like, but a construct is a variable to be measured, not an explanation of facts; and a theory proper is broader than a hypothesis, a tested framework that links many hypotheses.", "topic": "Hypothesis, concept and construct", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Author of \"A Theory of Human Motivation\":", "options": {"a": "Abraham Maslow", "b": "Rollo May", "c": "Victor Frankl", "d": "Carl Rogers"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'A Theory of Human Motivation' was written by Abraham Maslow (1943). Abraham Maslow published this paper in Psychological Review in 1943. In it he proposed the hierarchy of needs: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs, love and belongingness needs, esteem needs, and at the top self-actualisation, the need to realise one's full potential. Lower, more basic needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher needs become strong motives; a starving person thinks of food, not of status. He expanded the idea in Motivation and Personality (1954), where he described self-actualising people such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, and later contrasted deficiency needs (D-needs) with growth or being needs (B-needs). Maslow is a founder of humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism. The other three authors also belong to humanistic and existential psychology, which is why they are offered: Rollo May wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950), Viktor Frankl wrote Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German, English 1959), and Carl Rogers wrote On Becoming a Person (1961). Option a is right because the 1943 paper that introduced the hierarchy of needs is Maslow's. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (d) is the other famous humanistic name and also wrote about growth and self-actualisation, but his books are about client-centred therapy and the self, not a theory of motivation.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "According to Freud, resolution of Oedipal complex ------.", "options": {"a": "has no analogous process in girls", "b": "depends on the antipathy of the child towards the father", "c": "is a sign of emergent genital sexuality", "d": "is marked by a sublimation of libidinal energy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: according to Sigmund Freud (1924), resolution of the Oedipus complex is marked by a sublimation of libidinal energy. In Freud's psychosexual theory, during the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) the boy desires his mother and sees his father as a rival. Fearing that the father will punish him by castration (castration anxiety), he represses the desire and identifies with his father, taking in the father's values and standards. This identification forms the superego, the moral part of personality. The resolution carries the child into the latency period (about 6 years to puberty), in which sexual wishes lie dormant and libidinal energy is sublimated, that is, redirected into socially valued activities such as schoolwork, friendships with same-sex peers, games, hobbies and skills. Freud described a parallel process in girls, the feminine Oedipus attitude: penis envy turns the girl from her mother to her father, and she resolves it more slowly and less completely by identifying with her mother. Carl Jung (1913) called this the Electra complex, a term Freud rejected. Example: after the age of six, Kabir stops clinging to his mother, copies his father's way of speaking, and pours his energy into cricket and school projects. Option d is right because the passing of the Oedipus complex leads into latency, where libido is sublimated into non-sexual pursuits. Exam trap: c sounds right because the Oedipal stage is sexual, but genital sexuality emerges only at puberty, after latency, so the resolution is a step away from sexual expression, not its arrival.", "topic": "Resolution of the Oedipus complex", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which one of the following best characterizes the difference between the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems?", "options": {"a": "Voluntary movements versus involuntary movements", "b": "Energy expenditure versus energy conservation", "c": "Peripheral nervous system versus somatic nervous system", "d": "Sensory neurons versus afferent nerve fibres"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the difference between the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems is best described as energy expenditure versus energy conservation. Both are branches of the autonomic nervous system, which, with the somatic nervous system, makes up the peripheral nervous system. The autonomic system works without conscious control, regulating the heart, blood vessels, glands, gut and pupils. The sympathetic branch, whose nerves leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, prepares the body for action and spends energy: heart rate and blood pressure rise, the airways widen, the pupils dilate, the liver releases glucose, the adrenal medulla pours out adrenaline, and digestion slows. Walter Cannon (1915, 1929) called this the fight or flight response. The parasympathetic branch, whose nerves leave the brainstem (including the vagus nerve) and the sacral spinal cord, conserves and restores energy: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils, stimulates salivation and digestion, and supports rest and repair, often summed up as 'rest and digest'. Most organs receive both branches, which work in balance. Example: when a dog suddenly runs at Riya, her heart pounds and her mouth goes dry (sympathetic); an hour later, relaxing after dinner, her heart rate slows and digestion picks up (parasympathetic). Option b is right because spending energy for action versus saving and restoring it is the core contrast between the two branches. Exam trap: a describes the somatic versus autonomic contrast, not sympathetic versus parasympathetic; both autonomic branches control involuntary activity.", "topic": "Sympathetic versus parasympathetic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Multiple Abstract Variance Analysis is used to study the effect of:", "options": {"a": "One independent variable on more than one dependent variables", "b": "More than one independent variables on one dependent variable", "c": "More than one independent variables on more than one dependent variables", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of these, because Multiple Abstract Variance Analysis (MAVA) is not a design for independent and dependent variables at all, and LBS's official key for this paper marks d. MAVA is Raymond Cattell's (1953, 1960) behaviour-genetic method for estimating how much of the variation in a personality trait comes from heredity and how much from environment. Cattell measured the same trait in several kinds of family pairs: identical twins reared together and apart, fraternal twins, siblings reared together and apart, and unrelated children reared together. Comparing how alike each kind of pair is, he solved a set of equations for the hereditary and environmental parts of the trait's variance, within families and between families, and for the correlation between heredity and environment. Hall and Lindzey's Theories of Personality describes it under Cattell's work on nature and nurture. Options a, b and c describe analysis of variance designs instead: a is a one-way multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA), b is a factorial ANOVA, and c is a factorial MANOVA. The names sound alike, but MAVA does not study the effect of manipulated independent variables on dependent variables; it splits the variance of one trait into its sources. Example: if identical twins reared apart are almost as alike in dominance as identical twins reared together, MAVA puts most of the variance in dominance down to heredity. Option d is right because none of the three ANOVA-type descriptions fits Cattell's method. Exam trap: the abbreviation tempts students to read MAVA as MANOVA and pick c. Key note: this mock used to mark c, on the reading that the options describe MANOVA and the stem names it loosely. LBS's official key for this paper marks d, none of these, which is the strict answer for Cattell's MAVA, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Cattell's MAVA (nature and nurture)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Parameter : statistic :: population mean : ------.", "options": {"a": "Standard deviation", "b": "Sample median", "c": "Kurtosis", "d": "Skewness"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: as a parameter is to a statistic, so the population mean is to a sample measure, and the only sample measure offered is the sample median. A population is the whole group a researcher wants to describe, such as all college students in Kerala; a sample is the part actually studied. A parameter is a numerical value that describes a population. It is usually unknown and is written with a Greek letter: mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation, rho for the population correlation. A statistic is the matching value calculated from a sample and is written with a Roman letter: x-bar for the sample mean, s for the sample standard deviation, r for the sample correlation. Statistics are used to estimate parameters, and they vary from sample to sample (sampling error), while a parameter is fixed. The analogy therefore needs a measure taken from a sample. The exact counterpart of the population mean would be the sample mean, which is not offered; the sample median is another measure of central tendency computed from a sample, so it is the only statistic among the options. Example: the average height of all Class 12 students in India is a parameter; Kabir measures 200 students in Kozhikode and finds their median height is 165 cm, which is a statistic. Option b is right because it is the only option explicitly described as a sample value, the second half of the parameter to statistic analogy. Exam trap: standard deviation (a) looks like a natural partner for the mean, but without the word 'sample' it could describe a population (a parameter) just as well, so it does not complete the analogy.", "topic": "Parameter versus statistic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "------ is not an important determinant when considering the effects of modelling on learning.", "options": {"a": "The role of reinforcement", "b": "Retention of the material modelled", "c": "The sex of the model", "d": "The functional value of the model's behaviour"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the sex of the model is not one of the important determinants listed when considering the effects of modelling on learning. Albert Bandura (1977), in his social learning theory, explained observational learning (modelling) through four processes. Attention: the observer must notice the model; this depends on the modelling stimuli, including the functional value of the model's behaviour (whether it brings useful results) and its distinctiveness, and on the observer's own characteristics such as arousal and past reinforcement. Retention: the observed act must be stored in memory as images and verbal codes and rehearsed. Motor reproduction: the observer must be able to turn the stored memory into action. Motivation: the observer needs a reason to perform, which comes from reinforcement, whether external, vicarious (seeing the model rewarded) or self-reinforcement. So reinforcement, retention and functional value are all named determinants. The classic evidence is the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961): children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the aggression. Example: Meera learns to roll chapatis by watching her grandmother; she must notice the technique, remember it, have the skill to copy it, and want the praise that follows. Option c is right because the sex of the model, as such, is not one of the determinants in Bandura's list. Exam trap: reinforcement (a) and retention (b) sound like general learning terms rather than modelling ones, but both are part of Bandura's account. Key note: the Bobo doll study did find sex-of-model effects (the male model was imitated more for physical aggression), so model characteristics matter through similarity and status; but the sex of the model as such is the one option not listed among the determinants, so c stands.", "topic": "Determinants of observational learning", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "A social psychologist finds that subjects who drink coffee before viewing a videotape of a comedian find her to be funnier than subjects who didn't drink coffee. These results best support:", "options": {"a": "James-Lange theory", "b": "Inoculation theory", "c": "Cognitive-physiological theory of emotion", "d": "Social comparison theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the coffee result best supports Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) cognitive-physiological, or two-factor, theory of emotion. The theory says an emotion has two ingredients: a state of physiological arousal and a cognitive label that explains it. Arousal itself is fairly undifferentiated; the same racing heart can be felt as fear, anger or joy, and the situation tells us which. In their classic study, Schachter and Singer injected volunteers with adrenaline (epinephrine), told some what side effects to expect and left others uninformed or misinformed, then placed each with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. The uninformed participants, who had no ready explanation for their arousal, tended to catch the stooge's mood, while the informed ones put their pounding hearts down to the injection. In the coffee study the caffeine raises arousal, the viewers do not attribute it to the drink, and they read it as amusement at the comedian: the arousal is misattributed to the most obvious cause in view. Dolf Zillmann (1971) developed the same idea as excitation transfer, in which leftover arousal from one source intensifies the emotion felt towards the next. Example: Arjun runs up four flights of stairs to a friend's party, then laughs louder than ever at a mediocre joke, because his lingering arousal is labelled as mirth. Option c is right because the result depends on arousal plus a cognitive label, the two factors of Schachter and Singer's theory. Exam trap: the James-Lange theory also puts bodily arousal first, so it tempts, but it has no labelling step; it says a specific bodily pattern is itself the emotion, so it cannot explain why non-specific caffeine arousal turns into amusement in particular.", "topic": "Two-factor theory of emotion", "fig": "Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer"}, {"q": "Carol Gilligan's criticism of Kohlberg's theory of moral development, center around:", "options": {"a": "Gender differences in orientations towards morality", "b": "A lack of emphasis on socialization", "c": "An absence of cross-cultural support", "d": "The way in which morality is operationalised"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: Carol Gilligan's criticism of Lawrence Kohlberg's theory centres on gender differences in orientations towards morality. Kohlberg (1958) scored how people reasoned about dilemmas, such as whether Heinz should steal a drug to save his dying wife, and placed the reasons in three levels and six stages: preconventional (obedience, self-interest), conventional (approval, law and order) and postconventional (social contract, universal principles). The top stages rest on an ethic of justice: abstract rights, fairness and impartial rules. Gilligan, who had worked with Kohlberg at Harvard, argued in In a Different Voice (1982) that the scale was built on a male model. Kohlberg's founding sample was all boys, and when women were scored by his manual they often landed at stage 3 (the good boy, nice girl orientation) and so looked less morally mature. Gilligan held that many women reason in an ethic of care: concern for relationships, responsibility to particular people and a wish that no one be hurt. From interviews with 29 women deciding about abortion, she traced three levels of care: caring only for oneself, goodness as self-sacrifice, and the morality of nonviolence, which balances care for self and others. Lawrence Walker's (1984) review later found little sex difference in Kohlberg scores once education was allowed for, but the care and justice distinction survived: both sexes use both voices. Example: Meera answers the Heinz dilemma by asking how Heinz, his wife and the chemist could talk it out and keep their relationships, not which rule ranks higher; Kohlberg's scoring would rate her lower. Option a is right because Gilligan's critique is precisely that the theory is gender biased and neglects the care orientation. Exam trap: Kohlberg was also criticised for weak cross-cultural support (Richard Shweder, 1987; John Snarey's 1985 review of 45 studies), so option c is a real criticism, but it belongs to those critics, not to Gilligan.", "topic": "Gilligan's ethic of care", "fig": "Carol Gilligan"}, {"q": "The sensory modality which is most integrated with limbic system?", "options": {"a": "Visual", "b": "Auditory", "c": "Olfactory", "d": "Tactile"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: smell (olfaction) is the sensory modality most closely integrated with the limbic system. Every other sense sends its signals to the cortex through a relay in the thalamus: vision through the lateral geniculate nucleus, hearing through the medial geniculate nucleus, touch and taste through the ventral posterior nuclei. Smell is the exception. Odour molecules bind receptors in the olfactory epithelium high in the nose, the olfactory nerve (cranial nerve I) carries the signal to the olfactory bulb, and the bulb projects directly to the piriform (primary olfactory) cortex, the amygdala and the entorhinal cortex, the gateway to the hippocampus. These are limbic and medial temporal structures for emotion and memory, and the old name for this region, the rhinencephalon or 'smell brain', records the link. Only later does information reach the orbitofrontal cortex, partly through the thalamus, for conscious identification of the odour. Because the route to emotion and memory is so short, smells trigger vivid, emotional memories, often from childhood. This is called the Proust effect, after Marcel Proust's madeleine dipped in tea in Swann's Way (1913), the first volume of In Search of Lost Time. Example: the scent of jasmine at a wedding instantly takes Riya back to her grandmother's house, with a rush of warmth, before she can even name the flower. Option c is right because olfaction alone projects from receptor to limbic structures without a thalamic relay, giving it the most direct link to emotion and memory. Exam trap: vision has the most cortex and touch the largest receptor surface, but both relay through the thalamus, as hearing does; the same fact is often asked as 'the only sense that bypasses the thalamus', and that answer is also smell, not taste.", "topic": "Olfaction and the limbic system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Put the parts of the brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: 1. Metencephalon 2. Mesencephalon 3. Diancephalon 4. Telencephalon 5. Myelencephalon", "options": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "b": "2, 4, 1, 3, 5", "c": "4, 3, 2, 1, 5", "d": "3, 2, 1, 5, 4"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: from higher to lower the order is telencephalon (4), diencephalon (3), mesencephalon (2), metencephalon (1) and myelencephalon (5). The names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube forms three swellings: the forebrain (prosencephalon), the midbrain (mesencephalon) and the hindbrain (rhombencephalon). Before birth the forebrain divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: cortex, basal ganglia, limbic structures and corpus callosum. The diencephalon ('between-brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with its tectum (the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow-brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. Example: Arjun remembers the order with a simple rule: telencephalon on top, then the other four in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: metencephalon and mesencephalon differ by one letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it. Option d keeps the middle of the order but drops the telencephalon to the bottom.", "topic": "Five divisions of the brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In prosopagnosia disorder a person is not able to:", "options": {"a": "Perceive size", "b": "Perceive colour", "c": "Perceive faces", "d": "Perceive shape"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: in prosopagnosia a person is not able to recognise faces. Prosopagnosia, or face blindness, is a visual agnosia: a failure of recognition despite intact sensation and intellect. The term was coined by the German neurologist Joachim Bodamer (1947), from the Greek prosopon, face, and agnosia, not knowing. The patient sees a face as a face, with eyes, nose and mouth, and can describe its features, but cannot tell whose face it is, even that of a spouse, a child or their own reflection in a mirror. They know people at once by voice, walk, clothes or a mole, which shows that the problem is specific to faces rather than to memory for people. The acquired form follows damage, usually bilateral or right-sided, to the fusiform gyrus on the underside of the brain where the occipital and temporal lobes meet. Nancy Kanwisher (1997) used fMRI to identify the fusiform face area there, a region that responds far more strongly to faces than to other objects. A developmental form, present from childhood without obvious brain injury, affects about 2 percent of people. Example: after a right occipitotemporal stroke, Kabir walks straight past his own wife at the railway station and recognises her only when she calls his name. Option c is right because face recognition is exactly the ability prosopagnosia removes, while the perception of size, colour and shape is spared. Exam trap: the stem says 'perceive', but prosopagnosia is a failure to recognise faces rather than to see them; the cortical colour loss in option b is a separate syndrome, cerebral achromatopsia, which can accompany prosopagnosia because the two regions lie close together.", "topic": "Prosopagnosia (face blindness)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Adler, b. Bandura, c. Jung, d. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "options": {"a": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4", "c": "a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1", "d": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (a-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (b-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (c-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (d-1). Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), founder of individual psychology, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal, never fully real, that pulls a person's behaviour forward, such as \"I must be the best\". Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) described reciprocal determinism (1978), later called triadic reciprocal causation (1986): person factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) treated libido as general psychic energy governed by principles borrowed from physics. By the principle of equivalence, energy withdrawn from one interest reappears in another of equal value; by the principle of entropy, energy flows toward a balance that is never reached. Karen Horney (The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, 1937) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives the strategies of moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Kabir retires from cricket and pours the same passion into coaching, Jung would call it the principle of equivalence. Option c is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs are correct. Exam trap: option d gets Adler and Horney right but swaps Bandura and Jung; \"reciprocal\" and \"equivalence\" both sound like a balance of forces, so students trade them. Bandura is the social-cognitive theorist of three interacting factors; Jung is the energy theorist.", "topic": "Personality theorists and concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to E.C. Tolman, there are two aversions: fright and pugnacity. Fright is avoidance of injury and pugnacity is avoidance of:", "options": {"a": "Reinforcement", "b": "Interference", "c": "Punishment", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: in Edward C. Tolman's scheme, pugnacity is the avoidance of interference (obstruction). Tolman (1886 to 1959) was a purposive behaviourist: he studied whole, goal-directed acts and inferred intervening variables, such as cognitive maps and expectancies, between stimulus and response. In Purposive Behavior in Animals and Men (1932) he also classified the drives that set an animal's goals. The basic, first-order drives fall into two groups. Appetites are drives towards something the body needs, such as food, water and sex; they rise with deprivation and fall once the goal object is reached. Aversions are drives away from something, set off by a disturbing situation rather than by a bodily deficit, and Tolman named two. Fright is the drive to avoid injury: it moves the animal away from a source of pain or danger, usually by flight. Pugnacity is the drive to avoid interference: it is roused when something blocks or obstructs the animal's ongoing activity, and it shows as fighting to remove the obstacle. The pairing echoes William McDougall's (1908) instincts, in which flight goes with fear and pugnacity with anger. Example: when a stray dog snarls at Kabir he runs away (fright), but when his younger brother snatches the cricket bat from his hands mid-game, Kabir shoves him to get it back (pugnacity). Option b is right because Tolman defined pugnacity as the aversion that removes obstruction or interference to behaviour. Exam trap: 'punishment' sounds right because aversions involve unpleasant events, but avoiding painful harm is fright, the other aversion; pugnacity is not about escaping harm at all but about removing whatever gets in the way.", "topic": "Tolman's appetites and aversions", "fig": "Edward C. Tolman"}, {"q": "Which of the following variable(s) can be labelled as organismic variable(s)? 1. Task complexity 2. Intelligence 3. Errors made while learning task 4. Heart rate", "options": {"a": "4 only", "b": "2 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 4 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: intelligence and heart rate are organismic variables, so 2 and 4 only. Robert Woodworth (1869 to 1962), whose Dynamic Psychology (1918) put the organism and its drives at the centre, expanded the behaviourist stimulus-response (S-R) formula into S-O-R, placing the organism between stimulus and response, and experimental psychology still sorts variables this way. Stimulus or task variables are features of the situation or task the experimenter presents: task complexity, list length, noise, instructions. Organismic (O, or subject) variables are characteristics or states that the participant brings to the study: age, sex, intelligence, personality, motivation, fatigue, and physiological states such as heart rate or blood sugar. Response variables are the behaviour measured as the outcome, the dependent variable: errors, reaction time, trials to criterion. Organismic variables usually cannot be manipulated, so they are measured, selected or controlled (by matching or random assignment); left uncontrolled, they act as extraneous variables. Example: Arjun learns a finger maze. The number of turns in the maze is a task variable, his IQ and resting heart rate are organismic variables, and the errors he makes are the response variable. Option b is right because only intelligence (a trait) and heart rate (a physiological state) belong to the organism. Exam trap: errors made while learning come from the person, so they feel organismic, but they are the response being measured (the DV); task complexity is a property of the task. Key note: heart rate is recorded as a response measure in some experiments, for example in psychophysiology, but as a state of the organism it is an O variable here; b stands.", "topic": "Organismic variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A large body of research indicates that violence and aggression is learned through observation. Reason (R): Learning through observation takes place due to exposure to similar situation and experienced reinforcement.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "Both (A) and (R) are false"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false. Assertion: a large body of research shows that aggression is learned through observation. True. In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hammer an inflatable doll later copied the model's exact acts and words far more than children who saw a calm model or none, without any reward. Their 1963 follow-up found filmed and cartoon models worked too, and decades of media-violence research point the same way. Reason: observational learning happens because of exposure to the same situation and experienced reinforcement. False. Observational learning needs neither. The observer learns by watching someone else, often in a situation they have never been in, and need not be reinforced. Bandura's four processes are attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation; the first three govern learning and the fourth governs performance. In Bandura's 1965 study, children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet when offered a reward for showing what the model had done, the differences between the groups all but disappeared: they had learned without reinforcement. Seeing a model rewarded or punished (vicarious reinforcement) changes whether an act is performed, not whether it is learned. Example: Kabir has never been in a fight, but after watching a film hero slap a rival he tries the same move on a classmate. Option c is right because A is well supported while R describes direct, operant learning rather than observational learning. Exam trap: R uses the language of reinforcement, which fits the earlier imitation theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941), who held that imitation must itself be reinforced; Bandura showed it need not be.", "topic": "Observational learning of aggression", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "You have tried all morning to use the phone, but have not been able to because you can't get a dial tone. You finally stop trying. Later in the afternoon you decide to try to use the phone again. Your afternoon behaviour is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Extinction", "b": "Shaping", "c": "Fixed-action pattern", "d": "Spontaneous recovery"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: trying the phone again in the afternoon, after giving up in the morning, is spontaneous recovery. The term comes from Ivan Pavlov's (1927) work on classical conditioning, and the same effect is seen in operant behaviour. When a learned response stops being reinforced, it weakens and finally stops: this is extinction. In Pavlov's dogs, a bell (the conditioned stimulus) that was no longer followed by food (the unconditioned stimulus) gradually stopped producing salivation. But if the dog was then left to rest and the bell was sounded again later, salivation reappeared, weaker than before, without any new pairing. That reappearance after a rest is spontaneous recovery. Pavlov concluded that extinction does not erase learning; it builds an inhibition that fades with time, letting the old response surface again. If extinction continues, each recovery is smaller and dies out faster. In the stem, picking up the phone is a response that a dial tone has reinforced thousands of times. All morning the dial tone is missing, so the response goes unreinforced and the person stops trying: that is extinction (option a). After an interval with no further training, the response returns in the afternoon: spontaneous recovery. The same idea explains why a phobia treated by exposure can partly return after a gap, and why therapists plan booster sessions. Option d is right because the afternoon attempt is a previously extinguished response returning after a rest period, without new reinforcement. Exam trap: option a describes the morning, not the afternoon; examiners put the extinction phase and the recovery phase in one story so that the student labels the whole story by its first half.", "topic": "Spontaneous recovery", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "All of the following are used to study perceptual development EXCEPT", "options": {"a": "The visual cliff", "b": "The rod and frame apparatus", "c": "Preferential looking", "d": "Animal experiments"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the rod and frame apparatus is not a method for studying perceptual development. Researchers need ways to learn what a baby, who cannot speak, sees. Robert Fantz (1961) introduced preferential looking: two stimuli are shown side by side and an observer times how long the infant looks at each; a reliable preference shows the infant can tell them apart, and young infants looked longest at faces and patterns. Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960) built the visual cliff, a glass-topped table with a checkerboard just under the glass on the shallow side and far below on the deep side; most crawling infants aged 6 to 14 months refused to cross the deep side. Animal experiments allow control of early experience that would be unethical in children: Richard Held and Alan Hein's (1963) kitten carousel showed that kittens need self-produced movement to develop visually guided behaviour, Colin Blakemore and Grahame Cooper (1970) reared kittens seeing only vertical stripes, and Torsten Wiesel and David Hubel (1963) showed that closing one eye early permanently changes the visual cortex. The rod and frame test of Herman Witkin (1948 to 1954) is different: in a dark room a person sets a luminous rod to vertical inside a tilted luminous frame. Field-dependent people tilt the rod towards the frame; field-independent people trust their bodily sense of upright. It measures a cognitive style in older children and adults whose perception is already mature. Example: to learn whether 4-month-old Aarav can tell red from green, a researcher times his looks at the two colours side by side; the rod and frame test could not be given to him. Option b is right because the rod and frame test measures an individual difference in cognitive style, not how perception develops. Exam trap: the rod and frame test is a genuine perception task, so it looks as if it belongs; but it compares mature perceivers rather than tracing development.", "topic": "Methods in perceptual development", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered as a binocular cue for depth?", "options": {"a": "Movement parallax", "b": "Accommodation", "c": "Stereopsis", "d": "Texture gradient"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: stereopsis is a binocular cue for depth. Depth cues are the pieces of information the visual system uses to turn a flat retinal image into a three-dimensional world. Binocular cues need both eyes; monocular cues work with one eye alone. Because the eyes sit about 6 cm apart, each retina receives a slightly different view of any nearby object. The difference between the two images is binocular (retinal) disparity, and stereopsis is the vivid sense of depth the brain builds by fusing the two views and computing that disparity: the greater the disparity, the nearer the object. Charles Wheatstone (1838) demonstrated it with his stereoscope, and Bela Julesz (1960) showed with random-dot stereograms that disparity alone, with no other cue, can create depth. The second binocular cue is convergence, the inward turning of the eyes to fixate a near object, which the brain reads from the eye muscles. Monocular cues include the pictorial cues (linear perspective, interposition, relative size, texture gradient, shading, aerial perspective), motion parallax, and accommodation, the change in lens shape for focusing. Example: Meera closes one eye and tries to thread a needle; she keeps missing, because without binocular disparity her fine judgement of near depth fails. Option c is right because stereopsis depends on the two eyes' different views and cannot exist with one eye. Exam trap: accommodation and convergence are both oculomotor (muscle) cues and are often listed together, but only convergence needs two eyes; accommodation happens in each eye separately, so it is monocular.", "topic": "Binocular depth cues", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If a researcher asks a subject to memorise a list of words and then asks her to fill out a questionnaire about how she memorised the list, the researcher is probably studying", "options": {"a": "Semantic priming", "b": "Retroactive inhibition", "c": "Metamemory", "d": "Spreading activation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: asking someone how she memorised a word list is a study of metamemory. Metamemory, a term introduced by John Flavell (1971), is knowledge about one's own memory and its control: knowing how good your memory is, which tasks are hard, which strategies work (rehearsal, grouping, imagery, mnemonics) and when you have studied enough. It is the memory part of metacognition, thinking about one's own thinking, which Flavell (1979) developed further. Metamemory has a monitoring side, as in judgements of learning and the feeling of knowing (the sense that you could recognise an answer you cannot recall), and a control side, as in deciding how long to study and which strategy to use. Researchers measure it with questionnaires and interviews about strategies, with predictions of how many items one will recall, and with confidence ratings. Developmental work shows that young children overestimate their memory span and seldom rehearse spontaneously, while older children know that a long list needs more study. In the stem the memory task itself is only a vehicle; the questionnaire about how she memorised the list asks her to report her own strategies and awareness, which is metamemory. Option c is right because the researcher is collecting the participant's knowledge of her own memorising, not measuring the memory processes themselves. Exam trap: semantic priming and spreading activation also involve word lists and are taught together in network models of memory, but they are measured through speeded responses such as reaction times, not through self-report about strategy.", "topic": "Metamemory", "fig": "John Flavell"}, {"q": "A subject listens to a tape recording of an ambiguous sound and is asked to describe what he heard. This is most analogous to which of the following tests?", "options": {"a": "Thematic apperception test", "b": "Blacky pictures", "c": "MMPI", "d": "Rorschach inkblot test"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: describing what one hears in an ambiguous sound is most like the Rorschach inkblot test. Projective tests rest on the projective hypothesis: when a stimulus is ambiguous and has no right answer, the way a person structures it reveals their needs, conflicts and habits of perceiving. Lawrence Frank (1939) named the idea, and Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted projective techniques by the response they ask for: association, construction, completion, choice or ordering, and expression. In the Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), the person is shown ten symmetrical inkblots and asked 'What might this be?'; the examiner scores what is seen, where on the blot (location), which features determined it (form, colour, shading, movement) and how common the response is. This is an association task: the person names what an ambiguous percept looks like. The stem's task is the same, but in sound. B. F. Skinner (1936) built exactly such a tool, the verbal summator, which played faint, meaningless vowel sounds and asked listeners what was being said; it was nicknamed an auditory inkblot, and a similar device was called the tautophone. Example: Arjun hears a muffled recording and says it sounds like someone crying for help, and the clinician reads this much as she would a Rorschach percept. Option d is right because both tasks ask the person to say what an unstructured stimulus resembles, with no story to build and no fixed answer. Exam trap: the TAT is the most famous projective test and also uses ambiguous material, but it asks for a story with a past, present and future about pictures of people, a construction task; saying what a sound is matches the Rorschach's 'what might this be?'.", "topic": "Rorschach and its auditory analogue", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Exceptional performance by a student as a result of high teacher expectations:", "options": {"a": "Primack Principle", "b": "Maintenance Rehearsal", "c": "Pygmalion Effect", "d": "Activation spreading"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: exceptional performance by a student that results from high teacher expectations is the Pygmalion effect. It is a self-fulfilling prophecy in the classroom: an expectation, true or false at first, changes the expecter's behaviour so that the expected outcome comes about. The name comes from the Greek myth of the sculptor Pygmalion, whose statue came to life, and from George Bernard Shaw's play Pygmalion (1913). Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson (1968) tested it at Oak School, a primary school in California. They gave every child a non-verbal intelligence test, disguised as the 'Harvard Test of Inflected Acquisition', then told teachers that about 20 percent of the pupils were 'bloomers' who would spurt intellectually in the coming year. The bloomers had in fact been chosen at random. Eight months later these children, especially in the first and second grades, had gained more IQ points than their classmates. The study was published as Pygmalion in the Classroom (1968). Later work showed that teachers give high-expectancy pupils a warmer climate, more input, more chances to respond and more feedback (Rosenthal's four-factor theory); in replications the effect is real but smaller than first claimed. The reverse, low expectations lowering performance, is the Golem effect. Example: told that Kabir is a late bloomer, his teacher calls on him more, praises his answers and sets him harder problems, and by the year's end his marks rise to match her belief. Option c is right because the stem defines the Pygmalion effect: the teacher's expectation itself producing the student's high performance. Exam trap: the Premack principle also concerns classrooms and rewards, so it tempts; but it is about using a preferred activity as a reinforcer, not about expectations.", "topic": "Pygmalion effect", "fig": "Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson"}, {"q": "Author of \"On Becoming a Person\":", "options": {"a": "Rollo May", "b": "J B Watson", "c": "Carl Rogers", "d": "E B Titchener"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: On Becoming a Person (1961) was written by Carl Rogers. Rogers (1902 to 1987), an American psychologist, founded client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, one of the main strands of humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism. On Becoming a Person: A Therapist's View of Psychotherapy (1961) is a collection of his essays and talks written for a general readership. In it Rogers argues that people have an innate actualising tendency and grow towards becoming a fully functioning person (open to experience, living in the present, trusting their own feelings) when a relationship offers three core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard and accurate empathic understanding. The book also describes the 'good life' as a process rather than a fixed state. His earlier books were Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and Client-Centered Therapy (1951); a later one was A Way of Being (1980). Example: Riya, a counselling student, reads On Becoming a Person and learns that her task is not to give advice but to listen, accept and reflect her client's feelings. Option c is right because Rogers wrote the book, and its title captures his central idea that a person is always in the process of becoming. Exam trap: Rollo May is another humanistic and existential writer of the same period, so his name tempts, but May wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950) and Love and Will (1969); the commonest look-alike title is Abraham Maslow's Toward a Psychology of Being (1962).", "topic": "Rogers: On Becoming a Person", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "If reliability is 0.00, which of the following is true?", "options": {"a": "The standard error of measurement is relatively high", "b": "The standard error of measurement is relatively low", "c": "The test is too long", "d": "Validity is high"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: if reliability is 0.00, the standard error of measurement is relatively high, in fact as high as it can be. In classical test theory every observed score is a true score plus random error, and reliability (r) is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance. The standard error of measurement (SEM) is the standard deviation of the errors one person's scores would show over many retestings, and it is found from SEM = SD x the square root of (1 minus r). For an IQ test with SD 15 and reliability .91, SEM = 15 x the square root of .09 = 15 x .30 = 4.5 points. As reliability falls the SEM rises: at r = 1 the SEM is 0, and at r = 0 it equals the SD itself, 15 points, so the score says nothing about where the true score lies beyond what the whole group's spread already says. A zero-reliability test also has zero validity, because a validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the reliability (the index of reliability). The usual remedy for low reliability is to lengthen the test with parallel items, as the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) predicts. Example: Arjun scores 110 on a scale with reliability 0.00 and SD 15; his 95 percent band would stretch about 29 points either side, so the score is useless for any decision about him. Option a is right because zero reliability means all score variance is error, which pushes the SEM to its maximum, equal to the SD. Exam trap: students sometimes reverse the relation and think low reliability gives low error; reliability and SEM move in opposite directions, so the lowest reliability gives the highest SEM.", "topic": "SEM at zero reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Surgery is one of the methods to understand brain behaviour relations in humans. Reason (R): Lesion method destroys part of the brain.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this item, in both the 2019 and the 2021 paper, marks (A) false and (R) true. Reason: the lesion method destroys part of the brain. True. A lesion is damage to or removal of tissue, and the lesion (ablation) method infers a region's function from what is lost, as in the ablation studies of Pierre Flourens (1820s) and the cortical lesions Karl Lashley made in rats (Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence, 1929). Assertion: surgery is one of the methods to understand brain-behaviour relations in humans. On the key's reading, false: as a research method, surgery means experimental lesioning, deliberately destroying or removing tissue to see what changes, and that is done only in animals. No one may operate on a human brain to find out what it does. In humans, brain-behaviour relations are studied through naturally occurring damage (strokes, tumours, head injuries), neuropsychological testing, electrical recording (EEG, event-related potentials), neuroimaging (CT, MRI, fMRI, PET) and non-invasive stimulation (transcranial magnetic stimulation). Patients who need surgery for epilepsy or a tumour can be tested before and after, and some of the best known findings came that way: Henry Molaison (H.M.), whose medial temporal lobes William Scoville removed in 1953 and whom Brenda Milner tested, and the split-brain patients of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s). But there the surgery is treatment, and the research method is the clinical study of a lesion. Example: a researcher who wants to know what the hippocampus does can lesion it in rats, but in people has to find patients whose hippocampus was damaged by illness or by a necessary operation. Option d is right on the key's reading because R correctly defines the lesion method, while A wrongly lists surgery as a method that can be used on humans. Exam trap: H.M. and the split-brain cases make A look obviously true, so most students pick a. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because therapeutic surgery such as H.M.'s has taught a great deal about the human brain and is a lesion in all but name. LBS's official key marks d for this question in both 2019 and 2021, on the reading that surgery is never a research method in humans. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Surgery and the lesion method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher is interested in the effect that meeting the University Vice Chancellor has on students' perceptions of the University. To obtain subjects for her research, the researcher stands at the entrance of the performing arts building and selects every fifth student who enters the building. This method is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Random sampling", "b": "Stratified random sampling", "c": "Representative sampling", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: selecting every fifth student at the entrance of the performing arts building is systematic selection at a convenient spot, and it is none of the three methods offered. A sample is drawn from a population, here the university's students, and the aim is usually that it represents that population. In probability sampling every member has a known, non-zero chance of selection. Simple random sampling gives every member an equal chance, usually by drawing from a complete list (the sampling frame) with random numbers. Stratified random sampling first divides the population into strata (faculty, year, sex) and then samples randomly within each. Systematic sampling picks every kth unit (k = population size divided by sample size) after a starting point, ideally random, within the list. The researcher in the stem has no list of students at all. She samples only those who walk into one building, so students who never use the performing arts building have no chance of selection, and arts students are likely to be over-represented. The every-fifth rule makes the procedure look systematic, but the frame is a doorway, so it is really a convenience sample with a fixed interval. That is why it is not random sampling (a), not stratified (b), and cannot be assumed representative (c). Option d is right because the method matches none of the labels offered: it is systematic selection from a non-representative location. Exam trap: 'every fifth' looks orderly and fair, so students call it random; regularity is not randomness, and fairness depends on who is in the frame. Key note: some books class systematic sampling under probability sampling when the start is random and the list covers the whole population; here there is no sampling frame and only the performing arts building, so it is neither simple random nor stratified, and representativeness cannot be assumed; d stands.", "topic": "Systematic versus random sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Perception of information that is common or redundant across multiple senses is known as:", "options": {"a": "Rhythmic perception", "b": "Amodal perception", "c": "Harmonic perception", "d": "Haptic perception"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: perceiving information that is common or redundant across several senses is amodal perception. Amodal means 'not tied to one modality'. Some properties of an event can be picked up by more than one sense at once: its rhythm, tempo, duration, intensity, timing and synchrony, and for objects their shape, size and texture. These are amodal properties. When a ball bounces, the eye sees it strike the floor at the same moments, and in the same rhythm, as the ear hears the thud. Lorraine Bahrick and Robert Lickliter (2000) proposed the intersensory redundancy hypothesis: in early infancy, information that is redundant across senses captures attention and is learned before information available to only one sense, which helps babies bind sights and sounds into one event. Elizabeth Spelke (1976) showed 4-month-olds two films side by side with a single soundtrack; they looked longer at the film whose timing matched the sound. The wider skill of combining the senses into one coherent event is called intermodal (intersensory) perception, and detecting amodal information is its core. The same word appears in amodal completion (Albert Michotte, 1964), seeing the hidden part of an occluded object, which is a different idea. Example: 3-month-old Aditi hears her mother clapping and looks at the video of her mother clapping in time rather than the one clapping out of step, because the shared rhythm links sight and sound. Option b is right because the stem defines amodal perception exactly: information that is the same whichever sense carries it. Exam trap: haptic perception also combines more than one kind of input (skin and movement), so it tempts, but it is one modality, active touch, not information shared across different senses.", "topic": "Amodal perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Project Orcon is:", "options": {"a": "Thorndike's Trial and Error work", "b": "Skinner's Pigeon-training work", "c": "Pavlov's research on \"Classical conditioning\"", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Project Orcon was B. F. Skinner's pigeon-training work. During the Second World War, Skinner (1904 to 1990), then at the University of Minnesota, proposed using trained pigeons to guide missiles, because electronic guidance was not yet reliable. The wartime effort, funded first by General Mills and then by the US government, was called Project Pigeon. Using operant conditioning, Skinner shaped pigeons to peck at an image of the target projected on a small screen in the missile's nose cone. Each peck was reinforced with grain, and on an intermittent schedule the birds pecked thousands of times without tiring. Pecks off centre tilted the screen and worked the steering, so the missile corrected its course. In demonstrations the pigeons tracked targets accurately, but officials could not take the idea seriously and the project was dropped in 1944. The US Naval Research Laboratory revived it after the war as Project Orcon, short for 'organic control', from 1948 to 1953; again the pigeons did well until electronic guidance made them unnecessary. Skinner told the whole story in his article 'Pigeons in a Pelican' (1960), the Pelican being the missile. The work shows the power of shaping and of intermittent reinforcement, which Skinner had analysed in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). Example: at a museum display, Kabir sees a nose cone with three small pigeon compartments and learns that birds once nearly steered missiles. Option b is right because Orcon (organic control) was the navy's continuation of Skinner's Project Pigeon. Exam trap: all three named psychologists ran famous animal studies, so any of them could seem to own 'the project'; the clue is 'Orcon', organic control of a guidance system, which fits only Skinner's trained pigeons.", "topic": "Skinner's Project Pigeon and Orcon", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Reminiscence is generally more after:", "options": {"a": "Massed Practice", "b": "Spaced Practice", "c": "Part Learning", "d": "Serial Learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: reminiscence is generally greater after massed practice. Reminiscence is an improvement in recall or performance after a rest interval, with no further practice in between. It runs against the usual forgetting curve, in which retention falls with time. Philip Ballard (1913) named it after finding that schoolchildren who had partly learned a poem recalled more lines two or three days later than right after learning. It was later shown most clearly with motor skills, such as tracking a moving target on a pursuit rotor: after a few minutes' rest, performance jumps above where it stood at the end of practice. The explanation ties reminiscence to the type of practice. In massed practice, trials follow one another with little or no rest, so fatigue, boredom and what Clark Hull (1943) called reactive inhibition (a temporary tendency not to repeat an effortful response) build up and push performance below what has actually been learned. During the rest these temporary effects dissipate and the learned level shows through, so performance rises. In spaced (distributed) practice the rests are built in, inhibition never piles up and performance already reflects learning, so there is little left to recover. Spaced practice still gives better learning and retention overall, as Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) found; massed practice simply leaves more room for a rebound. Example: Meera practises typing for an hour without a break and her speed sags; after a ten-minute tea break she types faster than she did at the end of the hour, with no extra practice. Option a is right because the rebound after rest is largest when massed practice has piled up the fatigue and inhibition that rest removes. Exam trap: spaced practice is the better learning method, so students choose it here; but the question asks where reminiscence, the recovery after rest, is greater, not where learning is better. Key note: LBS's first key for this paper (2 Sep 2019) marked a, but its revised key (17 Sep 2019) changed it to b, spaced practice. The revised key is wrong: reminiscence is reliably greater after massed practice (Carl Hovland, 1938; reviewed by Woodworth and Schlosberg, 1954), because the inhibition that builds up during massed trials wears off in the rest, so a stays.", "topic": "Reminiscence and massed practice", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first attempt for standardization of mental tests in India was made by:", "options": {"a": "Dr. S. M. Mohsin", "b": "V. V. Kamat", "c": "Dr. C.H. Rice", "d": "Dr. Sohen Lal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first attempt to standardise a mental test in India was made by C. Herbert Rice (1922). Standardisation means giving a test to a large, representative sample under uniform conditions, so that norms can be built and any one score can be read against them. Intelligence testing began with Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905) in Paris, and Binet-type scales soon spread across the world. In India, C. Herbert Rice, working in Lahore, began adapting the Binet scale for Indian children in 1922, work that produced the Hindustani Binet Performance Point Scale. As its name says, the scale put Binet-type items into Hindustani, added performance tasks and scored the whole by points, and it was tried out on Indian school children to give local norms. That makes it the first Indian adaptation and standardisation of a Binet-type test. Later Indian work built on it. V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test), and S. M. Mohsin and Sohan Lal developed Hindi group tests of general mental ability. Example: if Meera's teacher wants to know whether Meera's score is high, she needs norms from Indian children like Meera, and Rice's scale was the first to offer them. Option c is right because Rice's work, begun in 1922, came before the work of Kamat, Mohsin and Sohan Lal. Exam trap: the Binet-Kamat test is the best known Indian Binet, so it tempts as 'the first'; it was a later revision, not the first attempt.", "topic": "First Indian test standardisation", "fig": "C. Herbert Rice"}, {"q": "According to psychodynamic theory, individuals are sometimes motivated to respond to the world through the perspectives they have learnt from other important people in their developmental past. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Object permanence theory", "b": "Distorted perspective theory", "c": "Object-relations theory", "d": "Ego defence theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: this is object relations theory. In psychodynamic language an 'object' is not a thing but a person, or part of a person, towards whom feelings and wishes are directed, usually the mother or another early caregiver. Object relations theory holds that the infant takes in (internalises) mental pictures of the self and of important others from early relationships, along with the feelings that link them. These internal objects then act as templates: later in life the person sees and responds to partners, bosses and therapists through the perspectives learnt from those early figures. The school grew in Britain after Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960) described splitting of the world into good and bad objects and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; W. R. D. Fairbairn (1952) argued that the infant is object-seeking rather than pleasure-seeking; Donald Winnicott (1953) described the transitional object and the good-enough mother; Otto Kernberg (1975) linked split internal object relations to borderline personality organisation. Example: Kabir grew up with a harsh, critical father, and as an adult he hears criticism in every neutral remark from his manager, reacting to the internal father image rather than to the manager. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this internalised, relationship-based template learnt from important others. Exam trap: 'object permanence' shares the word 'object' but belongs to Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) sensorimotor stage, where the infant learns that things go on existing when out of sight.", "topic": "Object relations theory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Gamma Amino Butyric Acid (GABD) is a major ------ transmitter.", "options": {"a": "Alerting", "b": "Excitatory", "c": "Facilitatory", "d": "Inhibitory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. GABA is an amino acid transmitter made from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and it is released by many interneurons throughout the cortex and by projection neurons of the basal ganglia and the cerebellum (the Purkinje cells). When it binds the GABA-A receptor, a channel opens and chloride ions flow into the neuron, hyperpolarising it and producing an inhibitory postsynaptic potential, so the cell becomes less likely to fire. The slower GABA-B receptor works through G proteins and potassium channels. Glycine does a similar inhibitory job in the spinal cord and brainstem, while glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter, so the brain's balance of excitation and inhibition rests largely on glutamate and GABA. Many calming drugs act at the GABA-A receptor: benzodiazepines such as diazepam increase how often the channel opens, barbiturates lengthen each opening, and alcohol also enhances GABA action, which is why combining them is dangerous. Too little GABA activity is linked to anxiety and seizures, and GABA neurons of the striatum are lost in Huntington's disease. Example: when Arjun is given diazepam before a dental procedure, it boosts GABA's inhibition and his anxiety settles. Option d is right because GABA's main effect is to inhibit, making neurons less likely to fire. Exam trap: glutamate and GABA are both amino acids and GABA is made from glutamate, so examiners swap them; glutamate is excitatory and GABA inhibitory.", "topic": "GABA, the inhibitory transmitter", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who wrote the book \"Psychotherapy by reciprocal inhibition\" in 1958?", "options": {"a": "Albert Bandura", "b": "Albert Ellis", "c": "Joseph Wolpe", "d": "Andrew Salter"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958) was written by Joseph Wolpe. Wolpe (1915 to 1997) was a South African psychiatrist who later worked at Temple University in Philadelphia. Studying cats, he produced an 'experimental neurosis' by giving shocks in a cage, then removed the cats' fear by feeding them, first far from the cage and gradually nearer to it. From this he took the principle of reciprocal inhibition, a term borrowed from Charles Sherrington's (1906) work on opposing reflexes: if a response incompatible with anxiety is made in the presence of an anxiety-provoking stimulus, it weakens the bond between that stimulus and anxiety. In people, Wolpe used deep muscle relaxation, adapted from Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive relaxation, as the incompatible response. The resulting therapy is systematic desensitisation: the client learns relaxation, builds an anxiety hierarchy of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, and imagines each step while relaxed, moving up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Wolpe also used assertive and sexual responses as anxiety inhibitors, and he reported high success rates with neurotic patients. Example: Meera, afraid of injections, relaxes and imagines first a photograph of a syringe, then a nurse holding one, then the needle near her arm, until even the last step leaves her calm. Option c is right because Wolpe wrote this 1958 book, which founded systematic desensitisation on the principle of reciprocal inhibition. Exam trap: Andrew Salter also wrote an early conditioning-based therapy book and influenced Wolpe's use of assertion, so his name is the near miss; but his book was Conditioned Reflex Therapy (1949).", "topic": "Wolpe and reciprocal inhibition", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Author of \"Man's Search for Meaning\":", "options": {"a": "Victor Frankl", "b": "Carl Jung", "c": "Carl Rogers", "d": "J B Watson"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Man's Search for Meaning was written by Viktor Frankl (spelled Victor in the option). Frankl (1905 to 1997) was an Austrian psychiatrist and neurologist in Vienna. From 1942 to 1945 he was held in Nazi concentration camps, including Auschwitz, and his wife, parents and brother died in the camps. In 1946 he published a short account of camp life in German; it appeared in English in 1959 and became known as Man's Search for Meaning. The first part describes how prisoners survived. Frankl observed that those who kept a reason to live, such as a loved one or unfinished work, endured better, and he quoted Friedrich Nietzsche (Twilight of the Idols, 1889): he who has a why to live can bear almost any how. The second part sets out logotherapy (from the Greek logos, meaning), called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy after the psychoanalysis of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) and the individual psychology of Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937). Logotherapy holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning, not Freud's will to pleasure or Adler's will to power; when it is frustrated, the result is an existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis. Meaning can be found by creating a work or doing a deed, by experiencing something or loving someone, and through the attitude one takes to unavoidable suffering. Its techniques include paradoxical intention and dereflection. Example: after losing his business, Arjun feels that nothing matters; a logotherapist helps him find meaning in supporting his daughter's education and in facing the loss with courage. Option a is right because Frankl wrote the book and founded logotherapy. Exam trap: Carl Jung wrote the look-alike title Modern Man in Search of a Soul (1933), so 'search' plus a famous name tempts; Man's Search for Meaning is Frankl's.", "topic": "Frankl and logotherapy", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "Which of the following represents the Tetrad equation as given by Spearman?", "options": {"a": "r12 × r34 = r14 × r23", "b": "r13 × r24 = r14 × r23", "c": "r14 × r23 = r13 × r24", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three equations are forms of Spearman's tetrad equation, so \"All of these\" is correct. Charles Spearman (1904, 1927) argued that one general factor, g, plus a specific factor for each test explains why mental tests all correlate positively. If that is true, every correlation between two tests is simply the product of their g loadings: r12 = g1 x g2, r34 = g3 x g4, and so on. Multiply any two correlations that use each of the four tests once and you always get the same product, g1 x g2 x g3 x g4. Hence r12 x r34 = r13 x r24 = r14 x r23. The difference between any two of these products is a tetrad difference, and Spearman's tetrad difference criterion says that when a single general factor is enough, every tetrad difference is zero (within sampling error). If the tetrads do not vanish, more than one common factor is needed, which is what Louis Thurstone (1938) later argued with his group factors. Example: if Meera's four tests (vocabulary, arithmetic, matrices, memory) give r12 x r34, r13 x r24 and r14 x r23 all near 0.24, one g factor explains the table. Option d is right because option a states one equality, option b another, and option c is option b written the other way round, and all three follow from the same one-factor rule. Exam trap: students memorise one line, usually r13 x r24 = r14 x r23, and reject the others as wrong subscripts, but swapping the sides or choosing another pair of products does not change the criterion. Key note: many Indian guides quote only r13 x r24 = r14 x r23 (option b) as the tetrad equation, but option c is the same equation reversed and option a is another of the three equalities the rule implies, so d is the only consistent answer; d stands.", "topic": "Spearman's tetrad equation", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Electrically stimulating the lateral hypothalamus will cause a rat to:", "options": {"a": "Start eating", "b": "Stop eating", "c": "Start drinking", "d": "Stop drinking"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: electrical stimulation of the lateral hypothalamus makes a rat start eating. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, regulates hunger, thirst, temperature and sex, and two of its areas became famous in the study of hunger. In 1940 Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson showed that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) made rats overeat and grow obese, so the VMH was called the satiety centre. In 1951 Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (Anand was an Indian physiologist, later of AIIMS, New Delhi) showed that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) caused aphagia (refusal to eat) and adipsia (refusal to drink); such rats could starve beside a full food dish. Stimulation gives the mirror image of a lesion: current delivered to the LH makes even a sated rat start eating, while VMH stimulation stops a hungry rat's meal. Eliot Stellar (1954) joined these findings in the dual-centre theory: the LH is the feeding (\"start\") centre and the VMH the satiety (\"stop\") centre. Later work showed the real picture is more complex: the arcuate nucleus, leptin and insulin also regulate intake, and LH lesions damage fibres passing through. Example: if Kabir's well-fed lab rat receives current through an electrode in its LH, it walks to the food tray and eats again. Option a is right because the LH is the classic feeding centre, and stimulating a centre switches its function on. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair, so \"stop eating\", the VMH stimulation result, tempts; and because LH lesions also abolish drinking, \"start drinking\" looks plausible, but the tested result of LH stimulation is eating.", "topic": "Hypothalamic feeding centre", "fig": "Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck"}, {"q": "According to Gibson, the relationship between movement and flow is:", "options": {"a": "Movement creates flow, which then provides information to guiding further movement.", "b": "Flow and self-produced movement are related, but both are independent of guiding further movement.", "c": "Flow and movement are not related.", "d": "Movement creates flow, but this information does not affect further movement."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: movement creates optic flow, and that flow in turn provides information that guides further movement. James J. Gibson (The Perception of the Visual World, 1950; The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception, 1979) argued that perception is direct: the light reaching a moving observer already carries enough information to specify the world, with no need for mental inference. When you move forward, the whole optic array streams outwards from the point you are heading towards; that point, from which the texture expands, is the focus of expansion. Moving backwards makes the flow contract towards a point. The flow pattern tells you your heading, your speed and how soon you will reach an object (how fast its image expands). Gibson stressed that perception and action form one continuous loop: we move in order to perceive, and we perceive in order to move. He developed the idea while working on pilot training during the Second World War, where a safe landing depends on keeping the focus of expansion on the runway. Example: as Arjun rides his scooter towards a junction, the road texture flows outwards from the junction; when a lorry ahead suddenly fills more of his view, the rapid expansion warns him to brake, and braking changes the flow again. Option a is right because it states both halves of Gibson's loop: self-movement produces flow, and flow then steers the next movement. Exam trap: options b and d admit a link between movement and flow but cut the feedback to further movement, which removes the whole point of Gibson's ecological, perception and action view; that passive picture belongs closer to inference-based theories such as Richard Gregory's constructivism (Eye and Brain, 1966).", "topic": "Gibson's optic flow", "fig": "James J. Gibson"}, {"q": "Damage to sensory area 1 of the cerebral cortex results in:", "options": {"a": "Loss of perception of pain", "b": "Loss of tactile and two-point discrimination", "c": "Loss of perception of touch", "d": "Loss of only tactile discrimination"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: damage to the primary somatosensory cortex (sensory area 1) causes loss of tactile and two-point discrimination. Primary somatosensory cortex, S1, lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, in Brodmann areas 3, 1 and 2. It receives touch, pressure, vibration and limb-position signals relayed through the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus. Wilder Penfield, with Edwin Boldrey (1937) and later Theodore Rasmussen (1950), stimulated this strip in awake surgical patients and mapped the sensory homunculus: the hands, lips and tongue have the largest areas because they make the finest discriminations. S1 is needed for the discriminative, analytic side of touch: two-point discrimination (telling two pinpricks from one), localising exactly where you were touched, judging texture, recognising an object by feel (stereognosis), reading numbers traced on the skin (graphaesthesia) and sensing limb position. Crude awareness that something has touched you, and much of pain and temperature sense, survive a cortical lesion, because these are registered at thalamic level. Example: after a small stroke in her left postcentral gyrus, Riya still feels a pinprick and a hot cup in her right hand, but she cannot tell a coin from a key in her pocket by touch, and two points 1 centimetre apart on her fingertip feel like one. Option b is right because it names the hallmark loss after S1 damage, fine discriminative touch including two-point discrimination. Exam trap: \"loss of perception of pain\" and \"loss of touch\" sound like what a sensory area ought to lose, but pain and crude touch largely survive cortical damage; and \"only tactile discrimination\" is half right, yet \"only\" leaves out two-point discrimination and the other discriminative losses.", "topic": "Primary somatosensory cortex lesions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT a level in Cattell's dynamic lattice?", "options": {"a": "Sem level", "b": "Temperament level", "c": "Erg level", "d": "Attitude level"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: temperament is not a level in Raymond Cattell's dynamic lattice. Raymond Cattell (1950, 1957) sorted source traits into three kinds by modality: ability traits (how well we act), temperament traits (the style or tempo of action) and dynamic traits (why we act, our motivation). The dynamic lattice is his map of the dynamic traits alone, and it has three levels. At the base are ergs, innate drives shared with other species, such as hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion. Above them are sentiments, which Cattell also called sems: learned, stable clusters of attitudes centred on an important object such as career, home, religion or the self (the self-sentiment integrates all the others). At the top are attitudes, specific interests in a course of action, such as \"I want to revise tonight\". The levels are linked by subsidiation: each attitude serves one or more sentiments, and each sentiment ultimately serves ergs. Example: Kabir's attitude \"I want to buy the new statistics textbook\" is subsidiary to his career sentiment, which in turn serves the ergs of self-assertion and security. Option b is right because temperament belongs to Cattell's classification of source traits, beside ability and dynamic traits, and is never one of the rungs of the lattice. Exam trap: the options mix the lattice's own jargon (erg, sem) with a genuine Cattell term (temperament), so the familiar word looks safe; remember that the lattice maps motivation, while temperament describes style, a separate class of trait.", "topic": "Cattell's dynamic lattice", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Which of the following view of intelligence held that intelligence was one of the innate faculties along with learning, thinking and remembering etc?", "options": {"a": "Monarchic view", "b": "Oligarchic view", "c": "Gestalt view", "d": "Behaviouristic view"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the oligarchic view held that intelligence is one of several innate faculties, along with learning, thinking and remembering. Charles Spearman (The Abilities of Man, 1927) opened his account of ability by sorting the older doctrines under three political metaphors. The monarchic doctrine treated intelligence as one supreme general power ruling all mental activity, the \"intelligence\" of everyday speech. The oligarchic doctrine came from the faculty psychology of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries: the mind was governed by a small group of separate, inborn faculties, such as memory, attention, imagination, judgement, reasoning and intelligence, each a power in its own right, ruling together like an oligarchy. The anarchic doctrine, linked with Edward Thorndike (1927), said ability is made of countless independent, specific capacities with no general ruler. Spearman rejected all three and offered his own two-factor theory of g and s. Example: if Meera's teacher says \"she has a good faculty of memory, a weak faculty of reasoning and an average faculty of intelligence\", the teacher is speaking in the oligarchic, faculty manner. Option b is right because the stem describes intelligence as one innate faculty sitting beside others (learning, thinking, remembering), which is exactly the faculty doctrine Spearman called oligarchic. Exam trap: \"monarchic\" tempts because it is the only other option that sounds like a theory of intelligence, but monarchic means a single ruling power, whereas the stem lists several faculties side by side. Key note: some Indian guides use \"oligarchic\" for Louis Thurstone's group factors (primary mental abilities, 1938) and file faculty psychology under the monarchic view; Spearman's own scheme places the faculties under oligarchic, so b stands.", "topic": "Spearman's doctrines of ability", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Two factor theory was put forward by:", "options": {"a": "Thurston", "b": "Jensen", "c": "Guilford", "d": "Spearman"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the two-factor theory of intelligence was put forward by Charles Spearman. In 1904 Charles Spearman noticed that schoolchildren's marks in subjects as different as classics, mathematics and pitch discrimination all correlated positively with one another, a pattern now called the positive manifold. To explain it he developed factor analysis and proposed that every test score reflects two kinds of factor: g, a general factor common to all intellectual tasks (he likened it to mental energy), and s, a specific factor peculiar to each test. Tests that correlate highly with others are heavily saturated with g. He set the theory out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927), and his student John C. Raven built the Progressive Matrices (1938) as a near-pure measure of g. Example: if Arjun scores well in arithmetic, vocabulary and block design, Spearman would credit a high g, while his special flair for arithmetic reflects an s for number. Option d is right because g and s are the two factors of Spearman's theory. Exam trap: several other \"two-factor theories\" exist, so the label alone does not tell you the field: Frederick Herzberg's two-factor theory of job satisfaction (1959), O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory of avoidance learning (1947) and Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's two-factor theory of emotion (1962). For intelligence, the answer is always Spearman.", "topic": "Spearman's two-factor theory", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Patients with damage to the frontal lobes behave as if they lack control systems that monitor their processing resources appropriately. They are said to suffer from:", "options": {"a": "Gerstmann Syndrome", "b": "Dysexecutive Syndrome", "c": "Korsakoff's Syndrome", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: frontal-lobe patients who lack a system to monitor and control their processing resources show the dysexecutive syndrome. In the working memory model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), the central executive is the attentional controller that plans, focuses, switches and inhibits while storing nothing itself. Alan Baddeley and Barbara Wilson (1988) coined the term dysexecutive syndrome for the pattern seen when this control system fails after frontal, especially dorsolateral prefrontal, damage. Donald Norman and Tim Shallice (1986) described a similar controller, the supervisory attentional system, which takes over when routine habits are not enough. Such patients may have normal IQ and memory scores yet cannot plan, sequence steps, shift set, stop an unwanted response or notice their own errors; they perseverate, repeating a strategy that has stopped working, as on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. The Behavioural Assessment of the Dysexecutive Syndrome (BADS; Barbara Wilson and colleagues, 1996) tests these problems with everyday-like tasks. Example: after a road accident injured his frontal lobes, Kabir can recite a recipe perfectly, but when he cooks he skips steps, keeps stirring after the dish has burnt and does not notice his mistakes. Option b is right because the stem describes a failure of executive control and self-monitoring, the defining feature of the dysexecutive syndrome. Exam trap: Gerstmann and Korsakoff syndromes are also named neurological syndromes and look like fair alternatives, but one is a parietal syndrome and the other an amnesic one; the words \"control systems\" and \"monitor\" point to the central executive.", "topic": "Dysexecutive syndrome", "fig": "Alan Baddeley and Barbara Wilson"}, {"q": "The scaling method which is more likely to yield multidimensional attitude scale:", "options": {"a": "Guttman Scalogram analysis", "b": "Paired comparison method", "c": "Equal appearing intervals method", "d": "Method of summated rating"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: the method of summated ratings, the Likert scale, is the scaling method most likely to yield a multidimensional attitude scale. Rensis Likert (1932) asked respondents to rate each attitude statement on five ordered points, from strongly agree to strongly disagree, and simply added the item scores. Items are kept by item analysis: an item survives if it correlates with the total score or separates high from low scorers. Nothing in this procedure tests whether all items measure one dimension, so items tapping related but different aspects of the attitude object can all correlate with the total and stay in. That is why researchers run factor analysis on Likert scales and often report subscales. The other three methods are built to place items on a single continuum. Louis Thurstone's paired comparisons (1927) and his equal appearing intervals (Louis Thurstone and Ernest Chave, 1929) have judges give each statement a scale value on one favourable to unfavourable continuum. Louis Guttman's scalogram analysis (1944) is cumulative: anyone who endorses a strong item should also endorse all milder ones, and the scale is accepted only if the coefficient of reproducibility reaches .90, a direct test of unidimensionality. Example: Riya's 20-item Likert questionnaire on attitudes to online classes later splits into three factors: convenience, learning quality and social contact. Option d is right because summing items chosen by item-total correlation does not force a single dimension, unlike the Thurstone and Guttman methods. Exam trap: students link \"multidimensional\" with the most technical-sounding method, scalogram analysis, but Guttman's method is the strictest test of unidimensionality of all.", "topic": "Attitude scaling and dimensionality", "fig": "Rensis Likert"}, {"q": "The observed tendency of humans to quickly return to a relatively stable level of happiness despite major positive or negative events or life changes is termed as:", "options": {"a": "Pratfall effect", "b": "Positivity effect", "c": "Endowment effect", "d": "Hedonic treadmill effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the tendency to return quickly to a stable level of happiness after major good or bad events is the hedonic treadmill effect. Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971) built on Harry Helson's adaptation-level theory (1964) and argued that people adapt to new circumstances: gains in income or comfort raise expectations and leave happiness where it was, like walking on a treadmill that never takes you anywhere. Philip Brickman, Dan Coates and Ronnie Janoff-Bulman (1978) gave the classic evidence: lottery winners were not much happier than controls and took less pleasure in ordinary events, while people paralysed in accidents were less unhappy than expected. Today the process is called hedonic adaptation. Later research shows the adaptation is incomplete for some events, such as long unemployment, the death of a spouse or severe disability, so a person's baseline can shift. Example: when Arjun gets a large salary rise he is delighted for a few months, but within a year his new flat and car feel normal and his everyday mood is back where it was. Option d is right because the stem describes exactly this return to a baseline after both positive and negative life changes. Exam trap: all the distractors are named \"effects\" from social psychology and behavioural economics, and \"positivity effect\" sounds as if it should be about happiness, but none of them describes adapting back to a baseline.", "topic": "Hedonic treadmill", "fig": "Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell"}, {"q": "Autokinetic movement does not occur if there is a fixed ------.", "options": {"a": "Vision", "b": "Frame of reference", "c": "Distance between the stimulus and the eye", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: autokinetic movement does not occur when there is a fixed frame of reference. The autokinetic effect is an illusion of motion: a small, stationary point of light in a completely dark room appears to drift, wander or jerk about after a few seconds of staring. It happens because the darkness removes every visible landmark against which the light's position could be judged. The eyes make small involuntary drifts as they hold fixation, and without a frame of reference the brain cannot tell whether the image moved because the eyes moved or because the light moved, so it reads the change as movement of the light. The moment the room is lit, or a visible outline or second object appears, the background anchors the light and the illusion disappears. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) used this ambiguity to study how social norms form: people judging alone settled on personal ranges of movement, but in groups their estimates converged on a shared norm, which they kept when later tested alone. Example: Meera stares at a glowing dot on a dark wall in the laboratory and is sure it moved about 15 centimetres; when the technician switches on a dim lamp that shows the edges of the wall, the dot stays perfectly still. Option b is right because the illusion depends on the absence of a reference frame, so a fixed frame abolishes it. Exam trap: \"distance between the stimulus and the eye\" tempts because the light seems to move about in space, but fixing the viewing distance does not remove the illusion; only visible surroundings do.", "topic": "Autokinetic effect", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following disturbs initiation of sleep most?", "options": {"a": "Melatonin", "b": "Benzodiazepines", "c": "Amphetamines", "d": "Orexin"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this paper marks orexin. Orexin, also called hypocretin, is a neuropeptide made by a small group of neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, discovered in 1998 by two teams, one including Luis de Lecea and one led by Masashi Yanagisawa. Orexin neurons fire during waking and fall silent in sleep. They excite the brain's arousal systems (noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus, serotonin, dopamine and acetylcholine) and hold the brain in the waking state. In Clifford Saper's flip-flop switch model (2001), orexin sits on the wake side of the switch between the sleep-promoting ventrolateral preoptic area and the arousal centres, and while orexin is active the switch does not flip into sleep. So orexin is the brain's own signal that opposes sleep onset. The evidence runs both ways: loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy, with sudden sleep attacks and cataplexy, and drugs that block orexin receptors (suvorexant, lemborexant, daridorexant) are approved for insomnia because they make it easier to fall asleep and stay asleep. The other options: melatonin, from the pineal gland at night, eases sleep onset; benzodiazepines enhance GABA and shorten sleep latency; amphetamines release dopamine and noradrenaline and also delay sleep onset, which makes them the strong runner-up. Example: Kabir lies in bed at midnight, still alert after a stressful day; orexin activity driving his arousal systems is part of what keeps him awake, and it is exactly what an orexin-blocking hypnotic would switch off. Option d is right on the key's reading because orexin is the brain's central wake-promoting signal, the one the newest insomnia drugs target. Exam trap: amphetamines look like the obvious answer, since a stimulant taken in the evening clearly delays sleep. Key note: this mock used to mark c, amphetamines, because a stimulant drug is the plainest cause of trouble falling asleep. LBS's official key for this paper marks d, orexin, the brain's own wake signal. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Orexin and sleep onset", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Anu is below normal intelligence but she is able to tell you the exact day of the week of December 16, 1952. She has ------ syndrome.", "options": {"a": "Genius", "b": "Giftedness", "c": "Savant", "d": "Calendar assessment"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: Anu has savant syndrome. Savant syndrome is a rare condition in which a person with a developmental disability, most often autism or intellectual disability, shows an island of outstanding skill that contrasts sharply with their overall limitations. J. Langdon Down (1887), who also described the syndrome now named after him, called such people \"idiot savants\"; the psychiatrist Darold Treffert (Extraordinary People, 1989) popularised the term savant syndrome. Savant skills cluster in a few areas: calendar calculating, music (often piano played by ear), art, lightning arithmetic and mechanical or spatial skill, usually backed by an exceptional memory. Calendar calculating, naming the weekday of any date, is the commonest. Oliver Sacks (1985) described the twins he called John and Michael, who could give the weekday of dates thousands of years away yet could not do simple sums. Researchers think savants draw on vast memorised regularities of the calendar rather than on a formula. Anu, whose general intelligence is below normal, names the weekday of 16 December 1952 at once; that pairing of low general ability with one striking skill is the savant pattern. Option c is right because savant syndrome is the only option that combines below-normal intelligence with an isolated exceptional ability. Exam trap: \"calendar assessment\" borrows the wording of Anu's skill to sound like a real diagnosis, but no such syndrome exists; the skill is called calendar calculating, and it is one form of savant syndrome.", "topic": "Savant syndrome", "fig": ""}, {"q": "How many 'three-way interactions' can be studied in a 4×3×3 factorial design?", "options": {"a": "1 only", "b": "4 only", "c": "12 only", "d": "3 only"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a 4 x 3 x 3 factorial design has only one three-way interaction. In a factorial design each number stands for one independent variable (factor), and its value is the number of levels of that factor. So 4 x 3 x 3 means three factors, say A with 4 levels, B with 3 and C with 3, crossed to give 4 x 3 x 3 = 36 cells. The number of effects tested depends on how many factors there are, not on how many levels each has. With three factors, a factorial analysis of variance tests three main effects (A, B, C), three two-way interactions (A x B, A x C, B x C) and one three-way interaction (A x B x C). A three-way interaction means that the two-way interaction between two factors itself changes across the levels of the third. In general, with k factors the number of interactions involving r factors is the number of ways of choosing r factors from k, and there is only one way to choose all three of three. Example: Riya studies recall with four study methods, three noise levels and three times of day; if the method by noise pattern looks different in the morning from the evening, that is the single A x B x C interaction. Option a is right because three factors can be combined all together in only one way, whatever their numbers of levels. Exam trap: examiners count levels instead of factors; 4 echoes the first factor's levels, 12 is 4 x 3, and 3 is the number of two-way interactions, none of which is the number of three-way interactions.", "topic": "Interactions in factorial designs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following methods are employed to study Memory Processes? 1. Free recall and Recognition 2. Observation and Recognition 3. Conditioning and Neuroimaging 4. Concurrent memory task paradigm and Neuroimaging", "options": {"a": "1 and 3 only", "b": "2 and 3 only", "c": "3 and 4 only", "d": "1 and 4 only"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition (statement 1), and the concurrent memory task paradigm with neuroimaging (statement 4), are methods used to study memory processes. Experimental memory research began with Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), who learned nonsense syllables and measured retention by relearning (the savings method). Today retention is measured mainly in three ways: in free recall the person reproduces studied items in any order, in cued recall a hint is given, and in recognition the person picks studied items out from new ones. Recognition is usually easier than recall, so comparing the two shows how much is stored and how much can be retrieved. To study the structure of working memory, Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) used the concurrent task (dual-task) paradigm: if holding digits in mind barely disturbs reasoning, the two tasks must draw on partly separate systems. Combined with neuroimaging, such as functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and positron emission tomography (PET), such tasks show which regions, such as the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, work during encoding and retrieval. Example: Meera reads 20 words in the scanner, recalls as many as she can, and later picks them out from 40 words, while her brain activity is recorded. Option d is right because statements 1 and 4 each pair two genuine memory methods. Exam trap: recognition also appears in statement 2, which makes 2 look safe, but observation is a general data-collection method, not a memory paradigm. Key note: conditioning (for example eyeblink conditioning in amnesic patients) is used to study implicit memory, so statement 3 is arguable, but statements 1 and 4 are the two clearly correct methods and d is the only option containing both; d stands.", "topic": "Methods of studying memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which structure in the core of the brain is involved in the expression of stronger emotion such as fear and rage?", "options": {"a": "Thalamus", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Limbic system", "d": "Cortex"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: LBS's official key for this paper marks the limbic system. The limbic system is a set of linked structures deep in the centre of the brain, around the brainstem and under the cortex: the amygdala, hippocampus, cingulate gyrus, septal area and, in most accounts, the hypothalamus. James Papez (1937) proposed a circuit of these structures as the basis of emotion, and Paul MacLean (1952) named it the limbic system. Its role in fear and rage comes from lesion and stimulation work. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937) removed the temporal lobes, amygdala included, in rhesus monkeys, and the monkeys lost their fear: they became tame and placid and approached snakes and people they had avoided (Kluver-Bucy syndrome). Stimulating the amygdala, or the hypothalamus it controls, produces fear, defence and attack. Philip Bard (1928) found that cats without a cortex still showed sham rage as long as the hypothalamus was intact, and Walter Hess (Nobel Prize, 1949) evoked rage by stimulating the hypothalamus. Joseph LeDoux later traced a fast fear pathway from the thalamus straight to the amygdala, the low road that sets off a fear response before the cortex has identified the danger. Example: when a dog suddenly lunges at Arjun, his amygdala flags the threat and, through the hypothalamus, triggers the sympathetic fight or flight pattern before he has even thought about it. Option c is right on the key's reading because the limbic system, with the amygdala and hypothalamus inside it, is the brain's emotional core for fear and rage. Exam trap: the hypothalamus is itself part of the limbic system and organises the bodily expression of rage, so b is the near miss. Key note: this mock used to mark b, the hypothalamus, because Atkinson and Hilgard's three-layer model puts it in the central core while the limbic system forms a layer around the core. LBS's official key for this paper marks c, the limbic system. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Limbic system and emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is the best example of hindsight bias?", "options": {"a": "Changing one's attitude about a person base on how others view that person.", "b": "Misremembering one's previous views on a person in order to fit one's current knowledge or beliefs.", "c": "Changing one's choice of a prize, thinking that new information would be helpful", "d": "Paying attention only to information that is consistent with one's beliefs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: misremembering one's previous views to fit what one now knows is the best example of hindsight bias. Hindsight bias, the \"I knew it all along\" effect, is the tendency, once an outcome is known, to believe we would have predicted it and to recall our earlier judgements as closer to the outcome than they really were. Baruch Fischhoff (1975) showed it experimentally: people told the outcome of a historical conflict rated that outcome as far more likely than people not told, and they denied that the information had influenced them. Baruch Fischhoff and Ruth Beyth (1975) asked students, before President Richard Nixon's 1972 visits to China and the Soviet Union, how likely various outcomes were; afterwards the students recalled their own estimates as closer to what had actually happened. The bias works through memory reconstruction: the new knowledge blends into the old memory, so the original view can no longer be retrieved accurately. It makes past events seem more predictable than they were and makes research findings look obvious. Example: before the match Kabir said India had only a slim chance; after India won, he tells his friends he always knew they would win, and he honestly remembers having said so. Option b is right because it describes the core mechanism, rewriting a past opinion so that it fits present knowledge. Exam trap: option d also describes a belief-driven bias, but attending only to belief-consistent information is confirmation bias, which filters evidence as it arrives, whereas hindsight bias distorts the memory of what one thought before. Key note: LBS's official key for this paper marks a, changing one's attitude to match how others see a person. That key is wrong: a describes conformity, while hindsight bias is rewriting one's memory of an earlier judgement to fit what one now knows (Baruch Fischhoff, 1975), so b stays.", "topic": "Hindsight bias", "fig": "Baruch Fischhoff"}, {"q": "Those with more complex psychopathologies are likely to be excluded from treatment outcome studies and so denied access to the treatment programme associated with the study. This is referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Simple diagnosis criteria", "b": "A no-treatment control condition", "c": "Narrow inclusion criteria", "d": "Complex exclusion criteria"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: excluding people with complex psychopathologies from treatment outcome studies is referred to as narrow inclusion criteria. A treatment outcome study, usually a randomised controlled trial, tests whether a therapy works better than a comparison condition. To get a clean answer, researchers set strict inclusion criteria (who may enter: for example, adults aged 18 to 65 with a primary diagnosis of major depressive disorder) and exclusion criteria (who may not: people with comorbid substance use, psychosis, personality disorder, active suicide risk or serious medical illness). Narrow criteria make the sample homogeneous, reduce error variance and raise internal validity, so any difference can be credited to the treatment. The cost is twofold. First, people with complex, comorbid problems are denied the treatment the study offers. Second, the results generalise poorly to routine clinics, where comorbidity is the rule; this is the gap between efficacy (does the treatment work under ideal, controlled conditions?) and effectiveness (does it work in everyday practice?). Example: Meera, who has depression together with panic disorder and alcohol misuse, is screened out of a trial of cognitive behaviour therapy for depression; the trial shows the therapy works, but its patients were far simpler cases than Meera. Option c is right because the stem describes the consequence of narrowly drawn criteria for entering a study. Exam trap: \"complex exclusion criteria\" echoes the stem's word \"complex\" and sounds technical, but the recognised phrase is narrow (strict) inclusion criteria; exclusion criteria are simply the listed reasons for leaving someone out, and \"complex\" is not a standard label for them.", "topic": "Inclusion criteria in outcome research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Free recall and recognition, b. Sentence verification task, c. Priming, d. Scanning task. 1. Semantic memory, 2. Sensory memory, 3. Episodic memory, 4. Implicit memory", "options": {"a": "a-4, b-2, c-1, d-3", "b": "a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4", "c": "a-1, b-4, c-2, d-3", "d": "a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition test episodic memory (3), sentence verification tests semantic memory (1), priming tests implicit memory (4) and the scanning task, by elimination, sensory memory (2). Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory into episodic memory, personal events tied to a time and place, and semantic memory, general knowledge of facts and concepts. Free recall (write down every word from the list you just heard) and recognition (which of these words were on the list?) ask about one specific study episode, so they are episodic tasks. In a sentence verification task the person judges sentences such as 'A canary is a bird' as true or false as fast as possible; Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) used the reaction times to map the network of stored facts, so the task taps semantic memory. Priming is an influence of earlier exposure without conscious recollection: having seen BUTTER, a person completes BUT___ as BUTTER more often, even without remembering the word; Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) called this implicit memory. Scanning a briefly flashed display, as in George Sperling's (1960) partial report, taps the brief iconic store of sensory memory. Example: Riya recognises yesterday's list words (episodic), confirms that a sparrow is a bird (semantic) and fills BUT___ with BUTTER without knowing why (implicit). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: recall and recognition feel like knowledge tests, so students send them to semantic memory; they test memory for an episode. Key note: Saul Sternberg's (1966) memory scanning task is really a short-term memory task, while scanning a briefly flashed display, as in Sperling's partial report, taps sensory (iconic) memory; with the other three pairs fixed, d stands.", "topic": "Memory tasks and memory systems", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Saying that language is lateralized in the brain means that:", "options": {"a": "the cerebellum is the most prominent brain structure involved in language comprehension.", "b": "both hemispheres of the brain contribute equally to language function.", "c": "language function is primarily associated with one hemisphere of the brain.", "d": "only the right temporal lobe is responsible for language processing."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: saying language is lateralised means that language function is primarily associated with one hemisphere of the brain. Lateralisation, or hemispheric specialisation, means that the two cerebral hemispheres look alike but do not do the same jobs. For language the left hemisphere is dominant in about 95 percent of right-handers and in most left-handers (roughly 70 percent). The first evidence came from patients: Paul Broca (1861) described \"Tan\", who lost fluent speech after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe, and Carl Wernicke (1874) described loss of comprehension after damage to the left posterior temporal lobe. The Wada test of Juhn Wada (1949) injects sodium amytal into one carotid artery to anaesthetise one hemisphere; if that side holds language, the patient falls silent. Split-brain studies by Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (from 1962) showed that patients could name an object seen in the right visual field, which goes to the left hemisphere, but not one in the left field. Lateralisation is relative, not total: the right hemisphere handles prosody (the melody and emotional tone of speech), jokes, metaphor and some word comprehension. Example: after a left-hemisphere stroke Riya struggles to find words, while her neighbour, after a similar right-hemisphere stroke, speaks fluently but in a flat, toneless voice. Option c is right because lateralised means located mainly on one side, and \"primarily\" leaves room for the right hemisphere's smaller role. Exam trap: option d also puts language on one side, but on the wrong side and in absolute terms (\"only the right temporal lobe\"); language lateralisation almost always means the left hemisphere, and never \"only\".", "topic": "Lateralisation of language", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following are not Factor Analytically derived personality inventories? 1. 16 PF 2. Comrey Personality Scales 3. Jenkins Activity Survey 4. Edwards Personal Preference Schedule", "options": {"a": "1 and 2 only", "b": "3 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 3 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey (3) and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (4) are not factor-analytically derived. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content strategy writes items whose content obviously fits the trait; the theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory; the empirical criterion-group strategy keeps whatever items separate a criterion group from controls; and the factor-analytic strategy correlates many items, keeps those that load on each factor and names the factors afterwards. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949) came from factor analyses of trait terms, and Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970; 180 items, eight factors such as trust and orderliness) were built the same way, so 1 and 2 are factor-analytic. The Jenkins Activity Survey of C. David Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Ray Rosenman measures the Type A behaviour pattern; its Type A scale items were chosen because they agreed with Structured Interview classifications, a criterion approach. Allen Edwards's Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (revised edition, 1959) is theory-based: it measures 15 needs from Henry Murray's (1938) list, using forced-choice pairs matched for social desirability. Example: Arjun gives 300 new items to 1,000 students and keeps only the clusters that emerge from factor analysis; that is Cattell's and Comrey's route, not Edwards's. Option b is right because 3 and 4 are the only two not built by factor analysis. Exam trap: the EPPS controls social desirability so carefully that it looks statistical, but its items were written for Murray's need theory. Key note: the Jenkins Activity Survey also reports subscales (speed and impatience, job involvement, hard-driving competitiveness) found by factor analysis, but its main Type A scale was built to match interview classifications, so it is not classed as a factor-analytic inventory; b stands.", "topic": "Inventory construction strategies", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The antidiuretic hormone which mainly targets kidneys and arterioles, stimulates water retention; raises blood pressure by contracting arterioles:", "options": {"a": "Oxytocin", "b": "Thyroxine", "c": "Vasopressin", "d": "Progesterone"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the antidiuretic hormone that makes the kidneys retain water and raises blood pressure by contracting arterioles is vasopressin. Vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone (ADH), is a small peptide made by neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. Their axons run down the pituitary stalk to the posterior pituitary, which releases the hormone into the blood. Its two names record its two actions. It is antidiuretic because it makes the collecting ducts of the kidneys reabsorb water, so less but more concentrated urine is passed. It is a vasopressor because, at higher levels, it contracts the smooth muscle of arterioles and raises blood pressure. Release rises when osmoreceptors detect salty, concentrated blood or when blood volume falls, as in dehydration or bleeding. Lack of vasopressin causes diabetes insipidus, with large volumes of dilute urine and intense thirst; alcohol suppresses its release, which is why drinking makes people pass more urine. In the brain, vasopressin also plays a part in social bonding and memory. Example: after a long cricket match in the sun without water, Arjun's vasopressin rises, his kidneys save water, and his urine becomes dark and scanty. Option c is right because vasopressin is the posterior pituitary hormone that acts on the kidneys and arterioles exactly as the stem describes. Exam trap: oxytocin is vasopressin's twin, made in the same hypothalamic nuclei, released from the same posterior pituitary and differing by only two amino acids, so it is the classic decoy.", "topic": "Vasopressin (ADH)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Read the following statements carefully and give an appropriate response: I. Only about 6 percent of the population score above 140 on IQ tests. II. An IQ score below 90 indicates mental retardation.", "options": {"a": "I is true and II is false", "b": "I and II are false", "c": "I is false and II is true", "d": "I and II are true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are false. Statement I is false. Deviation IQs on the Wechsler scales have a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, and they are roughly normally distributed. A score of 140 is 40 points above the mean, which is 40 divided by 15, about 2.67 SD. Under the normal curve only about 0.4 percent of people lie above z = 2.67, roughly 1 person in 260, not 6 percent. Even above 130 (2 SD) there are only about 2 percent; 6 percent of people score above about 123. Statement II is also false. An IQ below 90 does not indicate intellectual disability (the older term was mental retardation). On the WAIS-IV, 90 to 109 is average, 80 to 89 is low average and 70 to 79 is borderline. Intellectual disability needs an IQ of about 70 or below (two SD under the mean, allowing about 5 points for measurement error) together with deficits in adaptive behaviour that begin in the developmental period (DSM-5, 2013). Example: Kabir scores 86 on the WAIS-IV; he is in the low average band, not intellectually disabled. Option b is right because statement I overstates the proportion above 140 about fifteenfold and statement II puts the cut-off for intellectual disability 20 points too high. Exam trap: each statement sounds near enough to a remembered fact (a few percent are gifted, and the old dull normal band began near 90), so check each against the normal curve and the cut-off of 70. Key note: the original source may have said \".6 percent\" (the old Stanford-Binet, with SD 16, puts about 0.6 percent above 140), so a dropped decimal could make the setter read statement I as true and want a; as printed, 6 percent is false, matching the same statement on IMHANS 2021 Q37, so b stands.", "topic": "IQ distribution and cut-offs", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"imhans-2019": [{"q": "------ is the process in which detecting, translating and transmitting from external environment to brain.", "concept": "The answer is d: sensation is the process of detecting physical energy from the external environment, translating it into nerve signals and transmitting those signals to the brain. Sensation has three steps. First, specialised receptor cells detect a stimulus: light on the rods and cones of the retina, sound waves in the cochlea, chemicals on the tongue and in the nose, pressure and temperature on the skin. Second, the receptors translate that physical energy into electrical nerve impulses, a step called transduction. Third, sensory nerves transmit the impulses to the brain, where most senses are relayed through the thalamus to their own areas of cortex. Perception is the next stage: the brain organises and interprets this input into meaningful objects and events. The scientific study of how physical energy relates to experience is psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860). Example: when Meera steps into a noisy street, sound waves bend the hair cells in her cochlea, which fire signals along the auditory nerve (sensation); recognising that one of the sounds is her brother calling her name is perception. Option d is right because detecting, translating (transducing) and transmitting raw stimulus energy to the brain is the textbook definition of sensation. Exam trap: perception (c) is the near miss, because the two words are so often used together; sensation ends when the signal reaches the brain, while perception begins there, with organising and interpreting that signal.", "traps": {"a": "Adaptation here means sensory adaptation, the drop in receptor response to an unchanging stimulus (you stop noticing a ticking clock); it is one property of sensation, not the whole process of detecting and transmitting.", "b": "Selective attention is filtering, choosing which inputs get further processing (Donald Broadbent's filter model, 1958); it acts on input that has already arrived rather than describing how it is detected and transmitted.", "c": "Perception is the organising and interpreting of sensory input into meaningful objects and events; it starts after the signals reach the brain, so it does not describe detecting and transmitting them."}}, {"q": "Destruction of the ------ part of the brain leads to anterograde amnesia.", "concept": "The answer is b: destruction of the hippocampus leads to anterograde amnesia, the inability to form new long-term memories after the damage. The hippocampus is a curved structure in the medial temporal lobe of each hemisphere. It does not store memories permanently; it consolidates them, binding new experiences and facts into lasting memories that are later held in the cortex. The classic evidence is patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Scoville removed much of the hippocampus, together with the nearby amygdala and surrounding cortex, from both sides of his brain to control severe epilepsy. The seizures eased, but, as William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported, H.M. could no longer remember new people, events or facts for more than a few minutes. His older memories, his intelligence and his short-term memory were largely intact, and he could still learn new motor skills such as mirror drawing without remembering the practice sessions. This showed that declarative (explicit) memory depends on the hippocampus, while procedural memory uses other systems. Retrograde amnesia, by contrast, is loss of memories formed before the damage. Example: after viral encephalitis damages both of Arjun's hippocampi, he can describe his childhood in detail but cannot recall the nurse he met an hour ago. Option b is right because bilateral hippocampal damage is the textbook cause of anterograde amnesia. Exam trap: the amygdala (c) sits right next to the hippocampus and was removed in H.M. too, but it handles fear and the emotional colouring of memory, not the formation of new declarative memories.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebrum is the whole of both cerebral hemispheres; it is far too broad, since damage to most of it does not cause amnesia, and the memory-forming structure is one small part of it.", "c": "The amygdala, beside the hippocampus in the medial temporal lobe, processes fear and emotional memory; damage to it alone blunts fear conditioning but does not stop new declarative learning.", "d": "The pons, in the brainstem, relays signals between the cerebrum and cerebellum and helps control breathing, sleep and REM; damage there affects movement and consciousness, not new memory formation."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals?", "concept": "The answer is a: depersonalisation disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals. Forensic surveys ask which mental disorders are over-represented among prisoners and offenders. The best known is the systematic review by Seena Fazel and John Danesh (2002) in The Lancet: about 47% of male prisoners had antisocial personality disorder, making it the most common diagnosis in prison samples, and about 3.7% had a psychotic illness, several times the rate in the general community. Antisocial personality disorder fits offending almost by definition, since its criteria include repeated unlawful acts, deceit, impulsivity, aggression and lack of remorse. Narcissistic personality disorder, with its grandiosity, entitlement and low empathy, is also over-represented in offender groups. Schizophrenia, especially when combined with substance misuse, raises the risk of violence and is common in prisons and forensic psychiatric units. Depersonalisation (now depersonalisation/derealisation) disorder is different: the person feels detached from his or her own self or body, as if watching from outside, or feels that the world is unreal, while reality testing stays intact. It is distressing, but it does not drive antisocial behaviour, and it is not a forensic staple. Example: Kabir, a college student, feels as if he is living in a dream and his hands do not belong to him; he seeks help for his distress, not because of any crime. Option a is right because depersonalisation disorder is the only option not linked with offending or common in prison populations. Exam trap: schizophrenia (c) tempts as the 'innocent' choice because most people with schizophrenia are never violent, yet psychotic illness is still over-represented among prisoners, while depersonalisation disorder is not. Key note: LBS's official key for this paper marks c, schizophrenia. That key is wrong: psychotic illness is several times more common in prisoners than in the general population (Seena Fazel and John Danesh, 2002), while depersonalisation disorder has no link with offending, so a stays.", "traps": {"b": "Narcissistic personality disorder (Cluster B, with grandiosity, entitlement and low empathy) is over-represented among offenders, so it is commonly diagnosed in forensic samples.", "c": "Schizophrenia and other psychotic illnesses occur several times more often in prisoners than in the community (about 3.7% of male prisoners in Seena Fazel and John Danesh's review), so it is commonly diagnosed in criminals.", "d": "Antisocial personality disorder is the commonest diagnosis in prison samples (about 47% of male prisoners), because its criteria include repeated law-breaking, deceit and aggression."}}, {"q": "An IQ test does not provide:", "concept": "The answer is d: an IQ test does not provide a good prediction of everyday behaviour. Modern intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales (David Wechsler, 1939 onwards) and the Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) are among the best built tools in psychology. Their full-scale IQs show high test-retest reliability, usually .90 or more, so scores stay stable over weeks and years. They show high internal consistency, because their items and subtests cohere in measuring general ability. They also have good validity for what they were built to predict: school grades and educational attainment, and to a fair degree job performance. What they do poorly is predict how a person will actually behave in daily life: social conduct, emotional adjustment, everyday adaptive behaviour, or how someone copes in a crisis. Behaviour depends on motivation, personality, emotional and social skills, culture and the situation, none of which an IQ score captures. That is why a diagnosis of intellectual disability also needs a separate measure of adaptive behaviour, such as the Vineland scales. Example: Arjun scores 125 on the WAIS, yet he struggles to keep friends and cannot manage his money; his IQ did not forecast these everyday difficulties. Option d is right because reliability, internal consistency and validity are strengths of IQ tests, while broad prediction of behaviour is their known limit. Exam trap: 'good validity' tempts because IQ tests are often criticised, but the criticism is about cultural bias and narrowness, not poor validity for academic outcomes.", "traps": {"a": "IQ tests do have good validity, especially criterion validity for school achievement and educational attainment, so validity is something they provide.", "b": "Full-scale IQ is highly stable over time, with test-retest coefficients around .90 and above, so high test-retest reliability is a strength, not what is missing.", "c": "The items and subtests of IQ tests cohere strongly in measuring general ability, giving high internal consistency (split-half and alpha coefficients often above .90)."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements are true? I) Classical conditioning regulates reflexive, involuntary responses exclusively. II) Classical conditioning can increase sexual arousal.", "concept": "The answer is c: only statement II is true. Classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus that already triggers a response, until the neutral stimulus alone triggers a conditioned response. Statement I is false because of the word 'exclusively'. Classical conditioning mainly shapes reflexive, involuntary responses such as salivation, heart rate and fear, but not only these: it also shapes emotional reactions, food preferences and aversions, attitudes (evaluative conditioning) and drug tolerance, and in autoshaping a pigeon's skeletal key-peck is produced simply by pairing a key light with food. Classical and operant processes also overlap in everyday learning. Statement II is true: sexual arousal can be classically conditioned. Stanley Rachman (1966) showed men a picture of women's boots followed by erotic slides; after repeated pairings the boots alone produced mild sexual arousal, measured by changes in penile volume, which offered a model of how fetishes might be learned. Michael Domjan's studies of male Japanese quail in the 1980s showed that a cue signalling access to a female came to produce sexual approach and quicker mating. Example: Riya feels a rush of excitement whenever she smells the perfume her partner wears, because the scent has been paired with intimacy many times. Option c is right because statement I overstates the case with 'exclusively', while statement II is supported by conditioning experiments. Exam trap: 'reflexive and involuntary' is the standard textbook description of classical conditioning, so statement I sounds correct; the single word 'exclusively' is what makes it false.", "traps": {"a": "Statement I is the false one: classical conditioning also shapes emotions, preferences, attitudes and some skeletal responses (autoshaping), so it does not govern reflexes exclusively; and statement II, which this option rejects, is true.", "b": "Only statement II holds; statement I fails on 'exclusively', because classical conditioning reaches emotional responses, attitudes and drug tolerance as well as reflexes.", "d": "Statement II is true: Stanley Rachman's (1966) boots experiment and Michael Domjan's quail studies showed that sexual arousal can be classically conditioned, so both statements cannot be false."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "concept": "The answer is d: concurrent validity means that scores on a test correlate highly with scores on an accepted measure of the same attribute taken at about the same time. Criterion-related validity is the correlation between a test and an independent, external measure of the attribute, the criterion, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis, an established gold-standard scale, school marks or job ratings. Its two types differ only in timing. In concurrent validity, test and criterion are measured at about the same time, so the test is checked against present status. In predictive validity, the criterion is gathered later, as when entrance scores are correlated with first-year marks. The classic concurrent design gives a new test together with an established one: a new 20-item depression questionnaire that correlates 0.85 with a gold-standard scale completed the same day shows concurrent validity. The correlation is called the validity coefficient. Example: Riya builds a 10-minute reading screener and gives it to 200 children on the same day as a full, well-established reading battery; a high correlation between the two shows concurrent validity. Option d is right because it is the only option that contains the essential idea, a high correlation with an accepted measure of the same attribute. Exam trap: the word 'concurrent' tempts students to pick 'two tests are done at the same time', but timing alone is not validity. Key note: strictly, correlating with tests of the same attribute blends concurrent and convergent validity, and option b is a half-truth (the timing) with nothing about correlation; d is the best of the four and stands.", "traps": {"a": "Items consistently relating to each other is internal consistency reliability (split-half, KR-20, Cronbach's alpha), a property of the test's own items, not validity against a criterion.", "b": "Giving two tests at the same time is only the timing half of concurrent validity; without a high correlation between the test and an accepted criterion it shows nothing about validity.", "c": "Two or more clinicians agreeing on the outcome is inter-rater (inter-scorer) reliability, measured by agreement indices such as kappa, not concurrent validity."}}, {"q": "The Weschler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for a disability allowance. The WAIS is an individually given adult intelligence test built by David Wechsler (1939, as the Wechsler-Bellevue; WAIS 1955, now in later editions such as the WAIS-IV, 2008). It covers ages 16 to 90 and gives a Full Scale IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, plus index scores for verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed. Because it yields a standardised, norm-based measure of intellectual functioning, it is used to document intellectual disability (IQ about 70 or below, together with deficits in adaptive behaviour) or cognitive decline after brain injury or dementia. When an adult claims a state disability benefit on these grounds, the claim needs objective evidence, and a WAIS report provides it. Example: Arjun, aged 24, applies for a disability pension; his psychologist gives the WAIS-IV and an adaptive behaviour scale, and his IQ of 62 with poor daily living skills documents his intellectual disability. Option b is right because eligibility for a disability allowance can hinge on a documented level of intellectual functioning, which is exactly what an adult IQ test provides. Exam trap: special educational needs (c) also rely on IQ testing, but they concern school children, who are tested with the child version, the WISC, not the adult scale. Key note: the item comes from a UK clinical psychology test bank (the one that accompanies Graham Davey's Psychopathology textbook), where disability allowance is a named benefit; the same question on Amity 2023 is keyed disability allowance too, and discrepancy scores are an output of testing rather than something one is eligible for; b stands. LBS's revised key for this paper (17 Sep 2019) deleted this question, so no one was marked on it; its first key (2 Sep) had marked c, special educational needs.", "traps": {"a": "A discrepancy score (for example IQ versus reading achievement, or one index versus another) is something the WAIS helps produce in learning disability assessment; it is a result of testing, not a benefit one is eligible for.", "c": "Special educational needs are assessed in school children, with the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC, ages 6 to 16) and educational tests, not with the adult scale.", "d": "Occupational benefits (work-related pay, pensions or compensation) are set by employment terms; intelligence tests are used in personnel selection, but a measured IQ does not decide eligibility for such benefits."}}, {"q": "An analogue observation enables:", "concept": "The answer is a: analogue observation enables surreptitious observation of the client. In behavioural assessment the clinician watches behaviour directly rather than relying only on what the client reports. Naturalistic observation happens in the real setting (home, classroom, ward). Analogue observation happens in a contrived, controlled setting built to resemble the real one: a clinic playroom where a parent and child carry out a set task, or a role-played social situation in which a client must refuse a request or start a conversation. Because the setting is arranged in advance, the clinician can watch from behind a one-way mirror or through a video link, unseen and unobtrusive, which is what 'surreptitious' means. This reduces reactivity (people behaving differently because they know they are being watched) and lets behaviour be coded in a standard way. The cost is generalisation: behaviour in a clinic room may not match behaviour at home. Example: Meera's parents say she refuses every instruction, so the psychologist asks her mother to have Meera tidy toys in a playroom and records Meera's compliance through a one-way mirror. Option a is right because the controlled analogue setting is what makes hidden, unobtrusive observation of the client possible. Exam trap: the word 'analogue' also appears in analogue research, where non-clinical people stand in for patients; that is a research strategy, not an observation method. Key note: the booklet prints 'Superstitious', plainly a slip for 'surreptitious' (unseen, unobtrusive), and the option is shown corrected; b describes analogue research, a different use of the word, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Using non-clinical people as stand-ins for a clinical group (snake-fearful students for phobic patients) is analogue research, a research design, not what analogue observation of a client enables.", "c": "Watching several clients at once belongs to group settings such as group therapy or classroom observation; it is not the defining advantage of an analogue setting.", "d": "Analogue observation needs only one observer behind a mirror or a video record; requiring three or more clinicians is not part of the method."}}, {"q": "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:", "concept": "The answer is b: the DSM-IV-TR intellectual criterion for mental retardation was an IQ about two standard deviations below the mean. Intelligence tests such as David Wechsler's scales (from 1939) are standardised to a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, so two SD below the mean is 100 minus 30, an IQ of 70. DSM-IV-TR (2000) required significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, an IQ of about 70 or below on an individually given test, together with deficits in adaptive functioning (communication, self-care, social and practical skills) and onset before age 18. Allowing for measurement error, scores up to about 75 could qualify when adaptive deficits were clear. On the normal curve only about 2.3 percent of people score below 70. Severity was graded by IQ: mild 50 to 55 up to about 70 (about 85 percent of cases), moderate 35 to 40 up to 50 to 55, severe 20 to 25 up to 35 to 40, and profound below 20 to 25. DSM-5 later renamed the condition intellectual disability and graded severity by adaptive functioning instead. Example: Kabir, aged 9, scores 68 on the WISC and needs help with dressing and handling money; he meets both the IQ and the adaptive criteria. Option b is right because 70 lies exactly two SD below the mean of 100. Exam trap: one SD below the mean (85) marks the upper edge of borderline intellectual functioning (71 to 84), not the cut-off for mental retardation.", "traps": {"a": "One SD below the mean is an IQ of 85, which lies in the low-average range; about 16 percent of people score below it, far too many for a diagnosis meant for about 2 percent.", "c": "Three SD below the mean is an IQ of 55, which lies around the boundary between mild and moderate retardation, not at the overall diagnostic cut-off.", "d": "Four SD below the mean is an IQ of 40, which falls in the moderate to severe range; as a cut-off it would exclude every mild case, about 85 percent of all cases."}}, {"q": "Trait theorists use: I. 16 PF - NEO-PI - MBTI II. MMPI - EPQ - Rorschach test III. TAT - MBTI - 16 PF IV. EPQ - MMPI - NEO-PI", "concept": "The answer is a: sets I and IV are the tests trait theorists use. Trait theorists describe personality as a small number of stable dimensions on which everyone can be placed, and they measure them with structured self-report inventories scored against norms. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949) measures 16 source traits found by factor analysis; Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's NEO Personality Inventory (NEO-PI, 1985) measures the Big Five; Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck's Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, 1975) measures psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI, Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is an objective self-report inventory scored on scales, so such questions file it with the trait measures. The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) of Isabel Briggs Myers and Katharine Briggs (published 1962) is also a structured questionnaire. Sets II and III each contain a projective technique: the Rorschach inkblots (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT, Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), which present ambiguous material, assume that people project hidden motives into it, and are scored by interpretation. Example: Meera, studying conscientiousness in bank staff, gives them the NEO-PI and the 16PF, not inkblots. Option a is right because I and IV are the only sets made up entirely of structured inventories. Exam trap: one projective test spoils a whole set, so scan each set for the Rorschach or the TAT first; that removes II and III, and with them options b, c and d. Key note: strictly, the MBTI is a type measure based on Carl Jung's (1921) psychological types, not a trait measure, and the MMPI was built by empirical keying rather than from trait theory; but I and IV is still the only option without a projective test, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "This adds set III, but set III contains the TAT, a projective storytelling test from Henry Murray's need-press theory, which trait theorists do not use.", "c": "Set I is acceptable, but set III includes the TAT, a projective technique scored by interpretation, so I and III cannot both be trait measures.", "d": "Set IV is acceptable, but set II includes the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), a projective method, not a trait inventory."}}, {"q": "Find out the correct answers from the codes given below: Topographical organization: 1. Applies to sensory cortical areas 2. High degree of topographical organisation shows in the auditory pathway 3. Fibres in this pathway originate from the ganglion cells of the retina 4. Fibres then synapse at the lateral geniculate body of the hypothalamus", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 3 are correct. Topographical (topographic) organisation means that neighbouring points on a sensory surface are represented by neighbouring points in the brain, so each sensory cortex carries an orderly map: retinotopic in vision (the primary visual cortex maps the retina, with the small fovea given a disproportionately large share), tonotopic in hearing (each sound frequency has its own place in auditory cortex) and somatotopic in touch (the body map, or homunculus, of the postcentral gyrus). So statement 1, that it applies to sensory cortical areas, is true. Statement 3 describes the visual pathway: the fibres of the optic nerve are the axons of the retina's ganglion cells, which carry the signal out of the eye, so it is true. Statement 4 is false: optic tract fibres synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (body) of the thalamus, not of the hypothalamus, before the optic radiations carry the signal to the striate cortex (area 17). The hypothalamus receives only a small retinal input, to its suprachiasmatic nucleus, for setting circadian rhythms. Example: a small stroke in one spot of Arjun's left primary visual cortex blinds one matching patch of his right visual field, because the map keeps retinal neighbours together. Option c is right because 1 and 3 are true and 4 is false. Exam trap: the lateral geniculate body sounds hypothalamic because both lie in the diencephalon, but the geniculate bodies are thalamic relays (lateral for vision, medial for hearing). Key note: statement 2 names the auditory pathway, which is tonotopic in reality, but statement 3 then says 'this pathway' starts in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot both describe the same pathway; the paired statements describe the visual pathway and c (1 and 3) is the only fully correct set; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 and 3 are right, but 4 is false: the lateral geniculate body belongs to the thalamus, not the hypothalamus.", "b": "2 places the pathway in hearing, yet 3 says 'this pathway' begins in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot describe the same pathway (hearing relays through the medial geniculate nucleus); this option also leaves out the true statement 1.", "d": "1 is true, but 4 is false because the lateral geniculate body is a thalamic relay nucleus, not part of the hypothalamus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the notion of confirmatory bias?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe confirmatory (confirmation) bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice, interpret and remember information in ways that support what one already believes, while giving too little weight to information that contradicts it. Peter Wason (1960) showed it with his 2-4-6 task: told that the triple 2-4-6 fits a rule, people tested only triples that fitted their own guess (such as 8-10-12) and rarely tried triples that could prove the guess wrong, so they often announced a wrong rule with confidence. In clinical work the bias appears in three linked ways. A clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may ignore information that does not fit it (a). A clinician may hold on to a stereotype about a group, such as assuming an elderly client's low mood is just part of ageing, and disregard evidence against it (b). And when information is ambiguous, such as a vague answer in an interview, the clinician tends to read it as support for the favoured hypothesis (c). Together these lead to premature closure, where assessment stops too early. Example: within five minutes Dr Kabir decides that Riya has borderline personality disorder; he notes every sign of mood swings, skips her clear history of hypomanic episodes, and reads her tearfulness as further proof. Option d is right because ignoring disconfirming data, clinging to stereotypes and reading ambiguity as support are all forms of the same bias. Exam trap: each single option is true, so picking one of them alone loses the mark; when every part describes the same process, 'all the above' is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "Ignoring information that does not support one's hypothesis is a true form of confirmation bias, but it is only one of three correct statements, so a alone is incomplete.", "b": "Disregarding information that challenges a stereotype is confirmation bias applied to beliefs about groups; it is correct but incomplete on its own.", "c": "Reading ambiguous information as support for the favoured hypothesis is biased interpretation, a core part of confirmation bias, but a and b are also correct."}}, {"q": "OCD is NOT thought to play a role in which of the following cognitive constructs?", "concept": "The answer is b: underestimation of personal ability is not one of the cognitive constructs thought to play a role in obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Cognitive models of OCD start from the finding that almost everyone has odd, unwanted intrusive thoughts. Paul Salkovskis (1985) argued that OCD develops when a person appraises such intrusions as meaning that he or she is responsible for preventing harm, which drives neutralising and compulsions. Stanley Rachman (1993) added thought-action fusion, the belief that thinking about an act makes it more likely or is as bad as doing it. The Obsessive Compulsive Cognitions Working Group (1997, 2005), an international team of researchers, grouped the central beliefs into six domains: inflated responsibility; overimportance of thoughts; importance of controlling one's thoughts; overestimation of threat; intolerance of uncertainty; and perfectionism. They built the Obsessive Beliefs Questionnaire to measure them. A sense of low competence fits depression or low self-efficacy better than OCD, and it is not one of the domains. Example: Arjun has a sudden thought of pushing a stranger onto the railway tracks; he believes that having the thought means he might do it (overimportance of thoughts), that he must stop such thoughts (control), and he checks the platform edge again and again because he cannot bear not being sure (intolerance of uncertainty). Option b is right because it is the only option outside the recognised OCD belief domains. Exam trap: the stem is worded backwards (it asks which construct does not play a role in OCD), and 'control of thoughts' (a) can look like a healthy skill rather than a problem, yet the belief that one must control one's thoughts is itself one of the six domains.", "traps": {"a": "The importance of controlling one's thoughts is one of the six belief domains of the Obsessive Compulsive Cognitions Working Group; trying hard to suppress intrusions makes them return more often.", "c": "Intolerance of uncertainty is a working group domain: the person cannot bear not knowing for sure, which drives checking and reassurance seeking.", "d": "Inflated responsibility, the sense of being responsible for preventing harm, is the central belief in Paul Salkovskis's (1985) model and a working group domain."}}, {"q": "Standardization allows the clinician to:", "concept": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this paper marks all of the above. Standardisation has two parts. First, the test is given and scored in the same way for everyone: identical instructions, materials, time limits and scoring rules, so that differences in scores reflect differences between people rather than differences in procedure. Second, the test is given to a large, representative norm sample (the standardisation sample), matched to the target population on age, sex, region, education and so on, and the distribution of their scores becomes the norms. A raw score means little on its own; compared with the norms, it can be turned into a percentile rank, a z score, a T score or a deviation IQ, which shows how far above or below the average of comparable people the client stands (option a). Many clinical tests are also standardised on diagnosed groups, so the norms carry cut-off scores: a T score of 65 or more on an MMPI-2 clinical scale, or a Beck Depression Inventory-II total of 29 or more (the severe range), tells the clinician that the client scores like people who meet criteria for a disorder, and how likely that is (options b and c). Example: Riya gets a raw score of 38 on an anxiety scale; the norm table for women of her age puts this at the 90th percentile, and the manual says most people above this cut-off in the clinical sample met criteria for generalised anxiety disorder, so the clinician follows up with a diagnostic interview. Option d is right on the key's reading because a standardised score both locates the client in the normal distribution and, through clinical norms and cut-offs, suggests whether the client might meet diagnostic criteria. Exam trap: a test score alone never makes a diagnosis; diagnosis still needs an interview, history and clinical judgement. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because norms on their own only locate a score in a distribution and a test score never diagnoses. LBS's official key for this paper marks d, all of the above. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "Locating the score in the normal distribution is the first thing standardisation gives, but not the only one: clinical norms and cut-off scores also let the score suggest whether criteria might be met, so the key wants all of the above.", "b": "Estimating how likely a client is to meet DSM-IV criteria is also possible with standardised clinical norms (for example, how many people above a cut-off met criteria in the norm sample), so b is part of the answer, not all of it.", "c": "Using a score to estimate whether diagnostic criteria might be met is what cut-offs from standardised clinical samples allow, but a and b are true as well, so c on its own is incomplete."}}, {"q": "The famous book 'Principles of psychology' was authored by ------.", "concept": "The answer is c: The Principles of Psychology was written by William James (1890). William James (1842 to 1910) trained in medicine at Harvard and taught physiology, philosophy and psychology there. He worked on the Principles for about twelve years and published it in two volumes in 1890, followed by a shorter version, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892). In the book he described consciousness as a continuous, ever-changing stream of consciousness rather than a set of elements; called habit 'the enormous fly-wheel of society'; split the self into the 'I' (the self as knower) and the 'me' (the self as known, with material, social and spiritual parts); and set out his theory of emotion, proposed independently by Carl Lange (1885), that we feel afraid because we tremble and run, not the other way round. He defined psychology as 'the science of mental life'. The Principles made James the father of American psychology and the inspiration of functionalism, which asked what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Option c is right because The Principles of Psychology is William James's book. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (a) wrote a book with a look-alike title, Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), the founding text of experimental psychology, so a hurried reader picks a.", "traps": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt wrote Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), a different book with a similar title, and founded the Leipzig laboratory in 1879.", "b": "Edward B. Titchener, Wundt's student and the founder of structuralism at Cornell, wrote the Experimental Psychology manuals (1901 to 1905) and A Textbook of Psychology (1909), not The Principles of Psychology.", "d": "John B. Watson wrote 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' (1913) and Behaviorism (1925); he rejected the study of consciousness that the Principles centred on."}}, {"q": "The functionalists are strongly influenced by ------.", "concept": "The answer is a: the functionalists were strongly influenced by Charles Darwin. In On the Origin of Species (1859), Charles Darwin argued that living things evolve by natural selection: features that help an organism adapt to its environment survive and are passed on. Functionalism, the first truly American school of psychology, carried this idea into the study of the mind. Instead of asking, as the structuralists did, what consciousness is made of, the functionalists asked what it is for: how mental processes such as perception, memory, emotion and habit help the organism adapt and survive. William James (1890) laid the ground in The Principles of Psychology; John Dewey's paper on the reflex arc (1896) and James Rowland Angell's 'The Province of Functional Psychology' (1907) made Chicago the centre of the school, and Harvey Carr (1925) took the adaptive act as its unit. Darwin's influence also explains functionalism's openness to animal psychology, individual differences, child development and applied fields such as education and mental testing, all of which bear on adaptation. Example: a functionalist studying how Kabir learns to drive would ask how attention and habit help him cope with busy Kozhikode traffic, not which sensations make up his experience of the road. Option a is right because Darwin's evolutionary theory of adaptation was the main outside influence that shaped functionalism. Exam trap: William James (d) is the tempting choice, but he is counted among the founders of functionalism, not as an outside influence on it. Key note: some readers argue that James also shaped the Chicago functionalists, which is true; but he is usually classed as a founder or forerunner of the school, and the influence behind all of them, James included, was Darwin's evolutionary theory; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Wilhelm Wundt's analysis of consciousness into elements was the approach the functionalists reacted against, through Titchener's structuralism, rather than the influence that shaped them.", "c": "Edward B. Titchener founded structuralism and coined the label 'functional psychology' in 1898 to contrast it with his own approach; he was the functionalists' opponent, not their inspiration.", "d": "William James is a founder (or forerunner) of functionalism; the school's outside influence, which James himself drew on, was Darwin's evolutionary theory."}}, {"q": "Rejecting the concept of S-R connectionism, further S-O-R concept was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the S-O-R concept was developed by Robert S. Woodworth, who rejected plain S-R connectionism. Edward Thorndike's (1898) connectionism, and later John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism, explained behaviour as learned bonds between a stimulus (S) and a response (R). Robert S. Woodworth (1918), at Columbia University, argued that this left out the most important part: the organism (O) in between. The same stimulus produces different responses depending on what is going on inside the organism: its drives, needs, past learning, expectations, attention and bodily state. In Dynamic Psychology (1918) he stressed drive and motivation, and in his textbook Psychology (1929 edition) he set out the S-O-R formula. This mediational view, with inner states standing between stimulus and response, was later taken up by neobehaviourists such as Clark Hull (1943) and Edward Tolman (1932) and by cognitive psychology. Woodworth, a functionalist, is remembered for making psychology 'dynamic', concerned with why organisms act as well as how. Example: a plate of hot dosas (S) makes hungry Meera reach for one (R), but after a big lunch the same plate gets no response; the difference lies in the organism (O), her state of hunger. Option c is right because the S-O-R formula is Woodworth's correction to S-R connectionism. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike (d) tempts because his name is tied to connectionism, but he is the author of the S-R bonds that Woodworth rejected, not of the S-O-R alternative.", "traps": {"a": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism (1913) on an S-R model that explained behaviour without inner states, so he represents the view S-O-R was meant to correct.", "b": "Ivan Pavlov described classical conditioning, the pairing of two stimuli that produces reflexive responses; he did not propose an organism term between S and R.", "d": "Edward Thorndike's connectionism (1898), with its law of effect, is the S-R bond theory that Woodworth rejected, so Thorndike is the target, not the author of S-O-R."}}, {"q": "The concept of building block of consciousness was laid by ------.", "concept": "The answer is b: the idea of building blocks of consciousness was laid down by Wilhelm Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) founded the first formal psychology laboratory at the University of Leipzig, which is why 1879 is taken as the birth of psychology as a separate experimental science. He defined psychology as the study of immediate conscious experience and tried to analyse that experience into its simplest elements, much as chemists break compounds into elements. He named two kinds of elements: sensations, with quality and intensity, and simple feelings, which he placed on three axes in his tridimensional theory of feeling (1896): pleasure-displeasure, tension-relaxation and excitement-depression. These elements combine by creative synthesis into complex experiences such as perceptions and ideas, the whole gaining properties the parts lack, as a melody is more than its notes. His method was introspection under experimental control, with trained observers making short judgements of signalled stimuli, together with reaction-time measures. His student Edward Titchener (1898) took the element idea to Cornell and made it the core of structuralism, listing sensations, images and feelings as the basic elements. Example: when Arjun hears a single tone in a Wundtian experiment, he reports its pitch and loudness and whether it feels pleasant, tense or calm, breaking the experience into its parts. Option b is right because analysing consciousness into elementary building blocks is Wundt's programme. Exam trap: Edward Titchener is the name usually tied to structuralism, but he is not offered here; John B. Watson (a) is the opposite pole, since he rejected consciousness altogether.", "traps": {"a": "John B. Watson's behaviourism (1913) rejected consciousness and introspection entirely and studied only observable behaviour.", "c": "Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis around the unconscious mind and its conflicts, not an analysis of conscious experience into elements.", "d": "Carl Jung's analytical psychology dealt with the personal and collective unconscious, archetypes and psychological types, not the elements of consciousness."}}, {"q": "A provisional theory to explain observed facts is known as ------.", "concept": "The answer is c: a provisional theory put forward to explain observed facts is a hypothesis. A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement about the relationship between two or more variables, offered as a possible explanation of something observed and then checked against data. It is provisional: research may support it, or it may be rejected and replaced. A good hypothesis is clear, specific, testable (falsifiable, in Karl Popper's (1959) sense), consistent with known facts, and stated in terms of variables that can be measured. Researchers usually write a research (alternative) hypothesis, such as 'sleep loss impairs recall', and a null hypothesis of no difference or no relationship, which statistical tests try to reject. The other terms are building blocks rather than explanations. A concept is an abstraction formed by grouping observations that share features, such as 'reward' or 'fatigue'. A construct is a concept deliberately invented or adopted for scientific use, often not directly observable, such as anxiety, intelligence or self-esteem, and defined through the operations that measure it. Example: Meera notices that students who sleep late score poorly in morning tests; she proposes that sleep loss lowers memory performance and tests it by comparing recall in students who slept 4 hours with those who slept 8. Option c is right because a hypothesis is exactly a provisional explanation of observed facts awaiting test. Exam trap: construct (b) sounds technical and theory-like, but a construct is a variable to be measured, not an explanation of facts; and a theory proper is broader than a hypothesis, a tested framework that links many hypotheses.", "traps": {"a": "A concept is an abstraction that groups observations sharing features (such as 'fatigue'); it names a class of things but does not explain observed facts.", "b": "A construct is a concept adopted for scientific use, such as anxiety or intelligence, defined by how it is measured; it is a variable within an explanation, not the provisional explanation itself.", "d": "'Statement' is a general word for any declarative sentence, not a technical research term for a tentative explanation."}}, {"q": "Author of \"A Theory of Human Motivation\":", "concept": "The answer is a: 'A Theory of Human Motivation' was written by Abraham Maslow (1943). Abraham Maslow published this paper in Psychological Review in 1943. In it he proposed the hierarchy of needs: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs, love and belongingness needs, esteem needs, and at the top self-actualisation, the need to realise one's full potential. Lower, more basic needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher needs become strong motives; a starving person thinks of food, not of status. He expanded the idea in Motivation and Personality (1954), where he described self-actualising people such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, and later contrasted deficiency needs (D-needs) with growth or being needs (B-needs). Maslow is a founder of humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism. The other three authors also belong to humanistic and existential psychology, which is why they are offered: Rollo May wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950), Viktor Frankl wrote Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German, English 1959), and Carl Rogers wrote On Becoming a Person (1961). Option a is right because the 1943 paper that introduced the hierarchy of needs is Maslow's. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (d) is the other famous humanistic name and also wrote about growth and self-actualisation, but his books are about client-centred therapy and the self, not a theory of motivation.", "traps": {"b": "Rollo May, the American existential psychologist, wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950) and Love and Will (1969), not the 1943 motivation paper.", "c": "Viktor Frankl (printed 'Victor'), founder of logotherapy, wrote Man's Search for Meaning (1946, English 1959), which centres on the will to meaning rather than a hierarchy of needs.", "d": "Carl Rogers, founder of client-centred therapy, wrote Client-Centered Therapy (1951) and On Becoming a Person (1961), not 'A Theory of Human Motivation'."}}, {"q": "According to Freud, resolution of Oedipal complex ------.", "concept": "The answer is d: according to Sigmund Freud (1924), resolution of the Oedipus complex is marked by a sublimation of libidinal energy. In Freud's psychosexual theory, during the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) the boy desires his mother and sees his father as a rival. Fearing that the father will punish him by castration (castration anxiety), he represses the desire and identifies with his father, taking in the father's values and standards. This identification forms the superego, the moral part of personality. The resolution carries the child into the latency period (about 6 years to puberty), in which sexual wishes lie dormant and libidinal energy is sublimated, that is, redirected into socially valued activities such as schoolwork, friendships with same-sex peers, games, hobbies and skills. Freud described a parallel process in girls, the feminine Oedipus attitude: penis envy turns the girl from her mother to her father, and she resolves it more slowly and less completely by identifying with her mother. Carl Jung (1913) called this the Electra complex, a term Freud rejected. Example: after the age of six, Kabir stops clinging to his mother, copies his father's way of speaking, and pours his energy into cricket and school projects. Option d is right because the passing of the Oedipus complex leads into latency, where libido is sublimated into non-sexual pursuits. Exam trap: c sounds right because the Oedipal stage is sexual, but genital sexuality emerges only at puberty, after latency, so the resolution is a step away from sexual expression, not its arrival.", "traps": {"a": "Freud did describe an analogous process in girls, the feminine Oedipus attitude, driven by penis envy and resolved by identification with the mother; Carl Jung named it the Electra complex (1913).", "b": "Rivalry and hostility towards the father are part of the complex itself; its resolution comes when the boy gives up the rivalry and identifies with the father.", "c": "Genital sexuality emerges at puberty, in the genital stage after latency; the Oedipal resolution leads instead into the latency period of dormant sexual interest."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following best characterizes the difference between the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems?", "concept": "The answer is b: the difference between the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems is best described as energy expenditure versus energy conservation. Both are branches of the autonomic nervous system, which, with the somatic nervous system, makes up the peripheral nervous system. The autonomic system works without conscious control, regulating the heart, blood vessels, glands, gut and pupils. The sympathetic branch, whose nerves leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, prepares the body for action and spends energy: heart rate and blood pressure rise, the airways widen, the pupils dilate, the liver releases glucose, the adrenal medulla pours out adrenaline, and digestion slows. Walter Cannon (1915, 1929) called this the fight or flight response. The parasympathetic branch, whose nerves leave the brainstem (including the vagus nerve) and the sacral spinal cord, conserves and restores energy: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils, stimulates salivation and digestion, and supports rest and repair, often summed up as 'rest and digest'. Most organs receive both branches, which work in balance. Example: when a dog suddenly runs at Riya, her heart pounds and her mouth goes dry (sympathetic); an hour later, relaxing after dinner, her heart rate slows and digestion picks up (parasympathetic). Option b is right because spending energy for action versus saving and restoring it is the core contrast between the two branches. Exam trap: a describes the somatic versus autonomic contrast, not sympathetic versus parasympathetic; both autonomic branches control involuntary activity.", "traps": {"a": "Voluntary versus involuntary movement separates the somatic nervous system (skeletal muscles) from the autonomic system; both sympathetic and parasympathetic branches are involuntary.", "c": "The somatic nervous system is itself part of the peripheral nervous system, so this pair is not a contrast at all, and neither term names the sympathetic or parasympathetic branch.", "d": "Sensory neurons and afferent fibres are the same thing (both carry signals towards the central nervous system), so this is not a difference between any two systems."}}, {"q": "Multiple Abstract Variance Analysis is used to study the effect of:", "concept": "The answer is d: none of these, because Multiple Abstract Variance Analysis (MAVA) is not a design for independent and dependent variables at all, and LBS's official key for this paper marks d. MAVA is Raymond Cattell's (1953, 1960) behaviour-genetic method for estimating how much of the variation in a personality trait comes from heredity and how much from environment. Cattell measured the same trait in several kinds of family pairs: identical twins reared together and apart, fraternal twins, siblings reared together and apart, and unrelated children reared together. Comparing how alike each kind of pair is, he solved a set of equations for the hereditary and environmental parts of the trait's variance, within families and between families, and for the correlation between heredity and environment. Hall and Lindzey's Theories of Personality describes it under Cattell's work on nature and nurture. Options a, b and c describe analysis of variance designs instead: a is a one-way multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA), b is a factorial ANOVA, and c is a factorial MANOVA. The names sound alike, but MAVA does not study the effect of manipulated independent variables on dependent variables; it splits the variance of one trait into its sources. Example: if identical twins reared apart are almost as alike in dominance as identical twins reared together, MAVA puts most of the variance in dominance down to heredity. Option d is right because none of the three ANOVA-type descriptions fits Cattell's method. Exam trap: the abbreviation tempts students to read MAVA as MANOVA and pick c. Key note: this mock used to mark c, on the reading that the options describe MANOVA and the stem names it loosely. LBS's official key for this paper marks d, none of these, which is the strict answer for Cattell's MAVA, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "One independent variable on several dependent variables is a one-way MANOVA. MAVA is not an analysis of variance design; it splits the variance of one trait into hereditary and environmental parts.", "b": "Several independent variables on one dependent variable is a factorial ANOVA. It looks close because MAVA looks at several sources of variance in one trait, but those sources are estimated from family resemblance, not manipulated as independent variables.", "c": "Several independent variables on several dependent variables is a factorial MANOVA, the method the abbreviation is often confused with. Cattell's MAVA is a twin and sibling design for nature and nurture, so c does not describe it."}}, {"q": "Parameter : statistic :: population mean : ------.", "concept": "The answer is b: as a parameter is to a statistic, so the population mean is to a sample measure, and the only sample measure offered is the sample median. A population is the whole group a researcher wants to describe, such as all college students in Kerala; a sample is the part actually studied. A parameter is a numerical value that describes a population. It is usually unknown and is written with a Greek letter: mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation, rho for the population correlation. A statistic is the matching value calculated from a sample and is written with a Roman letter: x-bar for the sample mean, s for the sample standard deviation, r for the sample correlation. Statistics are used to estimate parameters, and they vary from sample to sample (sampling error), while a parameter is fixed. The analogy therefore needs a measure taken from a sample. The exact counterpart of the population mean would be the sample mean, which is not offered; the sample median is another measure of central tendency computed from a sample, so it is the only statistic among the options. Example: the average height of all Class 12 students in India is a parameter; Kabir measures 200 students in Kozhikode and finds their median height is 165 cm, which is a statistic. Option b is right because it is the only option explicitly described as a sample value, the second half of the parameter to statistic analogy. Exam trap: standard deviation (a) looks like a natural partner for the mean, but without the word 'sample' it could describe a population (a parameter) just as well, so it does not complete the analogy.", "traps": {"a": "Standard deviation is a measure of spread that can be a parameter (sigma) or a statistic (s); with no 'sample' label it does not supply the sample side of the analogy.", "c": "Kurtosis describes how peaked or heavy-tailed a distribution is; it can be computed for a population or a sample, and it is a shape measure, not a sample counterpart of the mean.", "d": "Skewness describes the asymmetry of a distribution; like kurtosis it is a shape measure that can belong to a population or a sample, so it does not mark the statistic side."}}, {"q": "------ is not an important determinant when considering the effects of modelling on learning.", "concept": "The answer is c: the sex of the model is not one of the important determinants listed when considering the effects of modelling on learning. Albert Bandura (1977), in his social learning theory, explained observational learning (modelling) through four processes. Attention: the observer must notice the model; this depends on the modelling stimuli, including the functional value of the model's behaviour (whether it brings useful results) and its distinctiveness, and on the observer's own characteristics such as arousal and past reinforcement. Retention: the observed act must be stored in memory as images and verbal codes and rehearsed. Motor reproduction: the observer must be able to turn the stored memory into action. Motivation: the observer needs a reason to perform, which comes from reinforcement, whether external, vicarious (seeing the model rewarded) or self-reinforcement. So reinforcement, retention and functional value are all named determinants. The classic evidence is the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961): children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the aggression. Example: Meera learns to roll chapatis by watching her grandmother; she must notice the technique, remember it, have the skill to copy it, and want the praise that follows. Option c is right because the sex of the model, as such, is not one of the determinants in Bandura's list. Exam trap: reinforcement (a) and retention (b) sound like general learning terms rather than modelling ones, but both are part of Bandura's account. Key note: the Bobo doll study did find sex-of-model effects (the male model was imitated more for physical aggression), so model characteristics matter through similarity and status; but the sex of the model as such is the one option not listed among the determinants, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Reinforcement (external, vicarious and self) is a named determinant in Bandura's motivational process: it decides whether a learned act is actually performed.", "b": "Retention, coding the modelled act into images and words and rehearsing it, is one of Bandura's four processes, so it is an important determinant.", "d": "The functional value of the model's behaviour, whether it leads to useful results, is listed among the attentional determinants: people attend to models whose actions pay off."}}, {"q": "A social psychologist finds that subjects who drink coffee before viewing a videotape of a comedian find her to be funnier than subjects who didn't drink coffee. These results best support:", "concept": "The answer is c: the coffee result best supports Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) cognitive-physiological, or two-factor, theory of emotion. The theory says an emotion has two ingredients: a state of physiological arousal and a cognitive label that explains it. Arousal itself is fairly undifferentiated; the same racing heart can be felt as fear, anger or joy, and the situation tells us which. In their classic study, Schachter and Singer injected volunteers with adrenaline (epinephrine), told some what side effects to expect and left others uninformed or misinformed, then placed each with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. The uninformed participants, who had no ready explanation for their arousal, tended to catch the stooge's mood, while the informed ones put their pounding hearts down to the injection. In the coffee study the caffeine raises arousal, the viewers do not attribute it to the drink, and they read it as amusement at the comedian: the arousal is misattributed to the most obvious cause in view. Dolf Zillmann (1971) developed the same idea as excitation transfer, in which leftover arousal from one source intensifies the emotion felt towards the next. Example: Arjun runs up four flights of stairs to a friend's party, then laughs louder than ever at a mediocre joke, because his lingering arousal is labelled as mirth. Option c is right because the result depends on arousal plus a cognitive label, the two factors of Schachter and Singer's theory. Exam trap: the James-Lange theory also puts bodily arousal first, so it tempts, but it has no labelling step; it says a specific bodily pattern is itself the emotion, so it cannot explain why non-specific caffeine arousal turns into amusement in particular.", "traps": {"a": "The James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885) says an emotion is the perception of a specific bodily change (we are afraid because we tremble); it has no cognitive labelling of ambiguous arousal, so it cannot explain why caffeine arousal is felt as amusement.", "b": "Inoculation theory (William McGuire, 1961) concerns resistance to persuasion: exposing people to weakened counterarguments, like a vaccine, makes their attitudes harder to change; it says nothing about arousal or emotion.", "d": "Social comparison theory (Leon Festinger, 1954) says people judge their own abilities and opinions by comparing themselves with similar others; no one in the coffee study is comparing themselves with anyone."}}, {"q": "Carol Gilligan's criticism of Kohlberg's theory of moral development, center around:", "concept": "The answer is a: Carol Gilligan's criticism of Lawrence Kohlberg's theory centres on gender differences in orientations towards morality. Kohlberg (1958) scored how people reasoned about dilemmas, such as whether Heinz should steal a drug to save his dying wife, and placed the reasons in three levels and six stages: preconventional (obedience, self-interest), conventional (approval, law and order) and postconventional (social contract, universal principles). The top stages rest on an ethic of justice: abstract rights, fairness and impartial rules. Gilligan, who had worked with Kohlberg at Harvard, argued in In a Different Voice (1982) that the scale was built on a male model. Kohlberg's founding sample was all boys, and when women were scored by his manual they often landed at stage 3 (the good boy, nice girl orientation) and so looked less morally mature. Gilligan held that many women reason in an ethic of care: concern for relationships, responsibility to particular people and a wish that no one be hurt. From interviews with 29 women deciding about abortion, she traced three levels of care: caring only for oneself, goodness as self-sacrifice, and the morality of nonviolence, which balances care for self and others. Lawrence Walker's (1984) review later found little sex difference in Kohlberg scores once education was allowed for, but the care and justice distinction survived: both sexes use both voices. Example: Meera answers the Heinz dilemma by asking how Heinz, his wife and the chemist could talk it out and keep their relationships, not which rule ranks higher; Kohlberg's scoring would rate her lower. Option a is right because Gilligan's critique is precisely that the theory is gender biased and neglects the care orientation. Exam trap: Kohlberg was also criticised for weak cross-cultural support (Richard Shweder, 1987; John Snarey's 1985 review of 45 studies), so option c is a real criticism, but it belongs to those critics, not to Gilligan.", "traps": {"b": "Neglect of socialisation is a social learning criticism (Albert Bandura and others argued that moral behaviour is learned through modelling and reinforcement); Gilligan's point was about gender and care, not about socialisation.", "c": "Weak cross-cultural support is the critique of Richard Shweder and of John Snarey's (1985) review, which found stage 5 mainly in urban, educated groups; it is a real criticism of Kohlberg, but not Gilligan's.", "d": "How morality is operationalised (moral reasoning on hypothetical dilemmas rather than moral behaviour) is the reasoning versus behaviour gap raised by other critics; Gilligan's complaint was that the scoring ignored a female care voice."}}, {"q": "The sensory modality which is most integrated with limbic system?", "concept": "The answer is c: smell (olfaction) is the sensory modality most closely integrated with the limbic system. Every other sense sends its signals to the cortex through a relay in the thalamus: vision through the lateral geniculate nucleus, hearing through the medial geniculate nucleus, touch and taste through the ventral posterior nuclei. Smell is the exception. Odour molecules bind receptors in the olfactory epithelium high in the nose, the olfactory nerve (cranial nerve I) carries the signal to the olfactory bulb, and the bulb projects directly to the piriform (primary olfactory) cortex, the amygdala and the entorhinal cortex, the gateway to the hippocampus. These are limbic and medial temporal structures for emotion and memory, and the old name for this region, the rhinencephalon or 'smell brain', records the link. Only later does information reach the orbitofrontal cortex, partly through the thalamus, for conscious identification of the odour. Because the route to emotion and memory is so short, smells trigger vivid, emotional memories, often from childhood. This is called the Proust effect, after Marcel Proust's madeleine dipped in tea in Swann's Way (1913), the first volume of In Search of Lost Time. Example: the scent of jasmine at a wedding instantly takes Riya back to her grandmother's house, with a rush of warmth, before she can even name the flower. Option c is right because olfaction alone projects from receptor to limbic structures without a thalamic relay, giving it the most direct link to emotion and memory. Exam trap: vision has the most cortex and touch the largest receptor surface, but both relay through the thalamus, as hearing does; the same fact is often asked as 'the only sense that bypasses the thalamus', and that answer is also smell, not taste.", "traps": {"a": "Vision runs from the retina along the optic nerve to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and on to the primary visual cortex of the occipital lobe; its main route is cortical, not limbic.", "b": "Hearing relays through the inferior colliculus and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the auditory cortex of the temporal lobe, so it reaches limbic structures only indirectly.", "d": "Touch ascends the spinal cord (dorsal columns and medial lemniscus) to the ventral posterior thalamus and then the somatosensory cortex of the parietal lobe, again through a thalamic relay rather than straight into the limbic system."}}, {"q": "Put the parts of the brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: 1. Metencephalon 2. Mesencephalon 3. Diancephalon 4. Telencephalon 5. Myelencephalon", "concept": "The answer is c: from higher to lower the order is telencephalon (4), diencephalon (3), mesencephalon (2), metencephalon (1) and myelencephalon (5). The names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube forms three swellings: the forebrain (prosencephalon), the midbrain (mesencephalon) and the hindbrain (rhombencephalon). Before birth the forebrain divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: cortex, basal ganglia, limbic structures and corpus callosum. The diencephalon ('between-brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with its tectum (the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow-brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. Example: Arjun remembers the order with a simple rule: telencephalon on top, then the other four in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: metencephalon and mesencephalon differ by one letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it. Option d keeps the middle of the order but drops the telencephalon to the bottom.", "traps": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5 simply follows the list as printed: it starts with the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and puts the telencephalon fourth, which is not a top-to-bottom order.", "b": "2, 4, 1, 3, 5 starts with the midbrain, places the cerebral hemispheres second and puts the diencephalon below the pons, scrambling the order.", "d": "3, 2, 1, 5, 4 has the middle stretch right (diencephalon, midbrain, pons and cerebellum, medulla) but puts the telencephalon, the highest division, at the bottom."}}, {"q": "In prosopagnosia disorder a person is not able to:", "concept": "The answer is c: in prosopagnosia a person is not able to recognise faces. Prosopagnosia, or face blindness, is a visual agnosia: a failure of recognition despite intact sensation and intellect. The term was coined by the German neurologist Joachim Bodamer (1947), from the Greek prosopon, face, and agnosia, not knowing. The patient sees a face as a face, with eyes, nose and mouth, and can describe its features, but cannot tell whose face it is, even that of a spouse, a child or their own reflection in a mirror. They know people at once by voice, walk, clothes or a mole, which shows that the problem is specific to faces rather than to memory for people. The acquired form follows damage, usually bilateral or right-sided, to the fusiform gyrus on the underside of the brain where the occipital and temporal lobes meet. Nancy Kanwisher (1997) used fMRI to identify the fusiform face area there, a region that responds far more strongly to faces than to other objects. A developmental form, present from childhood without obvious brain injury, affects about 2 percent of people. Example: after a right occipitotemporal stroke, Kabir walks straight past his own wife at the railway station and recognises her only when she calls his name. Option c is right because face recognition is exactly the ability prosopagnosia removes, while the perception of size, colour and shape is spared. Exam trap: the stem says 'perceive', but prosopagnosia is a failure to recognise faces rather than to see them; the cortical colour loss in option b is a separate syndrome, cerebral achromatopsia, which can accompany prosopagnosia because the two regions lie close together.", "traps": {"a": "Judging size depends on depth cues and size constancy; distorted size perception (micropsia, macropsia) occurs in migraine aura or temporal lobe seizures, not in prosopagnosia.", "b": "Loss of colour vision after cortical damage is cerebral achromatopsia, from lesions of the colour area (the V4 region on the lingual and fusiform gyri); people with prosopagnosia see colour normally.", "d": "Inability to perceive shape is apperceptive visual agnosia (visual form agnosia, as in the patient D.F. studied by David Milner and Melvyn Goodale), a broader failure in which even simple forms cannot be copied or matched."}}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Adler, b. Bandura, c. Jung, d. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "concept": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (a-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (b-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (c-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (d-1). Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), founder of individual psychology, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal, never fully real, that pulls a person's behaviour forward, such as \"I must be the best\". Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) described reciprocal determinism (1978), later called triadic reciprocal causation (1986): person factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) treated libido as general psychic energy governed by principles borrowed from physics. By the principle of equivalence, energy withdrawn from one interest reappears in another of equal value; by the principle of entropy, energy flows toward a balance that is never reached. Karen Horney (The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, 1937) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives the strategies of moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Kabir retires from cricket and pours the same passion into coaching, Jung would call it the principle of equivalence. Option c is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs are correct. Exam trap: option d gets Adler and Horney right but swaps Bandura and Jung; \"reciprocal\" and \"equivalence\" both sound like a balance of forces, so students trade them. Bandura is the social-cognitive theorist of three interacting factors; Jung is the energy theorist.", "traps": {"a": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1 gives Adler triadic reciprocal causation (Bandura's), Bandura the principle of equivalence (Jung's) and Jung fictional finalism (Adler's); only Horney with basic anxiety is right.", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4 has every pair wrong: basic anxiety is Horney's, fictional finalism is Adler's, triadic reciprocal causation is Bandura's and the principle of equivalence is Jung's.", "d": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1 is right for Adler and Horney but swaps the middle two: the principle of equivalence is Jung's law of psychic energy, and triadic reciprocal causation is Bandura's."}}, {"q": "According to E.C. Tolman, there are two aversions: fright and pugnacity. Fright is avoidance of injury and pugnacity is avoidance of:", "concept": "The answer is b: in Edward C. Tolman's scheme, pugnacity is the avoidance of interference (obstruction). Tolman (1886 to 1959) was a purposive behaviourist: he studied whole, goal-directed acts and inferred intervening variables, such as cognitive maps and expectancies, between stimulus and response. In Purposive Behavior in Animals and Men (1932) he also classified the drives that set an animal's goals. The basic, first-order drives fall into two groups. Appetites are drives towards something the body needs, such as food, water and sex; they rise with deprivation and fall once the goal object is reached. Aversions are drives away from something, set off by a disturbing situation rather than by a bodily deficit, and Tolman named two. Fright is the drive to avoid injury: it moves the animal away from a source of pain or danger, usually by flight. Pugnacity is the drive to avoid interference: it is roused when something blocks or obstructs the animal's ongoing activity, and it shows as fighting to remove the obstacle. The pairing echoes William McDougall's (1908) instincts, in which flight goes with fear and pugnacity with anger. Example: when a stray dog snarls at Kabir he runs away (fright), but when his younger brother snatches the cricket bat from his hands mid-game, Kabir shoves him to get it back (pugnacity). Option b is right because Tolman defined pugnacity as the aversion that removes obstruction or interference to behaviour. Exam trap: 'punishment' sounds right because aversions involve unpleasant events, but avoiding painful harm is fright, the other aversion; pugnacity is not about escaping harm at all but about removing whatever gets in the way.", "traps": {"a": "Reinforcement is a consequence that strengthens a response (Edward Thorndike's law of effect, B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning); animals approach reinforcers, so 'avoidance of reinforcement' is not one of Tolman's aversions.", "c": "Avoiding pain or harm is the work of Tolman's other aversion, fright (avoidance of injury); pugnacity concerns removing an obstruction, so punishment avoidance does not define it.", "d": "The blank has a definite answer, avoidance of interference or obstruction, which is option b, so 'none of the above' fails."}}, {"q": "Which of the following variable(s) can be labelled as organismic variable(s)? 1. Task complexity 2. Intelligence 3. Errors made while learning task 4. Heart rate", "concept": "The answer is b: intelligence and heart rate are organismic variables, so 2 and 4 only. Robert Woodworth (1869 to 1962), whose Dynamic Psychology (1918) put the organism and its drives at the centre, expanded the behaviourist stimulus-response (S-R) formula into S-O-R, placing the organism between stimulus and response, and experimental psychology still sorts variables this way. Stimulus or task variables are features of the situation or task the experimenter presents: task complexity, list length, noise, instructions. Organismic (O, or subject) variables are characteristics or states that the participant brings to the study: age, sex, intelligence, personality, motivation, fatigue, and physiological states such as heart rate or blood sugar. Response variables are the behaviour measured as the outcome, the dependent variable: errors, reaction time, trials to criterion. Organismic variables usually cannot be manipulated, so they are measured, selected or controlled (by matching or random assignment); left uncontrolled, they act as extraneous variables. Example: Arjun learns a finger maze. The number of turns in the maze is a task variable, his IQ and resting heart rate are organismic variables, and the errors he makes are the response variable. Option b is right because only intelligence (a trait) and heart rate (a physiological state) belong to the organism. Exam trap: errors made while learning come from the person, so they feel organismic, but they are the response being measured (the DV); task complexity is a property of the task. Key note: heart rate is recorded as a response measure in some experiments, for example in psychophysiology, but as a state of the organism it is an O variable here; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "\"4 only\" leaves out intelligence, which is the textbook example of an organismic (subject) variable, a stable characteristic the participant brings to the study.", "c": "c adds task complexity, which is a stimulus or task variable set by the experimenter, not a characteristic of the organism.", "d": "d adds errors made while learning, which is the response variable (the dependent measure), not an organismic variable."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A large body of research indicates that violence and aggression is learned through observation. Reason (R): Learning through observation takes place due to exposure to similar situation and experienced reinforcement.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false. Assertion: a large body of research shows that aggression is learned through observation. True. In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hammer an inflatable doll later copied the model's exact acts and words far more than children who saw a calm model or none, without any reward. Their 1963 follow-up found filmed and cartoon models worked too, and decades of media-violence research point the same way. Reason: observational learning happens because of exposure to the same situation and experienced reinforcement. False. Observational learning needs neither. The observer learns by watching someone else, often in a situation they have never been in, and need not be reinforced. Bandura's four processes are attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation; the first three govern learning and the fourth governs performance. In Bandura's 1965 study, children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet when offered a reward for showing what the model had done, the differences between the groups all but disappeared: they had learned without reinforcement. Seeing a model rewarded or punished (vicarious reinforcement) changes whether an act is performed, not whether it is learned. Example: Kabir has never been in a fight, but after watching a film hero slap a rival he tries the same move on a classmate. Option c is right because A is well supported while R describes direct, operant learning rather than observational learning. Exam trap: R uses the language of reinforcement, which fits the earlier imitation theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941), who held that imitation must itself be reinforced; Bandura showed it need not be.", "traps": {"a": "a fails because R is false, and a false statement cannot explain a true one; observational learning works without direct reinforcement or prior exposure to the same situation, as the Bobo doll children showed.", "b": "b needs both statements to be true, but R wrongly makes experienced reinforcement a condition of observational learning, the very claim Bandura's 1965 study disproved.", "d": "\"Both false\" fails because A is true: the Bobo doll studies and the wider media-violence literature support aggression being learned by watching models."}}, {"q": "You have tried all morning to use the phone, but have not been able to because you can't get a dial tone. You finally stop trying. Later in the afternoon you decide to try to use the phone again. Your afternoon behaviour is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is d: trying the phone again in the afternoon, after giving up in the morning, is spontaneous recovery. The term comes from Ivan Pavlov's (1927) work on classical conditioning, and the same effect is seen in operant behaviour. When a learned response stops being reinforced, it weakens and finally stops: this is extinction. In Pavlov's dogs, a bell (the conditioned stimulus) that was no longer followed by food (the unconditioned stimulus) gradually stopped producing salivation. But if the dog was then left to rest and the bell was sounded again later, salivation reappeared, weaker than before, without any new pairing. That reappearance after a rest is spontaneous recovery. Pavlov concluded that extinction does not erase learning; it builds an inhibition that fades with time, letting the old response surface again. If extinction continues, each recovery is smaller and dies out faster. In the stem, picking up the phone is a response that a dial tone has reinforced thousands of times. All morning the dial tone is missing, so the response goes unreinforced and the person stops trying: that is extinction (option a). After an interval with no further training, the response returns in the afternoon: spontaneous recovery. The same idea explains why a phobia treated by exposure can partly return after a gap, and why therapists plan booster sessions. Option d is right because the afternoon attempt is a previously extinguished response returning after a rest period, without new reinforcement. Exam trap: option a describes the morning, not the afternoon; examiners put the extinction phase and the recovery phase in one story so that the student labels the whole story by its first half.", "traps": {"a": "Extinction is the weakening and stopping of a response when reinforcement is withheld; it describes the morning, when repeated tries without a dial tone made the person give up, not the afternoon return.", "b": "Shaping is B. F. Skinner's method of building a new response by reinforcing successive approximations to it; no new behaviour is being built step by step here.", "c": "A fixed action pattern is an innate, stereotyped sequence released by a sign stimulus (the ethology of Konrad Lorenz and Niko Tinbergen, such as the stickleback attacking anything red underneath); using a phone is learned, not instinctive."}}, {"q": "All of the following are used to study perceptual development EXCEPT", "concept": "The answer is b: the rod and frame apparatus is not a method for studying perceptual development. Researchers need ways to learn what a baby, who cannot speak, sees. Robert Fantz (1961) introduced preferential looking: two stimuli are shown side by side and an observer times how long the infant looks at each; a reliable preference shows the infant can tell them apart, and young infants looked longest at faces and patterns. Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960) built the visual cliff, a glass-topped table with a checkerboard just under the glass on the shallow side and far below on the deep side; most crawling infants aged 6 to 14 months refused to cross the deep side. Animal experiments allow control of early experience that would be unethical in children: Richard Held and Alan Hein's (1963) kitten carousel showed that kittens need self-produced movement to develop visually guided behaviour, Colin Blakemore and Grahame Cooper (1970) reared kittens seeing only vertical stripes, and Torsten Wiesel and David Hubel (1963) showed that closing one eye early permanently changes the visual cortex. The rod and frame test of Herman Witkin (1948 to 1954) is different: in a dark room a person sets a luminous rod to vertical inside a tilted luminous frame. Field-dependent people tilt the rod towards the frame; field-independent people trust their bodily sense of upright. It measures a cognitive style in older children and adults whose perception is already mature. Example: to learn whether 4-month-old Aarav can tell red from green, a researcher times his looks at the two colours side by side; the rod and frame test could not be given to him. Option b is right because the rod and frame test measures an individual difference in cognitive style, not how perception develops. Exam trap: the rod and frame test is a genuine perception task, so it looks as if it belongs; but it compares mature perceivers rather than tracing development.", "traps": {"a": "The visual cliff (Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk, 1960) is a classic test of depth perception in crawling infants and young animals, so it is a perceptual development method.", "c": "Preferential looking (Robert Fantz, 1961) times how long infants look at each of two side-by-side stimuli; a reliable preference shows discrimination, making it a core method of infant perception research.", "d": "Animal experiments, such as Richard Held and Alan Hein's (1963) kitten carousel, Colin Blakemore and Grahame Cooper's (1970) stripe-reared kittens and David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel's eye-closure studies, show how early experience shapes perception, so they are a major method."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered as a binocular cue for depth?", "concept": "The answer is c: stereopsis is a binocular cue for depth. Depth cues are the pieces of information the visual system uses to turn a flat retinal image into a three-dimensional world. Binocular cues need both eyes; monocular cues work with one eye alone. Because the eyes sit about 6 cm apart, each retina receives a slightly different view of any nearby object. The difference between the two images is binocular (retinal) disparity, and stereopsis is the vivid sense of depth the brain builds by fusing the two views and computing that disparity: the greater the disparity, the nearer the object. Charles Wheatstone (1838) demonstrated it with his stereoscope, and Bela Julesz (1960) showed with random-dot stereograms that disparity alone, with no other cue, can create depth. The second binocular cue is convergence, the inward turning of the eyes to fixate a near object, which the brain reads from the eye muscles. Monocular cues include the pictorial cues (linear perspective, interposition, relative size, texture gradient, shading, aerial perspective), motion parallax, and accommodation, the change in lens shape for focusing. Example: Meera closes one eye and tries to thread a needle; she keeps missing, because without binocular disparity her fine judgement of near depth fails. Option c is right because stereopsis depends on the two eyes' different views and cannot exist with one eye. Exam trap: accommodation and convergence are both oculomotor (muscle) cues and are often listed together, but only convergence needs two eyes; accommodation happens in each eye separately, so it is monocular.", "traps": {"a": "Motion (movement) parallax is a monocular cue: as you move, near objects sweep past faster than distant ones, and this works with one eye closed.", "b": "Accommodation is a monocular oculomotor cue: the ciliary muscles change the shape of the lens to focus on near objects, and each eye does this on its own.", "d": "Texture gradient is a monocular pictorial cue stressed by James J. Gibson (1950): surface texture looks finer and denser with distance, which can be seen in a photograph with one eye."}}, {"q": "If a researcher asks a subject to memorise a list of words and then asks her to fill out a questionnaire about how she memorised the list, the researcher is probably studying", "concept": "The answer is c: asking someone how she memorised a word list is a study of metamemory. Metamemory, a term introduced by John Flavell (1971), is knowledge about one's own memory and its control: knowing how good your memory is, which tasks are hard, which strategies work (rehearsal, grouping, imagery, mnemonics) and when you have studied enough. It is the memory part of metacognition, thinking about one's own thinking, which Flavell (1979) developed further. Metamemory has a monitoring side, as in judgements of learning and the feeling of knowing (the sense that you could recognise an answer you cannot recall), and a control side, as in deciding how long to study and which strategy to use. Researchers measure it with questionnaires and interviews about strategies, with predictions of how many items one will recall, and with confidence ratings. Developmental work shows that young children overestimate their memory span and seldom rehearse spontaneously, while older children know that a long list needs more study. In the stem the memory task itself is only a vehicle; the questionnaire about how she memorised the list asks her to report her own strategies and awareness, which is metamemory. Option c is right because the researcher is collecting the participant's knowledge of her own memorising, not measuring the memory processes themselves. Exam trap: semantic priming and spreading activation also involve word lists and are taught together in network models of memory, but they are measured through speeded responses such as reaction times, not through self-report about strategy.", "traps": {"a": "Semantic priming is faster processing of a word after a related word (nurse after doctor; David Meyer and Roger Schvaneveldt, 1971), measured by reaction times in a lexical decision task, not by a questionnaire about strategies.", "b": "Retroactive inhibition is forgetting in which new learning interferes with recall of older material; it is studied by having a group learn a second list between learning and recalling the first, not by asking how the list was memorised.", "d": "Spreading activation (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975) is the network idea that activating one concept spreads activation to linked concepts; it explains priming effects and is not studied through self-report questionnaires."}}, {"q": "A subject listens to a tape recording of an ambiguous sound and is asked to describe what he heard. This is most analogous to which of the following tests?", "concept": "The answer is d: describing what one hears in an ambiguous sound is most like the Rorschach inkblot test. Projective tests rest on the projective hypothesis: when a stimulus is ambiguous and has no right answer, the way a person structures it reveals their needs, conflicts and habits of perceiving. Lawrence Frank (1939) named the idea, and Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted projective techniques by the response they ask for: association, construction, completion, choice or ordering, and expression. In the Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), the person is shown ten symmetrical inkblots and asked 'What might this be?'; the examiner scores what is seen, where on the blot (location), which features determined it (form, colour, shading, movement) and how common the response is. This is an association task: the person names what an ambiguous percept looks like. The stem's task is the same, but in sound. B. F. Skinner (1936) built exactly such a tool, the verbal summator, which played faint, meaningless vowel sounds and asked listeners what was being said; it was nicknamed an auditory inkblot, and a similar device was called the tautophone. Example: Arjun hears a muffled recording and says it sounds like someone crying for help, and the clinician reads this much as she would a Rorschach percept. Option d is right because both tasks ask the person to say what an unstructured stimulus resembles, with no story to build and no fixed answer. Exam trap: the TAT is the most famous projective test and also uses ambiguous material, but it asks for a story with a past, present and future about pictures of people, a construction task; saying what a sound is matches the Rorschach's 'what might this be?'.", "traps": {"a": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935) asks the person to tell a story about ambiguous pictures of people, a construction task, not a description of what an ambiguous stimulus looks or sounds like.", "b": "The Blacky Pictures (Gerald Blum, 1950) are cartoons of a dog in scenes built around Freud's psychosexual stages, and the person tells stories about them; the stimuli are structured pictures, not formless sounds.", "c": "The MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is an objective, empirically keyed true or false inventory (567 items in the MMPI-2); it has no ambiguous stimulus at all."}}, {"q": "Exceptional performance by a student as a result of high teacher expectations:", "concept": "The answer is c: exceptional performance by a student that results from high teacher expectations is the Pygmalion effect. It is a self-fulfilling prophecy in the classroom: an expectation, true or false at first, changes the expecter's behaviour so that the expected outcome comes about. The name comes from the Greek myth of the sculptor Pygmalion, whose statue came to life, and from George Bernard Shaw's play Pygmalion (1913). Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson (1968) tested it at Oak School, a primary school in California. They gave every child a non-verbal intelligence test, disguised as the 'Harvard Test of Inflected Acquisition', then told teachers that about 20 percent of the pupils were 'bloomers' who would spurt intellectually in the coming year. The bloomers had in fact been chosen at random. Eight months later these children, especially in the first and second grades, had gained more IQ points than their classmates. The study was published as Pygmalion in the Classroom (1968). Later work showed that teachers give high-expectancy pupils a warmer climate, more input, more chances to respond and more feedback (Rosenthal's four-factor theory); in replications the effect is real but smaller than first claimed. The reverse, low expectations lowering performance, is the Golem effect. Example: told that Kabir is a late bloomer, his teacher calls on him more, praises his answers and sets him harder problems, and by the year's end his marks rise to match her belief. Option c is right because the stem defines the Pygmalion effect: the teacher's expectation itself producing the student's high performance. Exam trap: the Premack principle also concerns classrooms and rewards, so it tempts; but it is about using a preferred activity as a reinforcer, not about expectations.", "traps": {"a": "The Premack principle (David Premack, 1959) holds that a more probable activity can reinforce a less probable one (homework first, then cricket); it is about reinforcement, not teacher expectations.", "b": "Maintenance rehearsal (Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart, 1972) is rote repetition that keeps information in short-term memory without deepening its processing, as opposed to elaborative rehearsal; it has nothing to do with expectations.", "d": "Spreading activation (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975) is the memory network idea that activating one concept primes linked concepts; it does not explain performance driven by expectations."}}, {"q": "Author of \"On Becoming a Person\":", "concept": "The answer is c: On Becoming a Person (1961) was written by Carl Rogers. Rogers (1902 to 1987), an American psychologist, founded client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, one of the main strands of humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism. On Becoming a Person: A Therapist's View of Psychotherapy (1961) is a collection of his essays and talks written for a general readership. In it Rogers argues that people have an innate actualising tendency and grow towards becoming a fully functioning person (open to experience, living in the present, trusting their own feelings) when a relationship offers three core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard and accurate empathic understanding. The book also describes the 'good life' as a process rather than a fixed state. His earlier books were Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and Client-Centered Therapy (1951); a later one was A Way of Being (1980). Example: Riya, a counselling student, reads On Becoming a Person and learns that her task is not to give advice but to listen, accept and reflect her client's feelings. Option c is right because Rogers wrote the book, and its title captures his central idea that a person is always in the process of becoming. Exam trap: Rollo May is another humanistic and existential writer of the same period, so his name tempts, but May wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950) and Love and Will (1969); the commonest look-alike title is Abraham Maslow's Toward a Psychology of Being (1962).", "traps": {"a": "Rollo May, the American existential psychologist, wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950), Love and Will (1969) and The Courage to Create (1975); he did not write On Becoming a Person.", "b": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism with 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' (1913) and wrote Behaviorism (1925); he rejected the inner experience that Rogers's book is about.", "d": "Edward B. Titchener, the structuralist at Cornell, wrote the laboratory manuals Experimental Psychology (1901 to 1905) and A Text-book of Psychology (1909 to 1910), not a book on personal growth."}}, {"q": "If reliability is 0.00, which of the following is true?", "concept": "The answer is a: if reliability is 0.00, the standard error of measurement is relatively high, in fact as high as it can be. In classical test theory every observed score is a true score plus random error, and reliability (r) is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance. The standard error of measurement (SEM) is the standard deviation of the errors one person's scores would show over many retestings, and it is found from SEM = SD x the square root of (1 minus r). For an IQ test with SD 15 and reliability .91, SEM = 15 x the square root of .09 = 15 x .30 = 4.5 points. As reliability falls the SEM rises: at r = 1 the SEM is 0, and at r = 0 it equals the SD itself, 15 points, so the score says nothing about where the true score lies beyond what the whole group's spread already says. A zero-reliability test also has zero validity, because a validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the reliability (the index of reliability). The usual remedy for low reliability is to lengthen the test with parallel items, as the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) predicts. Example: Arjun scores 110 on a scale with reliability 0.00 and SD 15; his 95 percent band would stretch about 29 points either side, so the score is useless for any decision about him. Option a is right because zero reliability means all score variance is error, which pushes the SEM to its maximum, equal to the SD. Exam trap: students sometimes reverse the relation and think low reliability gives low error; reliability and SEM move in opposite directions, so the lowest reliability gives the highest SEM.", "traps": {"b": "A relatively low SEM goes with high reliability (at r = 1 the SEM is 0); with reliability 0.00 the SEM reaches its maximum, equal to the SD.", "c": "Low reliability suggests a test that is too short, not too long: by the Spearman-Brown formula, adding parallel items raises reliability, so excess length is not what a zero coefficient shows.", "d": "Validity cannot exceed the square root of reliability, so with reliability 0.00 validity must also be zero; an unreliable test cannot be valid."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Surgery is one of the methods to understand brain behaviour relations in humans. Reason (R): Lesion method destroys part of the brain.", "concept": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this item, in both the 2019 and the 2021 paper, marks (A) false and (R) true. Reason: the lesion method destroys part of the brain. True. A lesion is damage to or removal of tissue, and the lesion (ablation) method infers a region's function from what is lost, as in the ablation studies of Pierre Flourens (1820s) and the cortical lesions Karl Lashley made in rats (Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence, 1929). Assertion: surgery is one of the methods to understand brain-behaviour relations in humans. On the key's reading, false: as a research method, surgery means experimental lesioning, deliberately destroying or removing tissue to see what changes, and that is done only in animals. No one may operate on a human brain to find out what it does. In humans, brain-behaviour relations are studied through naturally occurring damage (strokes, tumours, head injuries), neuropsychological testing, electrical recording (EEG, event-related potentials), neuroimaging (CT, MRI, fMRI, PET) and non-invasive stimulation (transcranial magnetic stimulation). Patients who need surgery for epilepsy or a tumour can be tested before and after, and some of the best known findings came that way: Henry Molaison (H.M.), whose medial temporal lobes William Scoville removed in 1953 and whom Brenda Milner tested, and the split-brain patients of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s). But there the surgery is treatment, and the research method is the clinical study of a lesion. Example: a researcher who wants to know what the hippocampus does can lesion it in rats, but in people has to find patients whose hippocampus was damaged by illness or by a necessary operation. Option d is right on the key's reading because R correctly defines the lesion method, while A wrongly lists surgery as a method that can be used on humans. Exam trap: H.M. and the split-brain cases make A look obviously true, so most students pick a. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because therapeutic surgery such as H.M.'s has taught a great deal about the human brain and is a lesion in all but name. LBS's official key marks d for this question in both 2019 and 2021, on the reading that surgery is never a research method in humans. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "a treats surgery as a human research method that the lesion method explains. On the key's reading, deliberate brain surgery for research is never done in people; what we learn from cases like H.M. comes from operations done as treatment, so A is false.", "b": "b also needs A to be true. R is true, but on the key's reading A is false, because surgery as a research method is used only in animals.", "c": "c has it the wrong way round: R is true, since destroying or removing tissue and watching what is lost is the definition of the lesion method; it is A that the key treats as false."}}, {"q": "A researcher is interested in the effect that meeting the University Vice Chancellor has on students' perceptions of the University. To obtain subjects for her research, the researcher stands at the entrance of the performing arts building and selects every fifth student who enters the building. This method is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is d: selecting every fifth student at the entrance of the performing arts building is systematic selection at a convenient spot, and it is none of the three methods offered. A sample is drawn from a population, here the university's students, and the aim is usually that it represents that population. In probability sampling every member has a known, non-zero chance of selection. Simple random sampling gives every member an equal chance, usually by drawing from a complete list (the sampling frame) with random numbers. Stratified random sampling first divides the population into strata (faculty, year, sex) and then samples randomly within each. Systematic sampling picks every kth unit (k = population size divided by sample size) after a starting point, ideally random, within the list. The researcher in the stem has no list of students at all. She samples only those who walk into one building, so students who never use the performing arts building have no chance of selection, and arts students are likely to be over-represented. The every-fifth rule makes the procedure look systematic, but the frame is a doorway, so it is really a convenience sample with a fixed interval. That is why it is not random sampling (a), not stratified (b), and cannot be assumed representative (c). Option d is right because the method matches none of the labels offered: it is systematic selection from a non-representative location. Exam trap: 'every fifth' looks orderly and fair, so students call it random; regularity is not randomness, and fairness depends on who is in the frame. Key note: some books class systematic sampling under probability sampling when the start is random and the list covers the whole population; here there is no sampling frame and only the performing arts building, so it is neither simple random nor stratified, and representativeness cannot be assumed; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Random sampling needs every student in the population to have an equal chance of selection, usually from a complete list; students who never enter the performing arts building have no chance at all, and a fixed every-fifth rule is not a random draw.", "b": "Stratified random sampling divides the population into subgroups (faculty, year, sex) and draws randomly within each; the researcher forms no strata and makes no random draw.", "c": "A representative sample mirrors the population on relevant characteristics, but sampling at one building's entrance over-represents students who use that building, so representativeness cannot be assumed."}}, {"q": "Perception of information that is common or redundant across multiple senses is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: perceiving information that is common or redundant across several senses is amodal perception. Amodal means 'not tied to one modality'. Some properties of an event can be picked up by more than one sense at once: its rhythm, tempo, duration, intensity, timing and synchrony, and for objects their shape, size and texture. These are amodal properties. When a ball bounces, the eye sees it strike the floor at the same moments, and in the same rhythm, as the ear hears the thud. Lorraine Bahrick and Robert Lickliter (2000) proposed the intersensory redundancy hypothesis: in early infancy, information that is redundant across senses captures attention and is learned before information available to only one sense, which helps babies bind sights and sounds into one event. Elizabeth Spelke (1976) showed 4-month-olds two films side by side with a single soundtrack; they looked longer at the film whose timing matched the sound. The wider skill of combining the senses into one coherent event is called intermodal (intersensory) perception, and detecting amodal information is its core. The same word appears in amodal completion (Albert Michotte, 1964), seeing the hidden part of an occluded object, which is a different idea. Example: 3-month-old Aditi hears her mother clapping and looks at the video of her mother clapping in time rather than the one clapping out of step, because the shared rhythm links sight and sound. Option b is right because the stem defines amodal perception exactly: information that is the same whichever sense carries it. Exam trap: haptic perception also combines more than one kind of input (skin and movement), so it tempts, but it is one modality, active touch, not information shared across different senses.", "traps": {"a": "'Rhythmic perception' is not a technical term for this; rhythm is only one of the amodal properties (with tempo, intensity and synchrony) that can be shared across senses.", "c": "'Harmonic perception' would at most mean hearing musical harmony, a property of sound alone, and it is not a term for information shared across senses.", "d": "Haptic perception is active touch, exploring objects with moving hands (studied by James J. Gibson, 1962); it combines skin and movement signals within touch and is not perception of information redundant across senses."}}, {"q": "Project Orcon is:", "concept": "The answer is b: Project Orcon was B. F. Skinner's pigeon-training work. During the Second World War, Skinner (1904 to 1990), then at the University of Minnesota, proposed using trained pigeons to guide missiles, because electronic guidance was not yet reliable. The wartime effort, funded first by General Mills and then by the US government, was called Project Pigeon. Using operant conditioning, Skinner shaped pigeons to peck at an image of the target projected on a small screen in the missile's nose cone. Each peck was reinforced with grain, and on an intermittent schedule the birds pecked thousands of times without tiring. Pecks off centre tilted the screen and worked the steering, so the missile corrected its course. In demonstrations the pigeons tracked targets accurately, but officials could not take the idea seriously and the project was dropped in 1944. The US Naval Research Laboratory revived it after the war as Project Orcon, short for 'organic control', from 1948 to 1953; again the pigeons did well until electronic guidance made them unnecessary. Skinner told the whole story in his article 'Pigeons in a Pelican' (1960), the Pelican being the missile. The work shows the power of shaping and of intermittent reinforcement, which Skinner had analysed in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). Example: at a museum display, Kabir sees a nose cone with three small pigeon compartments and learns that birds once nearly steered missiles. Option b is right because Orcon (organic control) was the navy's continuation of Skinner's Project Pigeon. Exam trap: all three named psychologists ran famous animal studies, so any of them could seem to own 'the project'; the clue is 'Orcon', organic control of a guidance system, which fits only Skinner's trained pigeons.", "traps": {"a": "Edward Thorndike's trial and error work (1898) had cats escaping from puzzle boxes and led to the law of effect; it was never part of a military guidance project.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov's classical conditioning research used dogs salivating to signals of food in St Petersburg from the 1890s; it had nothing to do with guiding missiles.", "d": "Project Orcon is a documented continuation of Skinner's Project Pigeon, so 'none of the above' fails."}}, {"q": "Reminiscence is generally more after:", "concept": "The answer is a: reminiscence is generally greater after massed practice. Reminiscence is an improvement in recall or performance after a rest interval, with no further practice in between. It runs against the usual forgetting curve, in which retention falls with time. Philip Ballard (1913) named it after finding that schoolchildren who had partly learned a poem recalled more lines two or three days later than right after learning. It was later shown most clearly with motor skills, such as tracking a moving target on a pursuit rotor: after a few minutes' rest, performance jumps above where it stood at the end of practice. The explanation ties reminiscence to the type of practice. In massed practice, trials follow one another with little or no rest, so fatigue, boredom and what Clark Hull (1943) called reactive inhibition (a temporary tendency not to repeat an effortful response) build up and push performance below what has actually been learned. During the rest these temporary effects dissipate and the learned level shows through, so performance rises. In spaced (distributed) practice the rests are built in, inhibition never piles up and performance already reflects learning, so there is little left to recover. Spaced practice still gives better learning and retention overall, as Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) found; massed practice simply leaves more room for a rebound. Example: Meera practises typing for an hour without a break and her speed sags; after a ten-minute tea break she types faster than she did at the end of the hour, with no extra practice. Option a is right because the rebound after rest is largest when massed practice has piled up the fatigue and inhibition that rest removes. Exam trap: spaced practice is the better learning method, so students choose it here; but the question asks where reminiscence, the recovery after rest, is greater, not where learning is better. Key note: LBS's first key for this paper (2 Sep 2019) marked a, but its revised key (17 Sep 2019) changed it to b, spaced practice. The revised key is wrong: reminiscence is reliably greater after massed practice (Carl Hovland, 1938; reviewed by Woodworth and Schlosberg, 1954), because the inhibition that builds up during massed trials wears off in the rest, so a stays.", "traps": {"b": "Spaced (distributed) practice builds rests into practice, so fatigue and reactive inhibition never pile up and there is little to recover; it gives better learning overall but smaller reminiscence.", "c": "Part learning (learning a long task in sections and then joining them) is the counterpart of whole learning; it is a way of dividing material, not the practice condition that produces the largest reminiscence.", "d": "Serial learning is learning items in a fixed order (as in Hermann Ebbinghaus's nonsense syllable lists), which gives the serial position effect; it is a type of task, not a practice schedule that sets the size of reminiscence."}}, {"q": "The first attempt for standardization of mental tests in India was made by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first attempt to standardise a mental test in India was made by C. Herbert Rice (1922). Standardisation means giving a test to a large, representative sample under uniform conditions, so that norms can be built and any one score can be read against them. Intelligence testing began with Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905) in Paris, and Binet-type scales soon spread across the world. In India, C. Herbert Rice, working in Lahore, began adapting the Binet scale for Indian children in 1922, work that produced the Hindustani Binet Performance Point Scale. As its name says, the scale put Binet-type items into Hindustani, added performance tasks and scored the whole by points, and it was tried out on Indian school children to give local norms. That makes it the first Indian adaptation and standardisation of a Binet-type test. Later Indian work built on it. V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test), and S. M. Mohsin and Sohan Lal developed Hindi group tests of general mental ability. Example: if Meera's teacher wants to know whether Meera's score is high, she needs norms from Indian children like Meera, and Rice's scale was the first to offer them. Option c is right because Rice's work, begun in 1922, came before the work of Kamat, Mohsin and Sohan Lal. Exam trap: the Binet-Kamat test is the best known Indian Binet, so it tempts as 'the first'; it was a later revision, not the first attempt.", "traps": {"a": "S. M. Mohsin built a Hindi group test of general intelligence; it is an important Indian test but came well after Rice's 1922 scale.", "b": "V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test); it is the best known Indian Binet revision, but it followed Rice's work.", "d": "Sohan Lal developed a Hindi group test of general mental ability later; it is a standard Indian group test, not the first attempt at standardisation."}}, {"q": "According to psychodynamic theory, individuals are sometimes motivated to respond to the world through the perspectives they have learnt from other important people in their developmental past. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: this is object relations theory. In psychodynamic language an 'object' is not a thing but a person, or part of a person, towards whom feelings and wishes are directed, usually the mother or another early caregiver. Object relations theory holds that the infant takes in (internalises) mental pictures of the self and of important others from early relationships, along with the feelings that link them. These internal objects then act as templates: later in life the person sees and responds to partners, bosses and therapists through the perspectives learnt from those early figures. The school grew in Britain after Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960) described splitting of the world into good and bad objects and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; W. R. D. Fairbairn (1952) argued that the infant is object-seeking rather than pleasure-seeking; Donald Winnicott (1953) described the transitional object and the good-enough mother; Otto Kernberg (1975) linked split internal object relations to borderline personality organisation. Example: Kabir grew up with a harsh, critical father, and as an adult he hears criticism in every neutral remark from his manager, reacting to the internal father image rather than to the manager. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this internalised, relationship-based template learnt from important others. Exam trap: 'object permanence' shares the word 'object' but belongs to Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) sensorimotor stage, where the infant learns that things go on existing when out of sight.", "traps": {"a": "Object permanence is Jean Piaget's sensorimotor achievement (knowing that things exist when unseen); it is a cognitive milestone, not a psychodynamic theory of relating to others.", "b": "'Distorted perspective theory' is not a recognised psychodynamic theory; it is a made-up distractor built from the stem's word 'perspectives'.", "d": "Ego defence refers to defence mechanisms such as repression and projection, set out by Anna Freud (1936); they protect the ego from anxiety rather than carry learnt templates of important others."}}, {"q": "Gamma Amino Butyric Acid (GABD) is a major ------ transmitter.", "concept": "The answer is d: gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. GABA is an amino acid transmitter made from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and it is released by many interneurons throughout the cortex and by projection neurons of the basal ganglia and the cerebellum (the Purkinje cells). When it binds the GABA-A receptor, a channel opens and chloride ions flow into the neuron, hyperpolarising it and producing an inhibitory postsynaptic potential, so the cell becomes less likely to fire. The slower GABA-B receptor works through G proteins and potassium channels. Glycine does a similar inhibitory job in the spinal cord and brainstem, while glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter, so the brain's balance of excitation and inhibition rests largely on glutamate and GABA. Many calming drugs act at the GABA-A receptor: benzodiazepines such as diazepam increase how often the channel opens, barbiturates lengthen each opening, and alcohol also enhances GABA action, which is why combining them is dangerous. Too little GABA activity is linked to anxiety and seizures, and GABA neurons of the striatum are lost in Huntington's disease. Example: when Arjun is given diazepam before a dental procedure, it boosts GABA's inhibition and his anxiety settles. Option d is right because GABA's main effect is to inhibit, making neurons less likely to fire. Exam trap: glutamate and GABA are both amino acids and GABA is made from glutamate, so examiners swap them; glutamate is excitatory and GABA inhibitory.", "traps": {"a": "'Alerting' describes arousal systems such as noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, acetylcholine and orexin; GABA works the other way, damping neural activity.", "b": "The major excitatory transmitter is glutamate, from which GABA is made; GABA itself opens chloride channels and inhibits.", "c": "'Facilitatory' means making firing easier, which is what excitatory transmitters and presynaptic facilitation do; GABA makes the neuron less likely to fire."}}, {"q": "Who wrote the book \"Psychotherapy by reciprocal inhibition\" in 1958?", "concept": "The answer is c: Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958) was written by Joseph Wolpe. Wolpe (1915 to 1997) was a South African psychiatrist who later worked at Temple University in Philadelphia. Studying cats, he produced an 'experimental neurosis' by giving shocks in a cage, then removed the cats' fear by feeding them, first far from the cage and gradually nearer to it. From this he took the principle of reciprocal inhibition, a term borrowed from Charles Sherrington's (1906) work on opposing reflexes: if a response incompatible with anxiety is made in the presence of an anxiety-provoking stimulus, it weakens the bond between that stimulus and anxiety. In people, Wolpe used deep muscle relaxation, adapted from Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive relaxation, as the incompatible response. The resulting therapy is systematic desensitisation: the client learns relaxation, builds an anxiety hierarchy of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, and imagines each step while relaxed, moving up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Wolpe also used assertive and sexual responses as anxiety inhibitors, and he reported high success rates with neurotic patients. Example: Meera, afraid of injections, relaxes and imagines first a photograph of a syringe, then a nurse holding one, then the needle near her arm, until even the last step leaves her calm. Option c is right because Wolpe wrote this 1958 book, which founded systematic desensitisation on the principle of reciprocal inhibition. Exam trap: Andrew Salter also wrote an early conditioning-based therapy book and influenced Wolpe's use of assertion, so his name is the near miss; but his book was Conditioned Reflex Therapy (1949).", "traps": {"a": "Albert Bandura developed social learning theory, with observational learning and modelling (the Bobo doll study, 1961) and later self-efficacy (1977); he did not write this book.", "b": "Albert Ellis founded rational emotive therapy (1955; Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy, 1962), which disputes irrational beliefs through the ABC model rather than counterconditioning anxiety.", "d": "Andrew Salter wrote Conditioned Reflex Therapy (1949), which stressed excitation and assertive expression and influenced Wolpe, but Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition is Wolpe's own book."}}, {"q": "Author of \"Man's Search for Meaning\":", "concept": "The answer is a: Man's Search for Meaning was written by Viktor Frankl (spelled Victor in the option). Frankl (1905 to 1997) was an Austrian psychiatrist and neurologist in Vienna. From 1942 to 1945 he was held in Nazi concentration camps, including Auschwitz, and his wife, parents and brother died in the camps. In 1946 he published a short account of camp life in German; it appeared in English in 1959 and became known as Man's Search for Meaning. The first part describes how prisoners survived. Frankl observed that those who kept a reason to live, such as a loved one or unfinished work, endured better, and he quoted Friedrich Nietzsche (Twilight of the Idols, 1889): he who has a why to live can bear almost any how. The second part sets out logotherapy (from the Greek logos, meaning), called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy after the psychoanalysis of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) and the individual psychology of Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937). Logotherapy holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning, not Freud's will to pleasure or Adler's will to power; when it is frustrated, the result is an existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis. Meaning can be found by creating a work or doing a deed, by experiencing something or loving someone, and through the attitude one takes to unavoidable suffering. Its techniques include paradoxical intention and dereflection. Example: after losing his business, Arjun feels that nothing matters; a logotherapist helps him find meaning in supporting his daughter's education and in facing the loss with courage. Option a is right because Frankl wrote the book and founded logotherapy. Exam trap: Carl Jung wrote the look-alike title Modern Man in Search of a Soul (1933), so 'search' plus a famous name tempts; Man's Search for Meaning is Frankl's.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Jung founded analytical psychology and wrote Modern Man in Search of a Soul (1933), a look-alike title; he did not write Man's Search for Meaning.", "c": "Carl Rogers founded person-centred therapy and wrote On Becoming a Person (1961); he was a humanistic therapist, not the founder of logotherapy.", "d": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism (1913) and wrote Behaviorism (1925); a camp memoir about meaning is far from his focus on observable behaviour."}}, {"q": "Which of the following represents the Tetrad equation as given by Spearman?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three equations are forms of Spearman's tetrad equation, so \"All of these\" is correct. Charles Spearman (1904, 1927) argued that one general factor, g, plus a specific factor for each test explains why mental tests all correlate positively. If that is true, every correlation between two tests is simply the product of their g loadings: r12 = g1 x g2, r34 = g3 x g4, and so on. Multiply any two correlations that use each of the four tests once and you always get the same product, g1 x g2 x g3 x g4. Hence r12 x r34 = r13 x r24 = r14 x r23. The difference between any two of these products is a tetrad difference, and Spearman's tetrad difference criterion says that when a single general factor is enough, every tetrad difference is zero (within sampling error). If the tetrads do not vanish, more than one common factor is needed, which is what Louis Thurstone (1938) later argued with his group factors. Example: if Meera's four tests (vocabulary, arithmetic, matrices, memory) give r12 x r34, r13 x r24 and r14 x r23 all near 0.24, one g factor explains the table. Option d is right because option a states one equality, option b another, and option c is option b written the other way round, and all three follow from the same one-factor rule. Exam trap: students memorise one line, usually r13 x r24 = r14 x r23, and reject the others as wrong subscripts, but swapping the sides or choosing another pair of products does not change the criterion. Key note: many Indian guides quote only r13 x r24 = r14 x r23 (option b) as the tetrad equation, but option c is the same equation reversed and option a is another of the three equalities the rule implies, so d is the only consistent answer; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "r12 x r34 = r14 x r23 is one of the three equalities that a single general factor implies, so it is a correct form of the tetrad equation; choosing it alone ignores that b and c are equally correct.", "b": "r13 x r24 = r14 x r23 is the version most guides print, but it is not the only form: option a states another equality from the same rule and option c is this very line reversed.", "c": "r14 x r23 = r13 x r24 is option b with its two sides swapped, so it is just as correct; picking c alone would mean rejecting b, which is the identical equation."}}, {"q": "Electrically stimulating the lateral hypothalamus will cause a rat to:", "concept": "The answer is a: electrical stimulation of the lateral hypothalamus makes a rat start eating. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, regulates hunger, thirst, temperature and sex, and two of its areas became famous in the study of hunger. In 1940 Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson showed that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) made rats overeat and grow obese, so the VMH was called the satiety centre. In 1951 Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (Anand was an Indian physiologist, later of AIIMS, New Delhi) showed that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) caused aphagia (refusal to eat) and adipsia (refusal to drink); such rats could starve beside a full food dish. Stimulation gives the mirror image of a lesion: current delivered to the LH makes even a sated rat start eating, while VMH stimulation stops a hungry rat's meal. Eliot Stellar (1954) joined these findings in the dual-centre theory: the LH is the feeding (\"start\") centre and the VMH the satiety (\"stop\") centre. Later work showed the real picture is more complex: the arcuate nucleus, leptin and insulin also regulate intake, and LH lesions damage fibres passing through. Example: if Kabir's well-fed lab rat receives current through an electrode in its LH, it walks to the food tray and eats again. Option a is right because the LH is the classic feeding centre, and stimulating a centre switches its function on. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair, so \"stop eating\", the VMH stimulation result, tempts; and because LH lesions also abolish drinking, \"start drinking\" looks plausible, but the tested result of LH stimulation is eating.", "traps": {"b": "Stopping eating is the effect of stimulating the ventromedial hypothalamus, the satiety centre of Eliot Stellar's dual-centre theory; it is the reverse of the lateral hypothalamic effect.", "c": "Thirst is governed mainly by the lateral preoptic area, the subfornical organ and osmoreceptors around the third ventricle; some LH electrodes can evoke drinking, but the classic, tested result of LH stimulation is feeding.", "d": "Stopping drinking depends on satiety signals from the mouth, stomach and blood osmolality; LH stimulation switches ingestive behaviour on, not off."}}, {"q": "According to Gibson, the relationship between movement and flow is:", "concept": "The answer is a: movement creates optic flow, and that flow in turn provides information that guides further movement. James J. Gibson (The Perception of the Visual World, 1950; The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception, 1979) argued that perception is direct: the light reaching a moving observer already carries enough information to specify the world, with no need for mental inference. When you move forward, the whole optic array streams outwards from the point you are heading towards; that point, from which the texture expands, is the focus of expansion. Moving backwards makes the flow contract towards a point. The flow pattern tells you your heading, your speed and how soon you will reach an object (how fast its image expands). Gibson stressed that perception and action form one continuous loop: we move in order to perceive, and we perceive in order to move. He developed the idea while working on pilot training during the Second World War, where a safe landing depends on keeping the focus of expansion on the runway. Example: as Arjun rides his scooter towards a junction, the road texture flows outwards from the junction; when a lorry ahead suddenly fills more of his view, the rapid expansion warns him to brake, and braking changes the flow again. Option a is right because it states both halves of Gibson's loop: self-movement produces flow, and flow then steers the next movement. Exam trap: options b and d admit a link between movement and flow but cut the feedback to further movement, which removes the whole point of Gibson's ecological, perception and action view; that passive picture belongs closer to inference-based theories such as Richard Gregory's constructivism (Eye and Brain, 1966).", "traps": {"b": "This grants that flow and self-produced movement are linked but makes both independent of guiding further movement, which contradicts Gibson's claim that perception and action form one continuous loop.", "c": "Saying flow and movement are unrelated contradicts the definition of optic flow itself, which is the pattern of change in the optic array produced by the observer's own movement.", "d": "This keeps the first half of Gibson's idea (movement creates flow) but drops the second; for Gibson the value of flow is that it specifies heading and approaching collisions, so it does steer further movement."}}, {"q": "Damage to sensory area 1 of the cerebral cortex results in:", "concept": "The answer is b: damage to the primary somatosensory cortex (sensory area 1) causes loss of tactile and two-point discrimination. Primary somatosensory cortex, S1, lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, in Brodmann areas 3, 1 and 2. It receives touch, pressure, vibration and limb-position signals relayed through the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus. Wilder Penfield, with Edwin Boldrey (1937) and later Theodore Rasmussen (1950), stimulated this strip in awake surgical patients and mapped the sensory homunculus: the hands, lips and tongue have the largest areas because they make the finest discriminations. S1 is needed for the discriminative, analytic side of touch: two-point discrimination (telling two pinpricks from one), localising exactly where you were touched, judging texture, recognising an object by feel (stereognosis), reading numbers traced on the skin (graphaesthesia) and sensing limb position. Crude awareness that something has touched you, and much of pain and temperature sense, survive a cortical lesion, because these are registered at thalamic level. Example: after a small stroke in her left postcentral gyrus, Riya still feels a pinprick and a hot cup in her right hand, but she cannot tell a coin from a key in her pocket by touch, and two points 1 centimetre apart on her fingertip feel like one. Option b is right because it names the hallmark loss after S1 damage, fine discriminative touch including two-point discrimination. Exam trap: \"loss of perception of pain\" and \"loss of touch\" sound like what a sensory area ought to lose, but pain and crude touch largely survive cortical damage; and \"only tactile discrimination\" is half right, yet \"only\" leaves out two-point discrimination and the other discriminative losses.", "traps": {"a": "Pain travels in the anterolateral (spinothalamic) system and is registered largely at thalamic level and in cingulate and insular cortex, so S1 damage usually leaves pain felt, though less precisely localised.", "c": "Crude touch, the bare awareness that something has touched the skin, survives S1 damage because the thalamus registers it; what is lost is the fine, discriminative part of touch.", "d": "Tactile discrimination is indeed lost, but \"only\" makes the option too narrow: S1 damage also removes two-point discrimination, localisation, stereognosis and position sense, so b is the fuller answer."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT a level in Cattell's dynamic lattice?", "concept": "The answer is b: temperament is not a level in Raymond Cattell's dynamic lattice. Raymond Cattell (1950, 1957) sorted source traits into three kinds by modality: ability traits (how well we act), temperament traits (the style or tempo of action) and dynamic traits (why we act, our motivation). The dynamic lattice is his map of the dynamic traits alone, and it has three levels. At the base are ergs, innate drives shared with other species, such as hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion. Above them are sentiments, which Cattell also called sems: learned, stable clusters of attitudes centred on an important object such as career, home, religion or the self (the self-sentiment integrates all the others). At the top are attitudes, specific interests in a course of action, such as \"I want to revise tonight\". The levels are linked by subsidiation: each attitude serves one or more sentiments, and each sentiment ultimately serves ergs. Example: Kabir's attitude \"I want to buy the new statistics textbook\" is subsidiary to his career sentiment, which in turn serves the ergs of self-assertion and security. Option b is right because temperament belongs to Cattell's classification of source traits, beside ability and dynamic traits, and is never one of the rungs of the lattice. Exam trap: the options mix the lattice's own jargon (erg, sem) with a genuine Cattell term (temperament), so the familiar word looks safe; remember that the lattice maps motivation, while temperament describes style, a separate class of trait.", "traps": {"a": "The sem level is the middle tier of the lattice: sem is Cattell's short name for sentiments, learned attitude clusters centred on objects such as career, home or the self.", "c": "The erg level is the base of the lattice: ergs are Cattell's innate drives (hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness), which all sentiments and attitudes ultimately serve.", "d": "The attitude level is the top of the lattice: specific interests in a course of action, each subsidiary to one or more sentiments."}}, {"q": "Which of the following view of intelligence held that intelligence was one of the innate faculties along with learning, thinking and remembering etc?", "concept": "The answer is b: the oligarchic view held that intelligence is one of several innate faculties, along with learning, thinking and remembering. Charles Spearman (The Abilities of Man, 1927) opened his account of ability by sorting the older doctrines under three political metaphors. The monarchic doctrine treated intelligence as one supreme general power ruling all mental activity, the \"intelligence\" of everyday speech. The oligarchic doctrine came from the faculty psychology of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries: the mind was governed by a small group of separate, inborn faculties, such as memory, attention, imagination, judgement, reasoning and intelligence, each a power in its own right, ruling together like an oligarchy. The anarchic doctrine, linked with Edward Thorndike (1927), said ability is made of countless independent, specific capacities with no general ruler. Spearman rejected all three and offered his own two-factor theory of g and s. Example: if Meera's teacher says \"she has a good faculty of memory, a weak faculty of reasoning and an average faculty of intelligence\", the teacher is speaking in the oligarchic, faculty manner. Option b is right because the stem describes intelligence as one innate faculty sitting beside others (learning, thinking, remembering), which is exactly the faculty doctrine Spearman called oligarchic. Exam trap: \"monarchic\" tempts because it is the only other option that sounds like a theory of intelligence, but monarchic means a single ruling power, whereas the stem lists several faculties side by side. Key note: some Indian guides use \"oligarchic\" for Louis Thurstone's group factors (primary mental abilities, 1938) and file faculty psychology under the monarchic view; Spearman's own scheme places the faculties under oligarchic, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The monarchic view, in Spearman's scheme, treated intelligence as one supreme general power governing all mental activity, not as one faculty among several such as learning and remembering.", "c": "The Gestalt view (Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Köhler) explains intelligent behaviour as insight, the sudden restructuring of a problem as a whole, and does not divide the mind into innate faculties.", "d": "The behaviouristic view (John B. Watson, B. F. Skinner) treats intelligent behaviour as learned and shaped by the environment and rejects innate mental faculties altogether."}}, {"q": "Two factor theory was put forward by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the two-factor theory of intelligence was put forward by Charles Spearman. In 1904 Charles Spearman noticed that schoolchildren's marks in subjects as different as classics, mathematics and pitch discrimination all correlated positively with one another, a pattern now called the positive manifold. To explain it he developed factor analysis and proposed that every test score reflects two kinds of factor: g, a general factor common to all intellectual tasks (he likened it to mental energy), and s, a specific factor peculiar to each test. Tests that correlate highly with others are heavily saturated with g. He set the theory out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927), and his student John C. Raven built the Progressive Matrices (1938) as a near-pure measure of g. Example: if Arjun scores well in arithmetic, vocabulary and block design, Spearman would credit a high g, while his special flair for arithmetic reflects an s for number. Option d is right because g and s are the two factors of Spearman's theory. Exam trap: several other \"two-factor theories\" exist, so the label alone does not tell you the field: Frederick Herzberg's two-factor theory of job satisfaction (1959), O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory of avoidance learning (1947) and Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's two-factor theory of emotion (1962). For intelligence, the answer is always Spearman.", "traps": {"a": "Louis Thurstone (1938) proposed primary mental abilities, about seven group factors (verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, memory, perceptual speed, reasoning), and at first played down g.", "b": "Arthur Jensen (1969) proposed Level I (associative, rote) and Level II (conceptual, reasoning) abilities and was a strong defender of g, but the two-factor theory is not his.", "c": "J. P. Guilford's structure of intellect model (1967) crossed operations, contents and products into a cube of 120 abilities, far from a two-factor scheme."}}, {"q": "Patients with damage to the frontal lobes behave as if they lack control systems that monitor their processing resources appropriately. They are said to suffer from:", "concept": "The answer is b: frontal-lobe patients who lack a system to monitor and control their processing resources show the dysexecutive syndrome. In the working memory model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), the central executive is the attentional controller that plans, focuses, switches and inhibits while storing nothing itself. Alan Baddeley and Barbara Wilson (1988) coined the term dysexecutive syndrome for the pattern seen when this control system fails after frontal, especially dorsolateral prefrontal, damage. Donald Norman and Tim Shallice (1986) described a similar controller, the supervisory attentional system, which takes over when routine habits are not enough. Such patients may have normal IQ and memory scores yet cannot plan, sequence steps, shift set, stop an unwanted response or notice their own errors; they perseverate, repeating a strategy that has stopped working, as on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. The Behavioural Assessment of the Dysexecutive Syndrome (BADS; Barbara Wilson and colleagues, 1996) tests these problems with everyday-like tasks. Example: after a road accident injured his frontal lobes, Kabir can recite a recipe perfectly, but when he cooks he skips steps, keeps stirring after the dish has burnt and does not notice his mistakes. Option b is right because the stem describes a failure of executive control and self-monitoring, the defining feature of the dysexecutive syndrome. Exam trap: Gerstmann and Korsakoff syndromes are also named neurological syndromes and look like fair alternatives, but one is a parietal syndrome and the other an amnesic one; the words \"control systems\" and \"monitor\" point to the central executive.", "traps": {"a": "Gerstmann syndrome (Josef Gerstmann, 1924) follows damage to the left angular gyrus of the parietal lobe and has four signs, agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia and left-right disorientation, not a failure of executive control.", "c": "Korsakoff's syndrome is an amnesic syndrome caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, usually in chronic alcohol use, with severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation from damage to the mammillary bodies and thalamus.", "d": "None of the above fails because the stem describes, almost word for word, the dysexecutive syndrome named by Alan Baddeley and Barbara Wilson."}}, {"q": "The scaling method which is more likely to yield multidimensional attitude scale:", "concept": "The answer is d: the method of summated ratings, the Likert scale, is the scaling method most likely to yield a multidimensional attitude scale. Rensis Likert (1932) asked respondents to rate each attitude statement on five ordered points, from strongly agree to strongly disagree, and simply added the item scores. Items are kept by item analysis: an item survives if it correlates with the total score or separates high from low scorers. Nothing in this procedure tests whether all items measure one dimension, so items tapping related but different aspects of the attitude object can all correlate with the total and stay in. That is why researchers run factor analysis on Likert scales and often report subscales. The other three methods are built to place items on a single continuum. Louis Thurstone's paired comparisons (1927) and his equal appearing intervals (Louis Thurstone and Ernest Chave, 1929) have judges give each statement a scale value on one favourable to unfavourable continuum. Louis Guttman's scalogram analysis (1944) is cumulative: anyone who endorses a strong item should also endorse all milder ones, and the scale is accepted only if the coefficient of reproducibility reaches .90, a direct test of unidimensionality. Example: Riya's 20-item Likert questionnaire on attitudes to online classes later splits into three factors: convenience, learning quality and social contact. Option d is right because summing items chosen by item-total correlation does not force a single dimension, unlike the Thurstone and Guttman methods. Exam trap: students link \"multidimensional\" with the most technical-sounding method, scalogram analysis, but Guttman's method is the strictest test of unidimensionality of all.", "traps": {"a": "Louis Guttman's scalogram analysis builds a cumulative scale whose items form one ordered dimension, checked by a coefficient of reproducibility of .90 or more, so it is designed to be unidimensional.", "b": "Louis Thurstone's paired comparison method (1927) has judges compare every pair of statements to place them on one psychological continuum, so it yields a single dimension.", "c": "Thurstone and Chave's equal appearing intervals method (1929) has judges sort statements into 11 piles along one favourable to unfavourable continuum, giving each item a value on that single dimension."}}, {"q": "The observed tendency of humans to quickly return to a relatively stable level of happiness despite major positive or negative events or life changes is termed as:", "concept": "The answer is d: the tendency to return quickly to a stable level of happiness after major good or bad events is the hedonic treadmill effect. Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971) built on Harry Helson's adaptation-level theory (1964) and argued that people adapt to new circumstances: gains in income or comfort raise expectations and leave happiness where it was, like walking on a treadmill that never takes you anywhere. Philip Brickman, Dan Coates and Ronnie Janoff-Bulman (1978) gave the classic evidence: lottery winners were not much happier than controls and took less pleasure in ordinary events, while people paralysed in accidents were less unhappy than expected. Today the process is called hedonic adaptation. Later research shows the adaptation is incomplete for some events, such as long unemployment, the death of a spouse or severe disability, so a person's baseline can shift. Example: when Arjun gets a large salary rise he is delighted for a few months, but within a year his new flat and car feel normal and his everyday mood is back where it was. Option d is right because the stem describes exactly this return to a baseline after both positive and negative life changes. Exam trap: all the distractors are named \"effects\" from social psychology and behavioural economics, and \"positivity effect\" sounds as if it should be about happiness, but none of them describes adapting back to a baseline.", "traps": {"a": "The pratfall effect (Elliot Aronson, 1966) is that a highly competent person becomes more likeable after a small blunder, such as spilling coffee; it concerns interpersonal attraction, not levels of happiness.", "b": "The positivity effect (Mara Mather and Laura Carstensen, 2005) is older adults' tendency to attend to and remember positive more than negative information; it is not a return to a happiness baseline.", "c": "The endowment effect (Richard Thaler, 1980; Daniel Kahneman, Jack Knetsch and Richard Thaler, 1990) is valuing something more just because you own it, as in the coffee mug studies; it is about valuation, not adaptation."}}, {"q": "Autokinetic movement does not occur if there is a fixed ------.", "concept": "The answer is b: autokinetic movement does not occur when there is a fixed frame of reference. The autokinetic effect is an illusion of motion: a small, stationary point of light in a completely dark room appears to drift, wander or jerk about after a few seconds of staring. It happens because the darkness removes every visible landmark against which the light's position could be judged. The eyes make small involuntary drifts as they hold fixation, and without a frame of reference the brain cannot tell whether the image moved because the eyes moved or because the light moved, so it reads the change as movement of the light. The moment the room is lit, or a visible outline or second object appears, the background anchors the light and the illusion disappears. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) used this ambiguity to study how social norms form: people judging alone settled on personal ranges of movement, but in groups their estimates converged on a shared norm, which they kept when later tested alone. Example: Meera stares at a glowing dot on a dark wall in the laboratory and is sure it moved about 15 centimetres; when the technician switches on a dim lamp that shows the edges of the wall, the dot stays perfectly still. Option b is right because the illusion depends on the absence of a reference frame, so a fixed frame abolishes it. Exam trap: \"distance between the stimulus and the eye\" tempts because the light seems to move about in space, but fixing the viewing distance does not remove the illusion; only visible surroundings do.", "traps": {"a": "Vision is needed to see the light at all, so fixed vision cannot prevent the effect; the illusion actually arises while the eyes fixate the light, partly from their small uncontrolled drifts.", "c": "The light seems to move whatever its distance from the eye, because the cause is the missing visual framework, not the viewing distance.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because a fixed frame of reference, such as lighting the room or showing an outline around the light, reliably abolishes autokinetic movement."}}, {"q": "Which among the following disturbs initiation of sleep most?", "concept": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this paper marks orexin. Orexin, also called hypocretin, is a neuropeptide made by a small group of neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, discovered in 1998 by two teams, one including Luis de Lecea and one led by Masashi Yanagisawa. Orexin neurons fire during waking and fall silent in sleep. They excite the brain's arousal systems (noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus, serotonin, dopamine and acetylcholine) and hold the brain in the waking state. In Clifford Saper's flip-flop switch model (2001), orexin sits on the wake side of the switch between the sleep-promoting ventrolateral preoptic area and the arousal centres, and while orexin is active the switch does not flip into sleep. So orexin is the brain's own signal that opposes sleep onset. The evidence runs both ways: loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy, with sudden sleep attacks and cataplexy, and drugs that block orexin receptors (suvorexant, lemborexant, daridorexant) are approved for insomnia because they make it easier to fall asleep and stay asleep. The other options: melatonin, from the pineal gland at night, eases sleep onset; benzodiazepines enhance GABA and shorten sleep latency; amphetamines release dopamine and noradrenaline and also delay sleep onset, which makes them the strong runner-up. Example: Kabir lies in bed at midnight, still alert after a stressful day; orexin activity driving his arousal systems is part of what keeps him awake, and it is exactly what an orexin-blocking hypnotic would switch off. Option d is right on the key's reading because orexin is the brain's central wake-promoting signal, the one the newest insomnia drugs target. Exam trap: amphetamines look like the obvious answer, since a stimulant taken in the evening clearly delays sleep. Key note: this mock used to mark c, amphetamines, because a stimulant drug is the plainest cause of trouble falling asleep. LBS's official key for this paper marks d, orexin, the brain's own wake signal. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "Melatonin, released by the pineal gland at night, promotes sleep onset and shifts the circadian clock; it is taken to help people fall asleep, not to keep them awake.", "b": "Benzodiazepines such as diazepam and nitrazepam enhance GABA at GABA-A receptors and shorten sleep latency, which is why they are used as hypnotics for insomnia.", "c": "Amphetamines do delay sleep onset, since they release dopamine and noradrenaline, and they are the strong runner-up. The key prefers orexin, the brain's own wake-promoting signal, whose blockade is now a standard treatment for insomnia."}}, {"q": "Anu is below normal intelligence but she is able to tell you the exact day of the week of December 16, 1952. She has ------ syndrome.", "concept": "The answer is c: Anu has savant syndrome. Savant syndrome is a rare condition in which a person with a developmental disability, most often autism or intellectual disability, shows an island of outstanding skill that contrasts sharply with their overall limitations. J. Langdon Down (1887), who also described the syndrome now named after him, called such people \"idiot savants\"; the psychiatrist Darold Treffert (Extraordinary People, 1989) popularised the term savant syndrome. Savant skills cluster in a few areas: calendar calculating, music (often piano played by ear), art, lightning arithmetic and mechanical or spatial skill, usually backed by an exceptional memory. Calendar calculating, naming the weekday of any date, is the commonest. Oliver Sacks (1985) described the twins he called John and Michael, who could give the weekday of dates thousands of years away yet could not do simple sums. Researchers think savants draw on vast memorised regularities of the calendar rather than on a formula. Anu, whose general intelligence is below normal, names the weekday of 16 December 1952 at once; that pairing of low general ability with one striking skill is the savant pattern. Option c is right because savant syndrome is the only option that combines below-normal intelligence with an isolated exceptional ability. Exam trap: \"calendar assessment\" borrows the wording of Anu's skill to sound like a real diagnosis, but no such syndrome exists; the skill is called calendar calculating, and it is one form of savant syndrome.", "traps": {"a": "Genius refers to exceptionally high general ability and creative achievement (Lewis Terman, 1916, used it for IQs of about 140 and above), the opposite of Anu's below-normal intelligence.", "b": "Giftedness means high general intellectual ability (usually IQ about 130 or more) or high talent in a domain, so it does not fit someone whose overall intelligence is below normal.", "d": "There is no calendar assessment syndrome; Anu's skill is called calendar calculating, and it is the commonest skill seen within savant syndrome."}}, {"q": "How many 'three-way interactions' can be studied in a 4×3×3 factorial design?", "concept": "The answer is a: a 4 x 3 x 3 factorial design has only one three-way interaction. In a factorial design each number stands for one independent variable (factor), and its value is the number of levels of that factor. So 4 x 3 x 3 means three factors, say A with 4 levels, B with 3 and C with 3, crossed to give 4 x 3 x 3 = 36 cells. The number of effects tested depends on how many factors there are, not on how many levels each has. With three factors, a factorial analysis of variance tests three main effects (A, B, C), three two-way interactions (A x B, A x C, B x C) and one three-way interaction (A x B x C). A three-way interaction means that the two-way interaction between two factors itself changes across the levels of the third. In general, with k factors the number of interactions involving r factors is the number of ways of choosing r factors from k, and there is only one way to choose all three of three. Example: Riya studies recall with four study methods, three noise levels and three times of day; if the method by noise pattern looks different in the morning from the evening, that is the single A x B x C interaction. Option a is right because three factors can be combined all together in only one way, whatever their numbers of levels. Exam trap: examiners count levels instead of factors; 4 echoes the first factor's levels, 12 is 4 x 3, and 3 is the number of two-way interactions, none of which is the number of three-way interactions.", "traps": {"b": "4 is the number of levels of the first factor; levels do not create extra three-way interactions, which depend only on the number of factors.", "c": "12 is 4 x 3, the number of cells in a two-factor 4 x 3 design, and has nothing to do with the count of three-way interactions.", "d": "3 is the number of two-way interactions (A x B, A x C, B x C), and also of main effects, in a three-factor design, but there is only one three-way interaction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following methods are employed to study Memory Processes? 1. Free recall and Recognition 2. Observation and Recognition 3. Conditioning and Neuroimaging 4. Concurrent memory task paradigm and Neuroimaging", "concept": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition (statement 1), and the concurrent memory task paradigm with neuroimaging (statement 4), are methods used to study memory processes. Experimental memory research began with Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), who learned nonsense syllables and measured retention by relearning (the savings method). Today retention is measured mainly in three ways: in free recall the person reproduces studied items in any order, in cued recall a hint is given, and in recognition the person picks studied items out from new ones. Recognition is usually easier than recall, so comparing the two shows how much is stored and how much can be retrieved. To study the structure of working memory, Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) used the concurrent task (dual-task) paradigm: if holding digits in mind barely disturbs reasoning, the two tasks must draw on partly separate systems. Combined with neuroimaging, such as functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and positron emission tomography (PET), such tasks show which regions, such as the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, work during encoding and retrieval. Example: Meera reads 20 words in the scanner, recalls as many as she can, and later picks them out from 40 words, while her brain activity is recorded. Option d is right because statements 1 and 4 each pair two genuine memory methods. Exam trap: recognition also appears in statement 2, which makes 2 look safe, but observation is a general data-collection method, not a memory paradigm. Key note: conditioning (for example eyeblink conditioning in amnesic patients) is used to study implicit memory, so statement 3 is arguable, but statements 1 and 4 are the two clearly correct methods and d is the only option containing both; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Statement 1 is correct, but statement 3 pairs conditioning, a learning paradigm, with neuroimaging; conditioning is not a standard method for studying memory processes as such, so a is weaker than d.", "b": "Statement 2 fails because observation is a general research method for recording behaviour, not a memory paradigm, and statement 3 rests on conditioning, a learning procedure.", "c": "Statement 4 is correct, but it is paired with statement 3's conditioning, a learning procedure, and the option leaves out free recall and recognition, the core memory tests."}}, {"q": "Which structure in the core of the brain is involved in the expression of stronger emotion such as fear and rage?", "concept": "The answer is c: LBS's official key for this paper marks the limbic system. The limbic system is a set of linked structures deep in the centre of the brain, around the brainstem and under the cortex: the amygdala, hippocampus, cingulate gyrus, septal area and, in most accounts, the hypothalamus. James Papez (1937) proposed a circuit of these structures as the basis of emotion, and Paul MacLean (1952) named it the limbic system. Its role in fear and rage comes from lesion and stimulation work. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937) removed the temporal lobes, amygdala included, in rhesus monkeys, and the monkeys lost their fear: they became tame and placid and approached snakes and people they had avoided (Kluver-Bucy syndrome). Stimulating the amygdala, or the hypothalamus it controls, produces fear, defence and attack. Philip Bard (1928) found that cats without a cortex still showed sham rage as long as the hypothalamus was intact, and Walter Hess (Nobel Prize, 1949) evoked rage by stimulating the hypothalamus. Joseph LeDoux later traced a fast fear pathway from the thalamus straight to the amygdala, the low road that sets off a fear response before the cortex has identified the danger. Example: when a dog suddenly lunges at Arjun, his amygdala flags the threat and, through the hypothalamus, triggers the sympathetic fight or flight pattern before he has even thought about it. Option c is right on the key's reading because the limbic system, with the amygdala and hypothalamus inside it, is the brain's emotional core for fear and rage. Exam trap: the hypothalamus is itself part of the limbic system and organises the bodily expression of rage, so b is the near miss. Key note: this mock used to mark b, the hypothalamus, because Atkinson and Hilgard's three-layer model puts it in the central core while the limbic system forms a layer around the core. LBS's official key for this paper marks c, the limbic system. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "The thalamus is the brain's sensory relay; in the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927) it sends signals to the cortex for the conscious experience of emotion, but fear and rage are organised by the limbic system and hypothalamus.", "b": "The hypothalamus does drive the bodily expression of rage (Bard's sham rage, Hess's stimulation studies), which makes it the near miss. But it is one part of the limbic system, and the key takes the whole system, amygdala included, as the answer.", "d": "The cortex is the outer layer that appraises situations and restrains emotional expression; cats without a cortex raged more, not less, showing that the cortex inhibits rage rather than producing it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the best example of hindsight bias?", "concept": "The answer is b: misremembering one's previous views to fit what one now knows is the best example of hindsight bias. Hindsight bias, the \"I knew it all along\" effect, is the tendency, once an outcome is known, to believe we would have predicted it and to recall our earlier judgements as closer to the outcome than they really were. Baruch Fischhoff (1975) showed it experimentally: people told the outcome of a historical conflict rated that outcome as far more likely than people not told, and they denied that the information had influenced them. Baruch Fischhoff and Ruth Beyth (1975) asked students, before President Richard Nixon's 1972 visits to China and the Soviet Union, how likely various outcomes were; afterwards the students recalled their own estimates as closer to what had actually happened. The bias works through memory reconstruction: the new knowledge blends into the old memory, so the original view can no longer be retrieved accurately. It makes past events seem more predictable than they were and makes research findings look obvious. Example: before the match Kabir said India had only a slim chance; after India won, he tells his friends he always knew they would win, and he honestly remembers having said so. Option b is right because it describes the core mechanism, rewriting a past opinion so that it fits present knowledge. Exam trap: option d also describes a belief-driven bias, but attending only to belief-consistent information is confirmation bias, which filters evidence as it arrives, whereas hindsight bias distorts the memory of what one thought before. Key note: LBS's official key for this paper marks a, changing one's attitude to match how others see a person. That key is wrong: a describes conformity, while hindsight bias is rewriting one's memory of an earlier judgement to fit what one now knows (Baruch Fischhoff, 1975), so b stays.", "traps": {"a": "Changing one's attitude to a person to match how others see them is social influence or conformity (as in Solomon Asch's 1951 studies), not a distortion of one's own remembered views.", "c": "Switching one's choice of prize in the hope that new information helps resembles the Monty Hall problem, a matter of probability reasoning and decision making, not of misremembering earlier views.", "d": "Attending only to information consistent with one's beliefs is confirmation bias (Peter Wason, 1960); it filters incoming evidence rather than rewriting memories of what one thought before."}}, {"q": "Those with more complex psychopathologies are likely to be excluded from treatment outcome studies and so denied access to the treatment programme associated with the study. This is referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is c: excluding people with complex psychopathologies from treatment outcome studies is referred to as narrow inclusion criteria. A treatment outcome study, usually a randomised controlled trial, tests whether a therapy works better than a comparison condition. To get a clean answer, researchers set strict inclusion criteria (who may enter: for example, adults aged 18 to 65 with a primary diagnosis of major depressive disorder) and exclusion criteria (who may not: people with comorbid substance use, psychosis, personality disorder, active suicide risk or serious medical illness). Narrow criteria make the sample homogeneous, reduce error variance and raise internal validity, so any difference can be credited to the treatment. The cost is twofold. First, people with complex, comorbid problems are denied the treatment the study offers. Second, the results generalise poorly to routine clinics, where comorbidity is the rule; this is the gap between efficacy (does the treatment work under ideal, controlled conditions?) and effectiveness (does it work in everyday practice?). Example: Meera, who has depression together with panic disorder and alcohol misuse, is screened out of a trial of cognitive behaviour therapy for depression; the trial shows the therapy works, but its patients were far simpler cases than Meera. Option c is right because the stem describes the consequence of narrowly drawn criteria for entering a study. Exam trap: \"complex exclusion criteria\" echoes the stem's word \"complex\" and sounds technical, but the recognised phrase is narrow (strict) inclusion criteria; exclusion criteria are simply the listed reasons for leaving someone out, and \"complex\" is not a standard label for them.", "traps": {"a": "Simple diagnosis criteria is not a standard research term; diagnostic criteria (DSM or ICD) define a disorder, whereas the stem is about who is allowed into a study.", "b": "A no-treatment control condition is a comparison group that receives no therapy (often a waiting list); it concerns how admitted participants are assigned, not who is kept out of the study.", "d": "Complex exclusion criteria borrows the stem's word complex but is not the recognised term; the standard phrase for screening out complicated cases is narrow (strict) inclusion criteria."}}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Free recall and recognition, b. Sentence verification task, c. Priming, d. Scanning task. 1. Semantic memory, 2. Sensory memory, 3. Episodic memory, 4. Implicit memory", "concept": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition test episodic memory (3), sentence verification tests semantic memory (1), priming tests implicit memory (4) and the scanning task, by elimination, sensory memory (2). Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory into episodic memory, personal events tied to a time and place, and semantic memory, general knowledge of facts and concepts. Free recall (write down every word from the list you just heard) and recognition (which of these words were on the list?) ask about one specific study episode, so they are episodic tasks. In a sentence verification task the person judges sentences such as 'A canary is a bird' as true or false as fast as possible; Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) used the reaction times to map the network of stored facts, so the task taps semantic memory. Priming is an influence of earlier exposure without conscious recollection: having seen BUTTER, a person completes BUT___ as BUTTER more often, even without remembering the word; Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) called this implicit memory. Scanning a briefly flashed display, as in George Sperling's (1960) partial report, taps the brief iconic store of sensory memory. Example: Riya recognises yesterday's list words (episodic), confirms that a sparrow is a bird (semantic) and fills BUT___ with BUTTER without knowing why (implicit). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: recall and recognition feel like knowledge tests, so students send them to semantic memory; they test memory for an episode. Key note: Saul Sternberg's (1966) memory scanning task is really a short-term memory task, while scanning a briefly flashed display, as in Sperling's partial report, taps sensory (iconic) memory; with the other three pairs fixed, d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Every pairing is wrong: a-4 sends recall and recognition to implicit memory though both need conscious remembering, b-2 makes sentence verification sensory, c-1 makes priming semantic and d-3 makes scanning episodic.", "b": "b-1 (sentence verification as semantic) is right, but a-2 calls recall and recognition sensory, c-3 makes priming episodic though it works without recollection, and d-4 makes scanning implicit.", "c": "Every pairing is wrong: recall and recognition are episodic (not semantic), sentence verification is semantic (not implicit), priming is implicit (not sensory) and scanning is sensory (not episodic)."}}, {"q": "Saying that language is lateralized in the brain means that:", "concept": "The answer is c: saying language is lateralised means that language function is primarily associated with one hemisphere of the brain. Lateralisation, or hemispheric specialisation, means that the two cerebral hemispheres look alike but do not do the same jobs. For language the left hemisphere is dominant in about 95 percent of right-handers and in most left-handers (roughly 70 percent). The first evidence came from patients: Paul Broca (1861) described \"Tan\", who lost fluent speech after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe, and Carl Wernicke (1874) described loss of comprehension after damage to the left posterior temporal lobe. The Wada test of Juhn Wada (1949) injects sodium amytal into one carotid artery to anaesthetise one hemisphere; if that side holds language, the patient falls silent. Split-brain studies by Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (from 1962) showed that patients could name an object seen in the right visual field, which goes to the left hemisphere, but not one in the left field. Lateralisation is relative, not total: the right hemisphere handles prosody (the melody and emotional tone of speech), jokes, metaphor and some word comprehension. Example: after a left-hemisphere stroke Riya struggles to find words, while her neighbour, after a similar right-hemisphere stroke, speaks fluently but in a flat, toneless voice. Option c is right because lateralised means located mainly on one side, and \"primarily\" leaves room for the right hemisphere's smaller role. Exam trap: option d also puts language on one side, but on the wrong side and in absolute terms (\"only the right temporal lobe\"); language lateralisation almost always means the left hemisphere, and never \"only\".", "traps": {"a": "The cerebellum coordinates movement and contributes a little to speech timing and verbal fluency, but language comprehension depends on cerebral cortex, chiefly Wernicke's area in the left temporal lobe; the cerebellum says nothing about one side being dominant.", "b": "Equal contribution by both hemispheres is the opposite of lateralisation; Broca's and Wernicke's aphasias follow damage to the left side, not to either side equally.", "d": "In most people language is lateralised to the left hemisphere, not the right, and only is too absolute: language uses frontal, temporal and parietal regions, and the right hemisphere adds prosody."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are not Factor Analytically derived personality inventories? 1. 16 PF 2. Comrey Personality Scales 3. Jenkins Activity Survey 4. Edwards Personal Preference Schedule", "concept": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey (3) and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (4) are not factor-analytically derived. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content strategy writes items whose content obviously fits the trait; the theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory; the empirical criterion-group strategy keeps whatever items separate a criterion group from controls; and the factor-analytic strategy correlates many items, keeps those that load on each factor and names the factors afterwards. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949) came from factor analyses of trait terms, and Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970; 180 items, eight factors such as trust and orderliness) were built the same way, so 1 and 2 are factor-analytic. The Jenkins Activity Survey of C. David Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Ray Rosenman measures the Type A behaviour pattern; its Type A scale items were chosen because they agreed with Structured Interview classifications, a criterion approach. Allen Edwards's Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (revised edition, 1959) is theory-based: it measures 15 needs from Henry Murray's (1938) list, using forced-choice pairs matched for social desirability. Example: Arjun gives 300 new items to 1,000 students and keeps only the clusters that emerge from factor analysis; that is Cattell's and Comrey's route, not Edwards's. Option b is right because 3 and 4 are the only two not built by factor analysis. Exam trap: the EPPS controls social desirability so carefully that it looks statistical, but its items were written for Murray's need theory. Key note: the Jenkins Activity Survey also reports subscales (speed and impatience, job involvement, hard-driving competitiveness) found by factor analysis, but its main Type A scale was built to match interview classifications, so it is not classed as a factor-analytic inventory; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 and 2 are exactly the two inventories that are factor-analytic (Cattell's 16PF and Comrey's scales), so this option names the opposite of what the stem asks.", "c": "1 and 2 are factor-analytic, so they cannot be on a 'not factor-analytic' list; only 3 in this set is correct, and 4 is left out.", "d": "2, the Comrey Personality Scales (Andrew Comrey, 1970), was derived by factor analysis, so it does not belong; 3 and 4 are correct."}}, {"q": "The antidiuretic hormone which mainly targets kidneys and arterioles, stimulates water retention; raises blood pressure by contracting arterioles:", "concept": "The answer is c: the antidiuretic hormone that makes the kidneys retain water and raises blood pressure by contracting arterioles is vasopressin. Vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone (ADH), is a small peptide made by neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. Their axons run down the pituitary stalk to the posterior pituitary, which releases the hormone into the blood. Its two names record its two actions. It is antidiuretic because it makes the collecting ducts of the kidneys reabsorb water, so less but more concentrated urine is passed. It is a vasopressor because, at higher levels, it contracts the smooth muscle of arterioles and raises blood pressure. Release rises when osmoreceptors detect salty, concentrated blood or when blood volume falls, as in dehydration or bleeding. Lack of vasopressin causes diabetes insipidus, with large volumes of dilute urine and intense thirst; alcohol suppresses its release, which is why drinking makes people pass more urine. In the brain, vasopressin also plays a part in social bonding and memory. Example: after a long cricket match in the sun without water, Arjun's vasopressin rises, his kidneys save water, and his urine becomes dark and scanty. Option c is right because vasopressin is the posterior pituitary hormone that acts on the kidneys and arterioles exactly as the stem describes. Exam trap: oxytocin is vasopressin's twin, made in the same hypothalamic nuclei, released from the same posterior pituitary and differing by only two amino acids, so it is the classic decoy.", "traps": {"a": "Oxytocin is the other posterior pituitary hormone, made in the same hypothalamic nuclei; it causes uterine contraction in labour and milk let-down in breastfeeding, not water retention.", "b": "Thyroxine (T4) from the thyroid gland raises the body's metabolic rate and is needed for brain development; it does not act on the kidneys to retain water.", "d": "Progesterone, from the corpus luteum and the placenta, prepares and maintains the uterine lining in pregnancy; it is a steroid sex hormone, not an antidiuretic."}}, {"q": "Read the following statements carefully and give an appropriate response: I. Only about 6 percent of the population score above 140 on IQ tests. II. An IQ score below 90 indicates mental retardation.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are false. Statement I is false. Deviation IQs on the Wechsler scales have a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, and they are roughly normally distributed. A score of 140 is 40 points above the mean, which is 40 divided by 15, about 2.67 SD. Under the normal curve only about 0.4 percent of people lie above z = 2.67, roughly 1 person in 260, not 6 percent. Even above 130 (2 SD) there are only about 2 percent; 6 percent of people score above about 123. Statement II is also false. An IQ below 90 does not indicate intellectual disability (the older term was mental retardation). On the WAIS-IV, 90 to 109 is average, 80 to 89 is low average and 70 to 79 is borderline. Intellectual disability needs an IQ of about 70 or below (two SD under the mean, allowing about 5 points for measurement error) together with deficits in adaptive behaviour that begin in the developmental period (DSM-5, 2013). Example: Kabir scores 86 on the WAIS-IV; he is in the low average band, not intellectually disabled. Option b is right because statement I overstates the proportion above 140 about fifteenfold and statement II puts the cut-off for intellectual disability 20 points too high. Exam trap: each statement sounds near enough to a remembered fact (a few percent are gifted, and the old dull normal band began near 90), so check each against the normal curve and the cut-off of 70. Key note: the original source may have said \".6 percent\" (the old Stanford-Binet, with SD 16, puts about 0.6 percent above 140), so a dropped decimal could make the setter read statement I as true and want a; as printed, 6 percent is false, matching the same statement on IMHANS 2021 Q37, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Statement II is indeed false, but statement I is not true: an IQ of 140 is about 2.67 SD above the mean, so only about 0.4 percent score above it, not 6 percent.", "c": "Statement I is indeed false, but statement II is not true: an IQ in the 80s is low average, and intellectual disability needs an IQ of about 70 or below plus adaptive deficits.", "d": "Neither statement is true: 6 percent above 140 overstates the real figure (about 0.4 percent) roughly fifteenfold, and an IQ below 90 falls in the low average band, not intellectual disability."}}]});
